\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Dollar closes at S/3.950 and continues its upward trend\b0\par\par\uc2 483 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 September 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The dollar completed seven consecutive upward movements yesterday -except for Thursday, September 22 (S/3.884)- and closed at S/3.950, after appreciating 0.23% against Monday's closing price of S/3.9410, its highest level since the first half of July, according to the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BCRP).\par \par \uc2 During the exchange session, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  with a direct sale of US$1 million at an average price of S/3.9650 and placed a 9-month S/870 million swap for an average rate of 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 Factors that strengthen the dollarIn the opinion of Javier Pineda, CEO of the Billex platform, the exchange rate has been appreciating influenced by external factors, among them, monetary policies to combat high inflation as in the United States, whose accumulated rate for the year reaches 6.2%.\par \par \uc2 The specialist recalls that the immediate effect of raising interest rates is that of a "cooling", that is, a country's economy and GDP tend to decrease. "There is a feeling that there may be a global cooling of the economy, and central banks in the world have increased their interest rates, starting with the Fed, which last week raised its rate by 0.75% and this caused the exchange rate to rise", said Pineda.\par \par \uc2 Bullish projectionsOn the other hand, Juan Carlos Odar, director of Phase Consultores, highlights that the sol is one of the best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Latin America and in the world.\par \par \uc2 "If you look at the Chilean peso and the Colombian peso, they have lost value, the sol and the real are among the few that have been appreciating in the region, although the Brazilian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does have greater volatility, which is a remarkable feature of our \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ", explains Odar.\par \par \uc2 It should be noted that both specialists estimate that the exchange rate in Peru will close the year above S/3.90, taking into account that the Fed is likely to raise its interest rate again in November.\par \par \uc2 Stock Exchange closed with gainsMeanwhile, the Lima Stock Exchange (BVL) closed in positive territory and registered 14 indicators up, 2 down and none without variation. Thus, the S&P/BVL Peru General index gained 0.56%. However, so far in 2022, the BVL lost 12.61% and falls 0.84% in the month.\par \par \uc2 In yesterday's session, the financial sector led the gains with an advance of 1.34%, followed by industrials (1.01%), mining (0.99%), construction (0.81%) and consumer goods (0.19%).\par \par \uc2 DataPerformance. Internationally, the dollar rose against the euro, but lost ground against the British pound and Japan's yen, according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Europe and the East. The euro declined 0.20% against the dollar to US$0.96 and the dollar rose 0.1% against the yen to US$144.86.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220928ei9s005l8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
