\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Dirty flotation: what it is and how it helps the Peruvian  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  to 'beat' the dollar\b0\par\par\uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Juni 2022\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 Peru is currently facing what is probably its worst inflation in the last 30 years. We are talking about consumer prices rising 8.1 percent between May 2021 and May 2022, much higher than the targeted 2 percent. Despite this, the country preserves an important economic stability, which has to do with its dirty float monetary policies.\par \par \uc2 What is the dirty float all about? Peru has an atypical policy vis-\u224\'00\'E0-vis the reference \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is the dollar. According to the BBC, this has allowed the Peruvian market to be one of the least volatile in Latin America, considering that there are other countries in the continent that suffer turbulences due to the increase of the dollar against their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The exchange rate with reference to the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is managed in different ways in several countries, according to their monetary policies with which they face one of the most influential \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the world.\par \par \uc2 First, there is the fixed exchange rate, in which central banks \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the dollar stable through buying and selling. This means that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  faces several challenges with respect to the markets and the growth of the dollar itself.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, there is the floating model, which allows the exchange rate to move freely, since the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not interfere directly with the exchange market. It is the one that is popularly implemented in European and American countries, and can give rise to large movements in the exchange rate due to external or conjunctural situations.\par \par \uc2 The Peruvian model is different, in this one there is also floating, but it is limited by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself, which 'stops' the Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the dollar from going to the sky.\par \par \uc2 How does the dirty float work in Peru?According to Waldo Mendoza, from the Economics Department of the Pontificia Universidad Cat\u243\'00\'F3lica de Per\u250\'00\'FA (PUCP), "the \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of Peru\uc2  (BCRP) rows against the current in the exchange market. It tends to buy dollars when the exchange rate goes down, and tends to sell when the exchange rate goes up".\par \par \uc2 This exchange rate is different from the fixed model in the aspect that there is a floating exchange rate. On the other hand, it is different from the floating model in the sense that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself is in charge of 'putting its hand' in the buying and selling of dollars to keep Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stable.\par \par \uc2 In the book Reg\u237\'00\'EDmenes Cambiarios y Desempe\u241\'00\'F1o Macroecon\u243\'00\'F3mico written by Marco Vega and Erick Lahura, both from the BCRP's economic research team, Peru was the first economically large country in Latin America to implement the dirty float, this in the 1990s.\par \par \uc2 "The adoption of the floating exchange rate regime was part of a comprehensive macroeconomic stabilization plan that sought to reduce hyperinflation and get out of the deep recession in which the economy was in the early 1990s\u8243\'20\'33, the book points out.\par \par \uc2 Countries such as Mexico, Brazil, Colombia and Chile also implemented this type of exchange rate, although they eventually abandoned it.\par \par \uc2 "In other countries, the timing and magnitude of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could be known more clearly, making it possible to manipulate the system. The BCRP has more discretion, which allows it to be more effective in reducing volatility," Diego Macera, an expert at the Peruvian Institute of Economics and a member of the BCRP, told the BBC.\par \par \uc2 "There is no \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  more in the exchange market than the Peruvian one," Waldo Mendoza told the BBC.\par \par \uc2 What will Peru do about inflation?The government needs to hand out cash, help communal kitchens and boost social programs while considering long-term reforms, said BCRP member Jose Tavara.\par \par \uc2 "The main problem is people's hunger, inflation hits the poorest households, because they spend a higher proportion of their income to buy food, T\u225\'00\'E1vara asserted. "In the cities, the poorest people are suffering, massive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is required from this government."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  has pledged to spend 3.2 billion soles ($845 million) on measures to contain inflation and soften its impact on the neediest. These plans include a tax exemption on diesel purchases, subsidies for fertilizers and aid for community kitchens. Together, the expenditures amount to four times the normal social program budget for a year, according to Economy Minister Oscar Graham.\par \par \uc2 With information from \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  and BBC.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020220627ei6r001yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
