\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Peru's international reserves would rise 2.6% due to \uc2 IMF\uc2  allocation of US$ 1,800 million\b0\par\par\uc2 1,046 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 August 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 By Felipe Hern\u225\'00\'E1ndez\par \par \uc2 The special drawing rights (SDRs) allocated to Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Peru by the \uc2 International Monetary Fund\uc2  (\uc2 IMF\uc2 ) will strengthen their external balance sheets, but are unlikely to have any impact on fiscal or public sector debt figures. While a positive development, the assets do not significantly change the countries' challenging economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IMF\uc2  allocated the SDRs last week. Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Peru received a combined total of 13.5 billion SDRs, equivalent to about US$19.1 billion. That represents about 1.1% of their aggregate GDP.\par \par \uc2 SDRs are likely to be used to increase international reserves. Current institutional frameworks make it difficult to use them to pay down debt, as proposed in Mexico, or to finance part of the budget deficit, as proposed in Colombia.\par \par \uc2 In Chile, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  aims to add US$12 billion to international reserves by May 2022. The allocations could reduce the amount of dollars the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  still has to buy in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market by $2.4 billion.\par \par \uc2 Mexico gets biggest boost, Peru the smallest\par \par \uc2 Mexico was allocated SDR 8.5 billion, equivalent to about US$ 12.1 billion, the largest amount in the country sample in absolute terms and relative to international reserves, as of July 2021. Colombia was allocated the largest amount relative to GDP in 2020. Peru received 1.3 billion SDRs, or about US$1.8 billion, the smallest amount of the three counts.\par \par \uc2 Central banks decide fate of SDRs\par \par \uc2 SDRs have no significant \uc2 IMF\uc2  conditions. Countries use them according to their own laws and institutional arrangements. The allocations were conceived by the \uc2 IMF\uc2  to help countries fight the pandemic and support their economies. It was thought that they could help increase international reserves, limit excessive monetary tightening, add fiscal stimulus, and/or retire costly public debt.\par \par \uc2 SDRs are part of each country's international reserves. In Mexico, Chile, Colombia and Peru, they are included in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet. In all four countries, central banks are independent from the government, which implies that SDRs are managed by the monetary authorities.\par \par \uc2 Likely increase in international reserves\par \par \uc2 Barring a surprise, SDRs should generate higher international reserves in all four countries, with \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets and liabilities increasing by equal amounts. Mexico's reserves would rise 6.3% from their July level, the most among the countries in \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  Intelligence's sample. They would rise 4.9% in Chile, 4.7% in Colombia and 2.6% in Peru. This would strengthen each country's external balance, but not significantly. Peru's international reserves would remain the highest relative to GDP.\par \par \uc2 The additional reserves would give central banks more ammunition to sell U.S. dollars if they wanted to. Bloomberg Intelligence does not expect any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now, but officials may consider it if \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  come under significant weakening pressure or spikes in exchange rate volatility.\par \par \uc2 This is more likely in \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  bias and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains under weakening pressure despite this year's sharp depreciation. It could happen in Colombia, where the exchange rate has weakened significantly since January. It seems less likely in Mexico; the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has remained relatively stable and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a bias against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is the outlier. It has been buying dollars since January, when it announced plans to buy US$12 billion over the next 15 months to boost international reserves. So far it has bought about US$6.2 billion through daily auctions. The SDRs could reduce the remaining dollars to be bought by $2.4 billion. That would mean that only $3.5 billion is needed to reach the target. Meanwhile, potentially lower demand could support the Chilean peso.\par \par \uc2 Institutions limit use of SDRs for fiscal and debt targets\par \par \uc2 Mexican President \uc2 Andres Manuel Lopez Obrador\uc2  said there were discussions about using SDRs to pay off costly public debt. Colombian government officials floated the idea of using the allocation to finance part of this year's fiscal deficit.\par \par \uc2 Such plans seem highly unlikely because direct \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  financing of the government is illegal in Mexico, Chile and Peru. It is possible in Colombia, but requires the approval of all seven members of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's board. This has been considered in the past, but was deemed unnecessary if Colombia still had access to external and/or domestic financial markets.\par \par \uc2 Even so, indirect financing-purchase of government bonds by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the secondary market-can be done without prior notice, except in Chile; special \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorization is needed, with the support of at least four of the five board members. In all four countries, the decision would be the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's, independent of the government. It would require the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to convert SDRs into dollars - or euros, sterling, yen or yuan - and then use them to buy external government debt, or sell them and use the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy local government bonds.\par \par \uc2 Both options would have a neutral monetary policy impact. They would not affect the level or cost of government debt, but would provide an additional source of demand in the secondary market. Purchases of local government bonds would imply a supply of dollars backing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The swap of SDRs for government debt would increase the credit and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 In Mexico, exchange rate policy is decided by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Commission. This committee is composed of the minister of finance and two of his or her alternates, plus the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor and two of his or her alternates. The committee could decide to sell dollars to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. This would have an impact on reserves, SDRs and the exchange rate, and would not provide financing for the government. Bloomberg Intelligence considers the scenario of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Committee making any changes to be highly unlikely.\par \par \uc2 Felipe Hern\u225\'00\'E1ndez covers Latin America for Bloomberg Economics in New York. He has more than 15 years of experience covering Latam at firms such as \uc2 Banco Santander\uc2  and \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210831eh8v0086p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
