\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  in  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Market as Sol Depreciates -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,405 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Juli 2021\par 21:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2021, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 1526 ET - \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $293M on the spot market as the sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciated to a historic low against the USD. The sol closed Friday at 4.07 per dollar, versus 3.928 in the previous session. The sol fell amid investor concerns about President Pedro Castillo's administration, which took power on Wednesday. Castillo appointed far-left politicians to his cabinet, including the prime minister and foreign minister, appearing to shun more moderate advisers. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1428 ET - The estimated 2.5% annualized advance in Canada's 2Q GDP is the latest in a series of positive surprises to the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  's forecasts, \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2  economist \uc2 Derek Holt\uc2  says. Holt notes the economy outperformed the \uc2 BoC\uc2 's earlier forecasts for both 4Q 2020 and 1Q growth, and the latest data from \uc2 Statistics Canada\uc2  suggest it's now set to surpass the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's most recent forecast for 2Q, which called for 2% annualized growth. "Given the persistent upsides to the \uc2 BoC\uc2 's growth forecasts and to its inflation forecasts... there doesn't appear to be a need for emergency levels of monetary stimulus to persist," Holt says. (kim.mackrael@wsj.com; @kimmackrael)\par \par \uc2 1349 ET - \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will probably choose to pick up the pace of recent rate hikes and raise its benchmark lending rate, the Selic, by a full point to 5.25%, after three consecutive increases of 75 basis points apiece, according to Alberto Ramos, an economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in New York. Since the previous rate hike in June, inflation has accelerated and industrial production, retail sales and services have all shown strength, he said. Policy makers, who will probably remain positive about the outlook for the economy, might also choose to eventually raise the Selic to a level higher than what would be considered neutral to help ground inflation expectations for 2022, said Ramos. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1311 ET - Cryptocurrency traders from the US, UK and Turkey often list countries other than their real ones when posting about their trading on social media, according to a report released this morning. The report from data firm Inca Digital used the \uc2 Twitter\uc2  activity of traders on seven major crypto exchanges to analyze which countries they came from. It found hundreds of Americans trading on exchanges such as FTX, which are supposedly off-limits to US traders. "Most of the users that are supposed to reside in the United States, United Kingdom and Turkey are showing other locations in their bio," the report observes. ( alexo@wsj.com, @aosipovich)\par \par \uc2 1244 ET - A surge in inflation that \uc2 St. Louis Fed\uc2  leader \uc2 James Bullard\uc2  says Friday had outstripped \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  expectations doesn't seem to be leaking very much into underlying price pressures. The \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of Dallas\uc2  says its Trimmed Mean PCE hit 2% in June from the same month a year ago, from a 1.9% annualized rise in May. The Trimmed Mean was at 1.7% at the start of 2021. The index strips out the biggest price movers in a given month to better gauge inflation trends. The June number compares to the 4% rise seen in the personal consumption expenditures price index for the same period. Fed officials expect inflation to ease over time and link the jump to pressures tied to the economic reopening process, although some worry high inflation might prove more persistent. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1145 ET - Peru's sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened sharply on Friday against the USD, amid investor concerns over recently-inaugurated President Pedro Castillo. The sol depreciated to 4.02 against the dollar from about 3.92 the day before, an historic low. Castillo announced his cabinet yesterday, which includes several far-left members while shunning more moderate advisers. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1106 ET - \uc2 MUFG Bank\uc2  advises taking a short position in the euro against the pound, betting on the exchange rate to fall. It targets 0.8350 in EUR/GBP and places a stop loss at 0.8670. EUR/GBP last trades at 0.8528. "We have been encouraged by recent positive Covid-19 data from the U.K. suggesting that the risk of further pandemic-related disruption to the U.K. economic recovery has diminished," \uc2 MUFG\uc2  analysts say. The U.K.'s vaccine rollout and the number of people who have already had Covid-19 should dampen the severity of the current and any future waves of infections, preventing any fresh restrictions, they say. The economic recovery and rising inflation will pressure the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to scale back stimulus, they say. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1044 ET - The Norwegian krone could rise in coming days as risk appetite improves and oil prices recover but gains will be limited, ING says. Low summer liquidity, which is set to continue through August, will restrict the krone's appreciation, ING analysts say. "But with \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  being the most hawkish G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 --two [interest rate] hikes expected in the reminder of the year--and the liquidity expected to come back after the current summer lull, there is more upside to NOK in the latter part of the third quarter," they say. EUR/NOK last trades up 0.8% at 10.4645. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1039 ET - Brazil's fiscal situation is being helped by high commodity prices, high inflation and a recovering economy, but the stock of debt remains high and needs to come down, according to Alberto Ramos, an economist at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  in New York. \uc2 Brazil's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday that the country's public sector had a primary deficit of 65.5 billion reais, the equivalent of $12.8 billion, in June, down from 188.7 billion in the same month of 2020. The decline in the primary deficit from a year ago is a significant improvement, but the federal government needs to engineer a sustained declining trend in debt and reduce the deficit once the pandemic is under control, Ramos said. (jeffrey.lewis@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1021 ET - The prospect of higher inflation is driving capital to alternative asset classes, according to a survey that Vidrio, a provider of investment-management systems, conducted with investors that combined manage more than $100B in alternative assets. Eighty-percent of the respondents say they have made at least moderate adjustments to include more alternative assets in their portfolios in response to near-term inflation expectations. When asked which alternative asset classes are likely to see the largest inflation-driven capital inflow, with the option of choosing more than one, 44% of the respondents say private equity and an equal 44% point to commodity funds, while 33% and 22% select macro-hedge funds and private credit, respectively. (luis.garcia@wsj.com; @lhvgarcia)\par \par \uc2 1013 ET - Major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates should trade sideways in coming weeks as the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  are unlikely to provide any fresh policy signals, \uc2 Unicredit\uc2  says. Further key communication from the Fed and the \uc2 ECB\uc2  will probably be delayed until Aug. 26-28, when Fed Chair \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  speaks at the annual Jackson Hole policy symposium, or Sept. 9, when the \uc2 ECB\uc2  holds its next policy decision, \uc2 Unicredit\b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Roberto Mialich says. That will keep major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs, including EUR/USD, GBP/USD and USD/JPY, trading in familiar ranges with the dollar maintaining the "bearish tone" that re-emerged after the Fed's cautious stance at Wednesday's policy meeting, he says. (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1012 ET - The Turkish lira will weaken even if the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  holds off on cutting interest rates, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  says. Data on Tuesday is likely to show continued high inflation in July, which could encourage \uc2 Turkey's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to delay the start of easing monetary policy until later this year despite pressure from President Recep \uc2 Tayyip Erdogan\uc2  to cut rates, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  's \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Roberto Mialich says. "Yet, high inflation is set to erode further real rates [rates adjusted for inflation] in Turkey and to weigh on the TRY," he says. "We would not buy any TRY recovery attempt in the meantime." (renae.dyer@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 July 30, 2021 15:26 ET (19:26 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020210730eh7u003x2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
