 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FINANCIAL RISK MANAGEMENT\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial risk management is carried out by the Corporation's Management. Management oversees the general management of risks in specific areas, such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate risk, price risk, cash flow and fair value interest rate risk, credit risk, the use of derivative and non-derivative financial instruments and the investment of excess liquidity, which are supervised and monitored periodically.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4.1\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financial risk factors\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Corporation's activities expose it to a variety of financial risks: market risk (including \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk, price risk, fair value interest rate risk and cash flow interest rate risk), credit risk and liquidity risk. The Corporation's overall risk management program focuses on the unpredictability of financial markets and seeks to minimize potential adverse effects on the Corporation's financial performance.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 a)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market risks\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 i)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Corporation is exposed to exchange rate risk as a result of the transactions carried out locally in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and due to its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  abroad. As of December 31, 2020 and June 30, 2021, this exposure is mainly concentrated in fluctuations of U.S. dollar, the Chilean and Colombian Pesos.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - 9 -\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The balances of financial assets and liabilities denominated in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  correspond to balances in U.S. dollars, which are expressed at the published bid and ask exchange rate in effect at that date, according to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  exchange rate:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                       At December 31,2020            AtJune 30,2021  \par                                                                                 \par Soles (a)                                 3.624                       3.866     \par Chilean Pesos (b)                         711.24                      727.76    \par Colombian Pesos (c)                       3,432.50                    3,756.67  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (a)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Soles published by the Superintendency of Banking, Insurance and Pension Fund Administrators (SBS).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (b)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Chilean pesos published by the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Chile\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (c)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Colombian pesos published by Banco of the Republica of Colombia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The consolidated statement of financial position includes the following:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                As of December 31, 2020                As of June 30, 2021  \par                                        S/(000)   \{2\}                      USD(000)             S/(000) \{2\}         USD(000)  \par                                                                                                                                        \par Assets                                 2,125,400                                   586,479             2,280,274              589,828  \par Liabilities                            1,165,475                                   321,599             2,023,607              523,437  \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the periods ended June 30, 2020 and 2021, the Corporation's exchange gains and losses for the Peruvian Sol, the Chilean and Colombian Pesos exposure against the U.S. dollar was:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2        2020               2021  \par Gain        259,174              184,352    \par Loss        (260,712) \{2\}        (197,942) \{2\}\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ii)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Price risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Management considers that the exposure of the Corporation to the price risk of its investments in mutual funds, bonds, and equity securities is low since the invested amounts are not significant. Any fluctuation in their fair value will not have any significant impact on the balances reported in the consolidated financial statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 iii)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash flow and fair value interest rate risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Corporation's interest rate risk mainly arises from its long-term borrowings. Borrowings issued at variable rates expose the Corporation to cash flow interest rate risk. Borrowings issued at fixed rates expose the Corporation to fair value interest rate risk.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 b)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Credit risk arises from cash and cash equivalents and deposits with banks and financial institutions, as well as customer credit counterparties, including the outstanding balance of accounts receivable and committed transactions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - 10 -\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Concerning to loans to related parties, the Corporation has measures in place to ensure the recovery of these loans through the controls maintained by the Corporate Finance Management and the performance evaluation conducted by the Board of Directors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Management does not expect the Corporation to incur any losses from the performance by these counterparties, except for the ones already recorded at the financial statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 c)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Liquidity risk\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prudent liquidity risk management implies maintaining sufficient cash and cash equivalents, the availability of funding through an adequate number of sources of committed credit facilities and the capacity to close out positions in the market. Historically, the Corporation cash flows enabled it to meet its obligations. Due to the COVID-19 pandemic (Note 1-e), the Corporation has implemented various actions to reduce its exposure to liquidity risk, and has developed a Financial Plan based on several steps, which were designed assuming attaining a plea bargain agreement within a reasonable time frame. The Financial Plan aims to enable compliance with the various obligations at the corporate and group companies' levels.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Corporation's Corporate Finance Office monitors rolling forecasts of the Corporation's liquidity requirements to ensure it exists sufficient cash to meet \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  needs so that the Corporation does not breach borrowing limits or covenants, where applicable, on any of its borrowing facilities. Less significant financing transactions are controlled by the Finance Management of each subsidiary.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such forecasting takes into consideration the Corporation's debt financing plans, covenant compliance, compliance with internal ratio targets in the statement of financial position and, if applicable, external regulatory or legal requirements, for example, foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  restrictions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Surplus cash held by the operating entities over the balance required for working capital management is invested in interest-bearing checking accounts or time deposits, selecting instruments with appropriate maturities and sufficient liquidity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The table below analyzes the Corporation's financial liabilities into relevant maturity groupings based on the remaining period from the date of the consolidated statement of financial position to the contractual maturity date. The amounts disclosed in the ta