 to 0.70.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                                               At December 31,2020               At June 30,2021  \par Total financial liabilities and bonds (Note 15 and Note 16)                       1,831,079                       1,795,732  \par Less: Cash and cash equivalents (Note 8)                                          (900,168) \{2\}                   (826,879) \{2\}\par Net debt                                                                          930,911                         968,853    \par Total equity                                                                      1,595,296                       1,514,519  \par Total capital                                                                     2,526,207                       2,483,372  \par Gearing ratio                                                                     0.37                            0.39       \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 4.3\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fair value estimation\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For the classification of the type of valuation used by the Corporation for its financial instruments at fair value, the following levels of measurement have been established.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - 12 -\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Level 1: Measurement based on quoted prices in active markets for identical assets or liabilities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Level 2: Measurement based on inputs other than quoted prices included within Level 1 that are observable for the asset or liability, either directly (that is, as prices) or indirectly (that is, derived from prices).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Level 3: Measurement based on inputs for the asset or liability that are not based on observable market data (that is, unobservable inputs, generally based on internal estimates and assumptions of the Corporation).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The table below shows the Corporation's liabilities measured at fair value:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2                                          Level 3   \par As of December 31, 2020                            \par                                                    \par Financial liabilities                              \par Other financial entities (Note 15-b)               152,523   \par                                                              \par As of June 30, 2021                                          \par                                                              \par Financial liabilities                                        \par Other financial entities (Note 15-b)               162,549   \par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 5.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CRITICAL ACCOUNTING ESTIMATES AND JUDGMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Estimates and judgments used are continuously evaluated and are based on historical experience and other factors, including expectations of future events that are believed to be reasonable under the circumstances.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In preparing these condensed interim consolidated financial statements, the significant judgements made by management in applying the Corporation's accounting policies and the key sources of uncertainty were the same as those that applied to the consolidated financial statements for the year ended December 31, 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 6.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SEASONALITY OF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 OPERATIONS\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The Corporation does not present seasonality in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of any of its subsidiaries; however, economic activities temporarily restricted due to COVID-19 pandemic and government measures implemented to contain the spread of the virus. As a result, this situation affected negatively Corporation's revenues and financial position (Note 1.e).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 OPERATING SEGMENTS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating segments are reported consistently with the internal reports that are reviewed by the Corporation' chief decision-maker; that is, the Executive Committee, which is led by the Chief Executive Officer. This Committee acts as the highest authority in making \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  decisions, responsible for allocating resources and evaluating the performance of each operating segment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Corporation's operating segments are assessed by the activities of the following business units: (i) engineering and construction, (ii) infrastructure, and (iii) real estate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As set forth under IFRS 8, reportable segments by significance of income are: 'engineering and construction' and 'infraestructure'. However, the Corporation has voluntarily decided to report on all its operating segments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - 13 -\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inter-segmental sales transactions are entered into at prices that are similar to those that would have been agreed to with unrelated third parties. Revenues from external customers reported are measured in a manner consistent with the basis of preparation of the financial statements. Sales of goods are related to Real State segment. Revenues from services are related to other segments.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Corporation sales and receivables are not concentrated on a few customers. There is no external customer that represents 10% or more of the Goup's revenue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The table below shows the Corporation's financial statements by operating segments:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Operating segments financial position                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            \par Segment reporting                                                                                                       