\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 Peruvian sun crumble in panic over possible election victory by communist Pedro Castillo\b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 April 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 The electoral contest in Peru sowed a climate of uncertainty that resulted in a strong panic at the eventual victory of communist candidate Pedro Castillo, known for his extremist positions.\par \par \uc2 Markets reacted and there was a sharp devaluation in the Peruvian sun. Peru's exchange rate against the dollar reached a price of S/3.79 last Friday, an all-time high that could not be reversed despite strong \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BCRP).\par \par \uc2 The BCRP went out to place \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  swaps of S/ 697 million in four trades and Certificates Re-adjustable Deposits (CDR) per S/ 280 million in three trades, in an attempt to contain the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The devaluation in the Peruvian place of exchange accumulates a 4.73% increase so far in 2021, most concentrated as a result of electoral uncertainty. Demand for suns is also expected to fall.\par \par \uc2 This behavior was a clear contrast to the region in terms of last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Countries such as Colombia, Chile, Uruguay and Brazil recorded mixed results without a clear trend, while in Peru the fall in the sun was relevant.\par \par \uc2 The S&P/BVL Peru General Index, which reflects the stock market performance in Lima Square, has been alerted by strong contractions since the first polls were known giving Castillo a certain advantage over Fujimori.\par \par \uc2 The Peruvian stock market collapsed by 3.48% as soon as the first polls gave Pedro Castillo the winner were known. The contraction was much more intense in strategic companies for the country.\par \par \uc2 There was a great aversion to risk by many foreign investors who view with distrust of economic dynamics in Peru before a possible new president with a Chavista vocation and with expropriational proposals, something that had not been seen in that country since the 1980s.\par \par \uc2 The communist candidate's party, "Peru Libre", had no qualms about openly proposing the nationalization of public services and strategic sectors in the economy, such as electricity, gas, mining, hydrocarbons and even much of tourism and foreign trade.\u160\'00\'A0The ads sowed panic in companies that have these services.\par \par \uc2 This was the case of Peru's five largest mining companies, in short in size and profitability, showing violent drops in their stock market value.\u160\'00\'A0The companies affected were: \uc2 Panoro Minerals\uc2  (-27.71%), Minsur (-24.66%), \uc2 Cerro Verde\uc2  (-21.66%), Volcan (-20.24%) Nexa (-18.37%).\par \par \uc2 Castillo's rejection of the current Peruvian Constitution, established in 1993 and a pillar of the economic model based on the market economy, only increased investor uncertainty.\par \par \uc2 Among other postulates, the Peruvian Constitution sets basic limits on political power, such as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  autonomy and monetary policy, a key piece in explaining price stability in Peru in recent decades.\par \par \uc2 In return \uc2 Keiko Fujimori\uc2  promised to continue with Peru's economic model and today's market economy, and denounced the meddling of "21st-century socialism" in his country.\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210425eh4o000c1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
