\par\par \uc2 CE Noticias Financieras English\par \b \uc2 MARKETS A.LATINA-Close disparates, between oil uptick and dollar strength\b0\par\par\uc2 597 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2021\par \uc2 CE NoticiasFinancieras\par \uc2 NFINCE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 Content Engine LLC \par \par \uc2 (Update with closures) By Nelson Bocanegra BOGOT\u193\'00\'C1, 4 Sea (Reuters) - Latin America's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets closed disparate on Thursday, in a volatile session amid news that strengthened the dollar while causing rises in oil prices, the region's major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  generator. * Some \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  succumbed to the uptick in the dollar at three-month highs, after Federal Reserve Chairman \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  expressed no concern about a recent liquidation of \uc2 U.S. Treasury\uc2  bonds, as some traders expected, which raised debt yields and demand for the green bill. * But other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  reacted favorably to the sharp rise in oil, from 4% in the session, reaching a peak of one year after \uc2 OPEC\uc2  and its allies agreed to extend most of their crude cuts until April, after deciding that the recovery in demand for the coronavirus pandemic is still fragile.\par \par \uc2  * The Colombian peso led the profits, rising from 0.94% to 3,646.40 units per dollar, while the COLCAP stock index reversed the initial trend and ended with a drop from 0.75% to 1,337.12 points. * In Brazil, the real closed steady at 5.66 units per dollar, while the stock market's benchmark, Bovespa, rose 0.1.35%, to 112,690 points and is on its way to achieving its first week of earnings in a month. * In Mexico, the peso ended with a 0.70% decline and the benchmark S&P;/BMV \uc2 IPC\uc2  share index declined by 0.83%. * Chilean peso closed the day with a 0.33% advance, at 725.80/726.10 units per dollar in a high volatility session. Santiago's IPSA stock market index ended with a 1.81% recoil at 4,700.57 points. * In Argentina, the peso lost 0.08% controlled to a new record low, because of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's exchange rate policy that leverages the arrival of fresh dollars from exporting cereals to recover reserves lost throughout 2020. * For its part, the S&P; Merval index of the Buenos Aires stock exchange rose by 1.02%, amid intraday ups and downs. * Peru's sun lost 0.35%, to 3,684/3,687 units, a new historical low. \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market with $30 million and exchange swaps of 1,099.9 million suns. * The reference index of the Peruvian stock exchange closed with a fall of 1.35%, to 596.58 points, due to the decline of shares of the mining sector. Quotes at 2145 GMT Stock indices Quote Var pct Var pct daily in the year MSCI Markets 1,378.02 1.35 6.68 emerging MSCI Latin America 2,218.73 -0.65 -9.5 Bovespa Brazil 112,690.1 7 1.35 -5,3161 CPI Mexico 46,004.19 -0.8 4.40 Argentina MerVal 47,808.21 1,021 57.73 COLCAP Colombia 1,337.12 -0.75 -7.783 IPSA Chile 4,700.57 -1.81 -7.92 Selective Peru 596.57 -1.35 16.44 Dollar vs. Coins Quote Var pct Var pct monthly in Brazilian Real year 5,6693 -1.25 -8.39 Mexican Peso 2 1,1180 -1.29 -5.81 Chilean Peso 731.6 -1.16 -2.92 Colombian Peso 3,643.15 0.07 -5.97 Peruvian Sun 3,6818 -0.95 -1.77 Argentine Peso 90.29 +0.00 -6.72 (Report by Nelson Bocanegra, additional report by Froil\u225\'00\'E1n Romero in Santiago, Jorge Otaola in Buenos Aires and Marco Aquino in Lima)\par \par \uc2 Dokument NFINCE0020210304eh34008m4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw103\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal1545\pichgoal600
