\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru \highlight\uc2  Sol at 9-Yr Low;  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank Intervenes \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,259 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2015\par 20:38\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:38 ET - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  increased its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today as the sol weakened further against the dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $90M on the spot market as the sol closed at 3.314/dollar, the weakest rate in 9 years. It sold $33M yesterday and $12M Wednesday. The sol is down 13% the past year. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 14:36 ET - InterContinental (IHG) gives up its gains for the day after denying press reports the hotelier is exploring its options. The shares rose as much as 7% in London trading and more than 5% in New York before saying the board "is not considering a potential sale or merger." In July, IHG also denied reports it was in merger talks with Starwood (HOT)--which is on the block. IHG is down 0.3% at $39.76. (craig.karmin@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:15 ET - While "Spectre" at $5.3M grossed more in late-Thursday debut than the $4.6M brought in 3 years ago by "Skyfall," the latest James Bond film isn't expected to match the $88.4 North American weekend haul generated by its immediate predecessor. "Skyfall" was by far the biggest Bond film at the box office, grossing $304M domestically and $1.1B globally. Nonetheless, it will debut big, potentially at $80M, as Bond kicks off the holiday movie season. It's a welcome change from Halloween-impacted last weekend, the weakest of 2015. "Spectre" alone will exceed what the top 12 movies grossed at $62.7M. And there's also the potential $50M debut this weekend from "Peanuts." (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 14:02 ET - Investors have known for some time there was a good chance the Fed would raise interest rates this year, but REIT stocks are nonetheless getting hit particularly hard in the wake of latest jobs report--which has boosted expectations the FOMC acts next month. The MSCI US REIT index is down 3.5% and none of its top 10 components are up. The index is on pace for its biggest drop since Aug. 24's 4.7% slide. (liam.pleven@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:00 ET - Digital sales of movies continued to be the sole bright spot for Hollywood's home-entertainment industry in 3Q as DVD sales and rentals and digital rental revenue all dropped. According to the Digital Entertainment Group, a trade association, sales of movies through digital services like iTunes rose 14% on-year to $433M, topping video-on-demand rental revenue; it dropped 1% to $419M. Meanwhile, sales of Blu-ray discs and DVDs fell a combined 14% to $1.15B and disc-rental revenue dropped 12% to $703M. (ben.fritz@wsj.com; @benfritz)\par \par \uc2 13:54 ET - Actuarial firm Milliman provides a new analysis of premiums on the Affordable Care Act exchanges--they're going up overall, but the effect varies by state. Among the 38 relying on the federal marketplace, the biggest average boosts were 36% in Oklahoma, 35% in Montana and 32% in Alaska. Indiana, Mississippi and Maine had declines. In an observation which might draw attention from antitrust regulators who are reviewing big proposed insurance mergers, Milliman notes "areas with fewer carriers tended to see higher rate increases." A number of states are losing nonprofit cooperative insurers created by the law, many of which are shutting down. (anna.mathews@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:40 ET - Colombia's inflation surged past market expectations in October, putting the annual rate at a 6-year high of 5.9%. Last month's rise doubled the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's estimate, and its forecast prompted a half-point interest-rate hike last week. Food is driving inflation right now amid drought caused by El Nino. Rapidly increasing prices could prompt another rate increase sooner than had been expected, says Credicorp Capital in Bogota, especially since \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials said last week that GDP is estimated to grow more than previously thought. "The better-than-expected activity figures" give room for a quarter-point hike on the 15th, it adds. (sara.munoz@wsj.com; @sarasmunoz)\par \par \uc2 13:34 ET - Off-price has been a rare shining light in a broadly soft department-store and apparel environment since the recession. As RBC starts coverage of the sector, it sees the strength continuing as it puts Burlington (BURL) and \uc2 TJX\uc2  at outperform. The investment bank notes that despite this decade's growth, the off-price space still only makes up 15% of domestic apparel sales despite the industry's revenue doubling the past decade. RBC expects thousand more stores to ultimately open. But BURL falls 6% while \uc2 TJX\uc2  drops 2% amid broad weakness in retail stocks today. Ross (ROST), which RBC fencesits on right now amid a lack of upside-valuation potential. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 13:25 ET - Analysts note 3Q results from \uc2 Cigna\uc2  (CI) and \uc2 Humana\uc2  (HUM) are the latest evidence of insurers' struggles with the individual market under the Affordable Care Act. CI's commercial medical-loss ratio was higher than consensus, notes Leerink, and the shortfall is "from underperformance in individual, which is a theme given challenges on exchanges." \uc2 Wells Fargo\uc2  headlines its HUM note: "Q3 Okay, Individual Business Deteriorates...Again." It says the "weak performance" on the individual business comes even though HUM continues to maintain a $241M receivable from the ACA's risk-corridor program. For its first year, the federal program paid out only a small share of what insurers requested. (anna.mathews@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:21 ET - In the wake of the jobs report, economists from \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  say a December rate increase is "very likely," noting not only has payrolls rebounded following tepid growth in late summer but U3 and U6 keep falling. Meanwhile, average hourly earnings growth hit a new high for the cycle, a sign wage inflation picks up some momentum. \uc2 Ward McCarthy\uc2 , Fed watcher at Jefferies, says the biggest risk to December liftoff is "a drop in crude oil prices. Otherwise, the stars are aligned." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 13:17 ET - There's a 40% Off Sale at \uc2 Men's Wearhouse\uc2  (MW) today. Unfortunately, it's the retailer's stock. MW is getting punished after executives warned of struggles with the acquisition of Jos. A Bank. After that chain's F3Q same-store sales skidded 15% amid MW's effort to wean Jos. A Bank customers off 4-for-1 promotions, it projects a 20-25% slump this quarter. "We've seen the movie before where a major change in tactics takes a long time to resonate," says Jefferies, which downgrades MW from buy and halves its price target to $32. It's down 43% at $22.81, hitting a 5-year low and notching what would easily be the stock's biggest-ever decline. (marcelo.prince@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:11 ET - The number of active oil rigs in the US fell 6 to 572 the past week, \uc2 Baker Hughes\uc2  says as the data mark 10 straight weeks of declines. The count is now 64% below October 2014's peak. While the drop in rig activity could lead to reduced US output, increased rig efficiency and new production from already-drilled wells is keeping daily output high. Natural-gas rigs rose 2 to 199. (dan.molinski@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 November 06, 2015 14:38 ET (19:38 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151106ebb6003fm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppic