\par\par \b \uc2 Peru  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Restarts Sale of USD -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,448 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2015\par 22:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 1740 EDT - The \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2  restarted its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market this week by selling USD for the first time in two months as the sol depreciates against the greenback. On Tuesday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $5M at a rate of 3.2798 per USD, its first \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Aug. 27. On Wednesday, it sold another $8 million at 3.2821 per USD. The sol closed today at 3.282 per USD, a depreciation of 12.55% over the last 12 months. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 2139 GMT - It's a good time for investors to rev up their portfolios with shares in car dealership AP Eagers (APE.AU), says Morgans technical analyst Violeta Todorova. She says the relative strength index indicator remains firmly in bull-market range, signaling the stock will start to move higher from their last trade at A$10.60. "A break above resistance of A$10.89 is likely, which will trigger a rally to A$12.00," Todorova says. "We are comfortable to accumulate the stock at current price levels." (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 17:38 EDT - YELP reports better-than-expected 3Q revenue and guides 4Q sales above Wall Street estimates on strong local advertising account gains. YELP's business is based on user-generated reviews and it in turn sells advertising to those businesses. Local advertising accounts grew 37% to approximately 104K and that tops the 102K consensus. YELP adds that adoption of its mobile phone app grew 39% to 20M, and made comScore's top 25 app & web properties list in September. YELP is up 2% at $22.49 in after-hours trading, but still off close to 60% YTD. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com; @patmsheridan)\par \par \uc2 17:33 EDT - \uc2 MDC Partners\uc2 ' (MDCA) \uc2 Crispin Porter + Bogusky\uc2  ad agency recently picked up the global account for \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 , adding to other MDCA global account wins over the past year such as Infiniti and Johnny Walker. MDCA, which is far smaller than its rival ad holding companies, declined to comment on the size of the \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  win on its third-quarter earnings call. MDCA said it doesn't expect to see any top-line impact from the \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  account and said it is a "2016 opportunity for us." CEO \uc2 Scott Kauffman\uc2  says the company's new business pipeline is as big as it's ever been. "Given our relative market share, we love it when accounts come up for review because odds are they aren't our accounts," he said. (nathalie.tadena@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2123 GMT - It wasn't a certainty, but the RBNZ's decision to leave the cash rate unchanged at 2.75% was in line with our expectations and the consensus, says ANZ Bank Chief Economist Cameron Bagrie. He notes the stronger NZD is now front and center - and the RBNZ has tied the outlook for monetary policy more directly to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements, stating that if NZD strength is sustained and dampens the outlook for tradable inflation "this would require a lower interest rate path than would otherwise be the case." Overall, he says the tone of the statement was as expected and "our base case remains that one further official cash rate cut will be delivered next year (March)." (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2122 GMT - Today's decision by the \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  to leave interest rates at 2.75% "is just a pause in a loosening cycle that we believe will end with rates falling to 2.00%," says Paul Dales, chief economist at Capital Economics. By stating again that "some further reduction in the official cash rate seems likely," the RBNZ sent a strong signal that it still plans to cut interest rates to 2.5%. "News the RBNZ is not happy with the recent strengthening in the New Zealand dollar lends some support to our view that the dollar could yet weaken to US$0.60 this year and US$0.55 next year," he adds. He notes, however, with the U.S. Fed continuing to delay its first rate hike this would probably require the RBNZ to signal it intends to reduce rates below 2.5%. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 2122 GMT - NZD/USD gets a lift after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps rates unchanged and gives "reasons not to cut rates," says Stuart Ive, a private client adviser at New Zealand trading firm \uc2 OM Financial\uc2 . He notes money markets have also pared back expectations for a December cut. He says RBNZ Gov Wheeler kept the door open to cutting rates further "but only basically if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is higher." Otherwise "it sounds like they will be on the fence," he says. NZD/USD is at 0.6696. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 1721 EDT - \uc2 LifeLock\uc2  (LOCK) shares are up sharply after the identity-theft protection company announces a preliminary settlement with the government regarding past marketing tactics and programs. Back in July, the government accused the company of violating a former settlement regarding deceptive claims and shares plunged 49%, but shares right now are up 41% in busy after-hours trading. Besides the announcement regarding the government agreement, LOCK also reports adjusted 3Q EPS and sales that are better than Wall Street expected, and gives FY15 guidance that tops the consensus. (patrick.sheridan@wsj.com; @patmsheridan)\par \par \uc2 2106 GMT - The \uc2 Reserve Bank of New Zealand\uc2  still looks likely to cut interest rates at its December meeting, after keeping rates unchanged at 2.75% Thursday, says Ray Attrill, global co-head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategy at \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  in Sydney. "The RBNZ, in just delivering its 'no change' decision, has maintained its explicit easing bias and notes that if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  keeps moving higher, it would require a lower [cash-rate] track," says Attrill. "December therefore still looks like a good bet for another move, and the New Zealand dollar reaction (up, then down, then back to fairly flat) is reasonably consistent with this view." AUD/NZD was last trading at 1.0606 early Thursday. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com; \uc2 Twitter\uc2 : @verasydney)\par \par \uc2 2106 GMT - Investors aren't fully appreciating \uc2 Senex Energy\uc2 's (SXY.AU) conversion from an incremental oil play focused on central Australia's Cooper Basin to a more transformational east coast gas story, says Canaccord Genuity. The broker keeps a buy call and A$0.24/share price target on Senex, following its latest quarterly production report. Senex pumped 280,000 barrels of oil equivalent in the three months through September, which was within 3% of Canaccord's forecast. "We highlight the quarter's A$4.4 million cash build shows Senex can self-fund its FY16 \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allow it to maintain its strong balance sheet position while progressing the Western Surat Gas Project," analyst Tim Masters says. SXY last traded at A$0.19. (david.winning@wsj.com; @dwinningWSJ)\par \par \uc2 1705 EDT - Venezuela ponied up $5B in past two weeks to pay bondholders, an important test for the government, Barclays Research says. That's because if the government wanted to default, a good time may have been before Dec. 6 midterm elections, the bank said. The government could have claimed it was helping the people, and the vote would have taken place before the negative effects of a default set in. So, Venezuela has the willingness to pay. But will it have the money? \uc2 Barclays\uc2  estimates readily available reserves will fall to just $8B by year's end. To meet next year's maturities, it may need higher oil prices and/or more Chinese loans. (David.luhnow@wsj.com; @davidluhnow)\par \par \uc2 2104 GMT - Major global central banks remain prepared to loosen money supply further, even as the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2  signaled it may raise interest rates in December. Analysts at Capital Economics list the \uc2 European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2  and \uc2 People's Bank of China\uc2  among those likely to ease further. They also think \uc2 New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will deliver another cut in December, after it left the cash rate unchanged at 2.75% Thursday. "This will just be a pause in the loosening cycle rather than the end," says economist Paul Dales. He reiterates that \uc2 Australia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also looks set to cut rates to 1.5% next year, from 2.0% currently, as it would be unable to ignore the economy's weakening inflation for long. (vera.sprothen@wsj.com; @verasydney)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 28, 2015 17:40 ET (21:40 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020151028ebas0043u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
