\par\par \b \uc2 Peru rate hike may embolden Chile, Colombia to follow suit\b0\par\par\uc2 By Mitra Taj   \par \uc2 462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 September 2015\par 21:52\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright 2015 Thomson Reuters. All Rights Reserved.   \par \par \uc2 BUENOS AIRES, Sept 11 (Reuters) - The surprise decision by \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week to hike its benchmark interest rate for the first time since 2011 may embolden regional peers also torn between weak growth and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -driven inflation to follow suit.\par \par \uc2 Central banks in Colombia and Chile have in recent meetings eyed raising rates to tame inflation fanned by their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' fall against the dollar. But they have held off due to lingering concerns that it would undermine their still-flagging economies.\par \par \uc2 "It will be a nudge for the rest of the banks to act," said 4Cast analyst Pedro Tuesta.\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  and a member of \uc2 Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board both declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 "Each one will be looking for the opportunity to make their respective modifications," as needed, said Adrian Armas, an economist with \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Peru raised its benchmark rate by 25 basis points to 3.5 percent on Thursday. The timing of the move - a week before the \uc2 U.S. Federal Reserve\uc2 's policy-setting meeting - left its bank ahead of the curve.\par \par \uc2 Expectations for a U.S. rate hike have helped the dollar surge against emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this past year. Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is down 12 percent in the past 12 months, while Chile and Colombia's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have slipped 17 and 56 percent respectively.\par \par \uc2 A U.S. rate hike could stoke further demand for greenbacks.\par \par \uc2 "With this move the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped out ahead of other central banks that have been waiting for the Fed to decide," said Mario Guerrero with Scotiabank.\par \par \uc2 Chile's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will mull a rate increase Sept. 15 while Colombia's next rate-setting meeting is Sept. 25.\par \par \uc2 Like Peru, the two countries saw inflation spike in August on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pressures.\par \par \uc2 "It is quite plausible that other central banks will also hike in the near term," said \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analyst Alberto Ramos. "Colombia may be next, and Chile and Mexico may not be that far away."\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to prop up the sol and tightened controls on derivatives to curb speculation.\par \par \uc2 The last time the bank lifted the key rate was in May 2011, after the mining-fueled economy rebounded from the global financial crisis. Today, as in neighboring Colombia and Chile, the economy is on wobblier footing.\par \par \uc2 "Raising rates while the growth remains weak is risky," said Hugo Perea with \uc2 BBVA\uc2 , "but I think we will see similar decisions in the region." (Additional Reporting by Rosalba O'Brien in Santiago and Helen Murphy in Bogota, Editing by Richard Lough and Chizu Nomiyama) \par \par \uc2 Released: 2015-9-11T20:52:43.000Z   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020150911eb9b00my5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
