\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru \highlight\uc2  Reserves in 'Good Position' Amid  \highlight22\uc2 FX Interventions \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,138 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 August 2015\par 15:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 09:03 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says it has sufficient international reserves to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to reduce \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility. The bank "has a good position with its reserves, one of the highest in the region," says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  general manager Renzo Rossini. \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been selling USD regularly on the spot market to support the local sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the dollar. Despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Peruvian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has depreciated 16.5% in the last 12-months to close Thursday at 3.306 per USD. Some economists have questioned if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to continue to support the sol with its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 8:59 EDT - Spending at all levels of government increased at the fastest pace in five years during the second quarter. Public outlays advanced 2.6% annual pace, the Commerce Department said. That's an upward revision from the initial estimate of a 0.8% advance. Federal spending was flat for the quarter and spending at the state and local level increased 4.3%, the strongest increase since 4Q 2001. (eric.morath@wsj.com, @ericmorath)\par \par \uc2 8:58 EDT - US jobless claims fell by 6,000 to 271,000 last week, a sign the labor market remains healthy. Claims had risen for four consecutive weeks during summer months that are often volatile for the data. But the four-week moving average has remained near a 15-year low, suggesting more jobs are being created than destroyed across the economy. (jeffrey.sparshott@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:56 EDT -- Solid numbers for growth and weekly jobless claims lifted the dollar against the euro and the yen. The second estimate for 2Q GDP showed the US economy grew at a swifter pace than had been thought, and above economists' expectations. The numbers boosted the dollar after a week of large market swings and amid signals from the Fed that a September interest-rate increase looks increasingly unlikely. Strong data could nudge those rate expectations back toward September liftoff. The dollar rose against the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as one euro bought $1.1251 from $1.1286 ahead of the data. The dollar's move pushed it up 0.6% for the session, leaving it near its closing New-York level one week ago. The greenback rose 0.5% against the yen to Y120.49 from Y120.18 beforehand. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:51 EDT - Investors are taking heart from more evidence that the U.S. economy is weathering tumult abroad. Stronger-than-expected reports on 2Q U.S. growth and the labor market buoy \uc2 S&P\uc2  stock futures, which are now up 22 points, or 1.2%. GDP expanded at a 3.7% annual rate in Q2, up from an initial estimate of 2.3% growth and beating expectations of 3.3% growth. Separately, initial jobless claims fall to 271,000 in the most recent week, suggesting continued health in the labor market. (saumya.vaishampayan@wsj.com; @saumvaish)\par \par \uc2 8:48 EDT - Businesses stepped up investments in 2Q, the Commerce Department said. Nonresidential fixed investment, spending on construction, equipment and software, advanced at a 3.2% annual pace in 2Q. That's an upward revision from the initial estimate of a 0.6% decline in such spending. The change contributed 0.48 percentage point to the overall upward revision of 1.4 percentage points. (eric.morath@wsj.com, @ericmorath)\par \par \uc2 8:43 EDT - Businesses built inventories, rather than let them dwindle, in the second quarter, according to a revised gross-domestic-product reading, the Commerce Department released. The change in private inventories contributed 0.22 percentage point to overall growth. The initial estimate was a 0.08 percentage point drag on the GDP advance. (eric.morath@wsj.com, @ericmorath)\par \par \uc2 8:41 EDT - US corporate profits posted the strongest quarterly increase in a year this spring, the Commerce Department said. Profits after tax and without inventory or capital consumption adjustments rose 5.1% in 2Q after increasing 2% in 1Q. It was the best quarterly gain since 2Q 2014. From a year earlier, profits advanced 7.3%. (eric.morath@wsj.com, @ericmorath)\par \par \uc2 8:38 EDT - \uc2 Treasury\uc2  prices, already under selling pressure on stabilization in global stocks, hit fresh session low after the US economy grew at a faster pace than initially reported in 2Q. Shorter-dated notes led the selling, reflecting concerns that the Fed may still raise rates in September if risky markets continue to stabilize while US data hold up. The 10-year yield was recently at 2.182% vs 2.172% Wednesday. The yield is up for a third day. The 2-year yield was 0.703% vs 0.664% Wednesday. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 8:36 EDT - The US economy expanded at a 3.7% seasonally adjusted annual pace in 2Q, the Commerce Department said. That's a much quicker pace than the first estimate of a 2.3% advance. Upward revisions to business investment, inventory building and government spending all contributed to the upward recasting of the growth figure. Last quarter's growth is also a sharp uptick from the first quarter's mild 0.6% expansion. (eric.morath@wsj.com, @ericmorath)\par \par \uc2 8:24 EDT - Small business owners' are optimistic about their own prospects, despite concerns about the broader economy. Fifty-seven percent of small firms expect their total number of employees to increase over the next 12 months, while 46% forecast growth in investment spending, according to a monthly survey of small firms by The Wall Street Journal and \uc2 Vistage International\uc2 . Nearly three-quarters expect higher revenue over the next 12 months and 57% forecast profitability to rise. The survey was completed in mid-August, before an economic slowdown in China triggered a selloff across global markets. (ruth.simon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:20 EDT - Small business owners' confidence in the overall economy is fading despite strength in their own \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a sign that the economy's gains may be slowing. In August, the portion of small businesses reporting that the U.S. economy was improving fell to its lowest level since the government shutdown of 2013, according to a monthly survey of small firms by The Wall Street Journal and \uc2 Vistage International\uc2 . The survey was completed in mid-August, before an economic slowdown in China triggered a selloff across global markets. In the most recent survey, only 42% of the 682 small companies polled said economic conditions were better than a year ago, down from 47% in July and a January peak of 67%. (ruth.simon@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 27, 2015 09:03 ET (13:03 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150827eb8r001nk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
