\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again to Buy Soles -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Juni 2015\par 22:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 16:47 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market for the second straight day, selling $7M worth of US dollars and easing depreciation pressures on the sol. On Tuesday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $49M. Over the last twelve months the sol has depreciated 12.8% against the US dollar. \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a lot of ammunition to support the sol. As of June 7, the net international reserves stood at $60.5B, equal to 18 months of imports. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 2045 GMT - NZD/USD may squeeze higher, depending on New Zealand's 1Q GDP report due this morning, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Raiko Shareef. "With such a large speculative short position likely still present in NZD, we favor the squeeze to extend modestly, depending on the local GDP report," he says. He says the market is expecting a 0.6% quarterly gain, in line with BNZ's forecast. The pair is at 0.6985. BNZ tips resistance at 0.7080. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 16:40 EDT - Even though President Rafael Correa temporarily withdrew legislation to increase taxes on inheritances and capital gains from the National Assembly, political tension continue in Ecuador as several unions and social organizations, including indigenous people, announced a series of demonstrations in the runup to a general strike planned for July. "Our battle is not only for taxes, but for a series of government policies that affect all Ecuadoreans, especially workers," says Mesias Tatamuez, head of the Cedocut Union. "We will continue taking to the streets to protest and to ask for rectifications and the derogation of several laws." (mercedes.alvaro@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:36 EDT - \uc2 Amazon.com\uc2  (AMZN) is experimenting with a plan to have regular people deliver its packages, which would bypass logistics giants. That could set up a huge battle between Amazon and companies like \uc2 UPS\uc2 , which has spent considerable time and money creating an algorithm called Orion, which automates the order in which packages are delivered along its routes. \uc2 UPS\uc2  already feels the financial pressure of delivering low-margin e-commerce goods, and has become ever-more efficient at its business, delivering packages in the U.S. at a cost of about $8 each. Yet Amazon's shipping costs grew 31% last year, faster than revenue, the Wall Street Journal reports. Uber and other companies are working to crowdsource delivery, he says. Having changed the way people shop, e-commerce companies are engaging in a new battle as algorithms make new business models possible. And we're not yet even talking about drones. (steven.rosenbush@wsj.com; @steve_rosenbush)\par \par \uc2 16:32 EDT - Range Resources (RRC) may be hit with another hefty fine in Pennsylvania. The state's \uc2 Department of Environmental Protection\uc2  is pursuing an $8.9M penalty against Range for a leaky gas well that officials say is polluting groundwater and a stream. The Texas-based energy company was notified of violations at the well in 2013, but failed to fix them, according to the state. A company plan to address the problem was recently rejected by the \uc2 DEP\uc2  as unsatisfactory. Last year, Range agreed to pay the state $4.15M after tests showed several temporary wastewater reservoirs had contaminated groundwater and soil. At the time, it was the largest fine ever levied against a shale-drilling company. (erin.ailworth@wsj.com; @ailworth)\par \par \uc2 16:29 EDT - Coking coal prices for 3Q have reportedly settled at $93 a ton, National Bank Financial says, which is down from the 2Q and below firm's previous 3Q estimate of $105/ton. That's not a surprise, but it isn't good news for the shares of steelmaking coal miner \uc2 Teck Resources\uc2  (TCK, TCK.B.T), which has already announced temporary shutdowns at its 6 Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  during the 3Q. The price outlook led \uc2 National Bank\uc2  to trim its target on TCK to C$12.50 from C$14, and it warned that could be too optimistic if contract prices keep settling at lower levels. TCK ended at C$13.67 in Toronto Wednesday. (carolyn.m.king@wsj.com; @CarolynKing1)\par \par \uc2 16:29 EDT - Inflation in economically troubled Venezuela accelerated in May to an annual rate of 108.1% from 100.7% in the previous month, BofA says using a new measure of price levels the bank developed as the South American government has not released CPI data since December. BofA's alternative measure uses 33 variables, including bank loans and monetary liquidity as well as the country's parallel market exchange rate where one USD fetches more than 400 bolivars, compared with the state's fixed official rate of 6.3 bolivars per dollar. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:27 EDT - Shorter-dated Treasurys outperform the long-end after the Fed's policy update. Zhiwei Ren, portfolio manager at Penn Mutual Asset Management, says the stance strengthens his view of a steepening curve--he is buying 5-years and selling 30-years to express that view. He notes that in the March meeting, three out of 17 Fed officials expected the Fed to raise interest rates less than two times this year. Now the number increased to seven. "This is very friendly" to shorter-dated Treasurys, Zhi says. He now sees the odds a September hike as 50%, down from the 70% he anticipated before the Fed statement. Zhi says he bought 10-year Treasury inflation-protected securities today as the Fed's slow rate-hike path increases the risk of higher inflation in the long-term, which boosts the value of TIPS. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 16:25 EDT - \uc2 Jabil Circuit\uc2  (JBL) warns on the current quarter and full-year after its fiscal 3Q revenue falls shy of Wall Street estimates. JBL sees 4Q adjusted EPS of 40c-50c on revenue of $4.45B-$4.65B, shy of analyst expectations. For the full year JBL narrows its adjusted EPS view to $1.95-$2.05 and its sales view to $17.7B-$17.9B. CEO Mark Mondello touts company's continued investment in a challenging economic environment, but shareholders turn cautious, sending the shares down 8.3% to $22.30 after hours. JBL had been up 21% over the last 12 months. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:11 EDT - \uc2 The Fed\uc2  doesn't need to raise rates right now to fight inflation, but it might as well get on with the process of increasing rates later this year, says \uc2 Jeffrey Immelt\uc2 , CEO of General Electric (\uc2 GE\uc2 ), after a speech in Washington. "People are kind of preparing themselves for it, so in some ways, the uncertainty creates its own uncertainty right now," he says. "At some point, we're going to have to get going here." The US economy "gets better every day," he adds, even though "I don't see anything that's inflationary." (nick.timiraos@wsj.com; @NickTimiraos)\par \par \uc2 16:09 EDT - \uc2 Oracle\uc2  (ORCL) shares down 6.3% in after-hours trading to $42.10. after the company misses revenue and earnings estimates for its fiscal 4Q. Revenue of $10.7B falls shy of the $10.95B expected by analysts. ORCL, like many big US firms, blames the stronger US dollar compared to other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ORCL says in constant \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , revenue would have been 3% higher, short of the 4% adjusted revenue bump expected by \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 . Adjusted earnings of 78c was shy of estimates for 87c. (shira.ovide@wsj.com; @ShiraOvide)\par \par \uc2 16:06 EDT - US stocks rise as the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  reiterates expectations it will lift rates gradually as it cuts expectations for 2015 growth following the weak 1Q. Stocks moved in and out of positive territory following the release of the Fed statement and climbed to highs as Chairwoman Yellen answered reporters questions. Utilities lead the gains as their dividends remain valuable with interest rates stuck near zero. DJIA climbs 30 points to 17935, \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  gains 4 to 2100 and the \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  rises 9 to 5064. Treasurys erase early losses following the Fed statement, with the 10-year yield at 2.31%. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 17, 2015 16:47 ET (20:47 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150617eb6h003n9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
