\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  to Sell $226M -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 20:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:13 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the third time this week to sell dollars, selling $226M on Friday to support the sol. Twice before this week the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold dollars, totaling $638M. The sol has come under sustained depreciation pressures in recent months, declining by 10.53% against the greenback in the last 12-month period. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:39 EST - Mexico consumer confidence rose in February after two months of declines on improving employment and falling inflation, but the report also raised some red flags. While consumers feel better about their household economies (that measure stood at 96.7 versus 92.3 a year ago), they also feel worse about the future of the country (down a half-point to 86.9). Banorte attributes those negative feelings to the fall in oil prices and bad social and political news. As such, it expects improvement, but Scotiabank is more downbeat. "There aren't substantial reasons to generate expectations of a sharp improvement in household consumption." (laurence.iliff@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:53 EST - Analysts and traders say one of the reasons why today's Treasury selloff is steep--the 10-year yield is set to post its biggest one-day rise since 2013--is job growth gained strength despite bad weather. "The market got caught leaning the wrong way because of expectations that the bad weather in February would suppress job growth as it did a year ago," notes Jefferies' Ward McCarthy. Meanwhile, today's action comes as he expects the bond market to be "very volatile" this year, predicting the market "will move ahead of the Fed and probably price in a rate increase on a number of occasions before the Fed finally implements the liftoff." The 10-year yield is at 2.25% versus 2.11% Thursday. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:55 EST - \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 ' Fed watcher expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to remove patience later this month from its statement not raise rates in June. \uc2 Jan Hatzius\uc2  said in a post-jobs note the FOMC will modify its forward guidance, with "the most likely scenario" seen as policy makers replacing "being 'patient' in hiking the funds rate with new language that affords the possibility--without suggesting an overwhelming likelihood--of a hike as early as June." For now, Goldman's base case rates the first hike not happening until September. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:39 EST - Sellers overwhelm the bond market today, with traders saying both fast-money types like macro hedge funds and real-money investors (i.e. money managers) shed holdings to position for a rate increase in June. But higher yields are bringing some foreign buyers into the market. \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 's Rick Klingman says buyers from both Europe and Asia--including money managers and some central banks--have bought since the jobs report. "The theme remains--there are not lots of places where you can get attractive yields" in high-grade government bonds. Klingman expects the Fed to raise rates in June, adding the pace of the tightening cycle will determine the pace of a rise in bond yields. The 10-year is at 2.238% versus 2.11% Thursday. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:35 EST - Job growth in the financial-services industry continues to recover from the losses sustained during the financial crisis. The sector added a net 10K workers last month, today's employment report shows, and the total number is up 2.6% from a year earlier. In February, insurance added 7,300 jobs while real estate and rental and leasing rose 3,600. Securities, commodity contracts, investments and funds--which had been dropping in recent months, added 1,200 jobs after seeing a 5K-position increase in January. Wall Street banks shed thousands of jobs in fixed income last year. (julie.steinberg@wsj.com; @JBSteins)\par \par \uc2 1616 GMT Credit rating upgrades are overdue for Hungary, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , with rating firm \uc2 Moody's\uc2  set to publish an update on the country later Friday. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  currently has Hungary at Ba1, one notch below investment grade. An upgrade would be even more compelling for \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2 , which ranks Hungary a notch below \uc2 Moody's\uc2 , says economist \uc2 Pasquale Diana\uc2 . Hungary's GDP is now finally back to pre-crisis levels in real terms, its current account surplus could reach 5% of GDP this year, and its external debt--although still high--is also improving, he adds. Projects 2015 GDP growth at 2.9%, higher than the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 's 2.4% growth call. The U.S. bank is constructive on the HUF while plenty of its clients are bearish, it says. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:58 EST - Canada's is "pretty ugly" on the headlines but it's almost single-handedly caused by plunging oil prices, says Jimmy Jean, economic strategist at Desjardins Capital Markets. There are signs of the rotation in growth to non-energy exports desired by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 , including gains in exports of vehicles and aircraft, which point to a decent manufacturing sales report for January, he says. "Outside of energy, I don't find this report to be dramatically bad" and the signs of rotation in growth will comfort BoC and keep it patient on potential future rate cuts, he adds. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com, @NirmalaMenon)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:40 EST - In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , it's not just all about the euro and the dollar. Rising US bond yields have finally pushed the dollar out of its trading range against the yen, moving the greenback closer to its highest level against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  since July 23, 2007. The dollar is up 0.8% today on the back of the robust jobs report, rising to Y121.13. Japanese investors are sensitive to shifts in US bond yields, notes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Western Union\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Joe Manimbo, and the jobs report helped send the 10-year Treasury to 2.24% from 2.12% yesterday. "That's inherently yen negative. It speaks to interest-rate differentials between US and Japan which encourages the Japanese investor to leave home and seek higher yields in the US." (james.ramage@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1511 GMT Czech deflationary pressures, driven by weak fuel prices, are to prevail as the February's consumer price index is expected to contract 0.1% against the same month a year earlier and to be flat on the monthly basis, says Raiffeisanbank. This contrasts CPI increases by 0.1% on both monthly and annual terms in January. "However, in following months we expect (the CPI) to return into slight positive levels" on a likely reversal of fuel price declines, the bank says. (leos.rousek@wsj.com, @LeoRousek) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:04 EST - Tame wage inflation may allow Yellen to wait longer than June to raise rates. The jobs report showed annual wage growth was 2% in February, versus January's 2.2%. Brian Edmonds, head of interest rates at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Cantor Fitzgerald\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says subdued inflation pressure means the Fed isn't going to be "very aggressive" in hiking--especially at a time many other central banks are either cutting or launching bond-buying programs to support growth. Meanwhile, he thinks today's Treasury selloff is going to attract dip-buyers. The 10-year yield is at 2.222% versus 2.11% Thursday. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:01 EST - While gasoline prices are falling in much of the world, they've risen sharply in Brazil, fueling the country's inflation and weighing on consumer spending. Fuel prices rose 10% on-year in February amid recent tax hikes needed to help shore up Brazil's shaky finances. Public-transportation fares rose 12% over the same period. The increases are fueling social unrest, with truckers recently blocking highways nationwide to protest rising diesel prices. (marla.dickerson@wsj.com; @medWSJ)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 06, 2015 14:13 ET (19:13 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150306eb36002ws\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
