\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  to Sell $335M -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 5 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 22:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 16:14 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell U.S. dollars to ease deprecation pressures on the sol. On Thursday the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $335M. It had sold $303M on Monday. The sol has depreciated 10.5% against the greenback over the past 12 months, ending Thursday at PEN3.095 per dollar. (Robert.Kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:09 EST - Ukrainian gas behemoth Naftogaz could be split and partially privatized as early as this year, said the country's energy minister. Separating Naftogaz's oil and gas production arm from its pipeline network would bring Ukraine in line with EU competition rules. Volodymyr Demchyshyn said that the government was ready to put up minority stakes in the split-up company. Whether a deal could be closed in 2015 amid the war in the country's east is less clear. "There is no queue--unfortunately--standing outside," Mr. Demchyshyn said. (gabriele.steinhauser@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:59 EST - Kiev could cut gas supplies to rebel-held regions in eastern Ukraine if continue to miss payments, Ukraine's energy minister said, highlighting another flashpoint with Moscow. Volodymyr Demchyshyn said local distributors in separatist territories have paid for just 20% of the gas they have received since August, at a great cost to state-owned Naftogaz. "If they are not paying I'm turning off" supplies, Mr. Demchyshyn said, a week after Russian President Putins said a gas cut-off "smells of genocide." Deliveries of eastern Ukraine were part of an EU-mediated gas deal between Ukraine and Russia that expires at the end of March and the Minsk peace plan. (gabriele.steinhauser@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:58 EST - European companies could rent capacity in Ukraine's huge gas storage facilities to help fill them up to levels that could get the country through "worst case scenarios" over the next winter, Ukraine's energy minister said. Such a solution would be cheaper for everyone involved, as European companies have much lower capital costs than state-owned Naftogaz, said Volodymyr Demchyshyn. The \uc2 European Commission\uc2 , the EU executive, says Ukraine's storage needs to contain around 20 billion cubic meters of gas ahead of the winter, while Mr. Demchyshyn believes some 14-15 bcm should suffice. The issue could become a sticking point in upcoming gas talks between Ukraine, Kiev and the EU. (gabriele.steinhauser@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:49 EST - While markets may be sensitive to news from New Zealand's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ANZ says NZD/AUD remains very elevated, helped by tepid retail sales and a 10th-straight trade deficit in Australia. Thursday's NZ \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  consultation paper was seen as the first step in allowing the entity to target the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  further when setting policy. But ANZ "still sees the threshold to RBNZ action as significant, seeing support for NZD remaining sound." The kiwi is at A$0.9626; ANZ expects a short-term range of A$0.9560-0.9660. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 14:35 EST - February was another dismal month for farm equipment in Brazil, as factory volume sales of tractors to dealers and distributors fell 29% from a year ago. Harvesting combines were off 46%, based on Brazilian trade association figures. Brazil had been a key growth market for US farm equipment manufacturers Deere (DE); CNH Industrial (CNHI), the maker of Case IH brand equipment; and Agco, the maker of Massey Ferguson machinery. But lower prices for Brazilian soybeans and sugar and less attractive terms on government-subsidized equipment loans are discouraging farmers from buying machinery. Equipment "spending is likely to be curtailed in the near term," says \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  in a note. Farm machinery stocks are mixed with DE up a touch, while Agco and CNHI are off 0.4% and 0.9% respectively. (robert.tita@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:29 EST - The Mexico peso continues to surprise on the downside against the dollar, and Monex says it could break new levels of resistance in coming days. It's fallen some 19% since May to reach MXN15.2020. "The breaking of its principal resistance (MXN15.10) and the recent rise in the dollar index open the door for the exchange rate to put to test the next levels of resistance, first in the range of MXN15.28-33," says Monex. The brokerage recommends buying dollars in the medium-to-long term with an objective of MXN15.28 and a stop at MXN15.05. (laurence.iliff@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:26 EST - The pace of labor market gains should remain steady in February despite severe snowstorms in parts of the country, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  economist Joe LaVorgna. "February nonfarm payrolls may have dodged the worst of the recent weather disruptions because during the February employment survey week [the week through the 14th], jobless claims plunged." They were the lowest for the month then, while in the survey week for February 2014, jobless claims peaked (they haven't reached the same level since). Tax-receipt data also suggest employers are adding more jobs. The initial read for February 2014 was job growth of 175K; consensus for tomorrow is 240K. (jeffrey.sparshott@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:12 EST - In what so far has been a repeat of last year's trend, economists are lowering economic-growth forecasts every couple weeks. It happened again at the end of February as the average 2015 GDP growth view of 35 economists is now 3.1%, versus 3.3% in January's \uc2 Bank of Mexico\uc2  survey. Expectations for next year also fall two-tenths, to 3.6%. The dismal scientists are also darken their views on the peso, predicting a year-end level of MXN14.54/dollar versus MXN14.18 previously. Last year, the GDP-growth consensus among economists started around 3.5% and slipped to around 2% year-end; growth ended up being 2.1%. (laurence.iliff@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:05 EST - The bond market is in a tug of war today as conflicting factors spark volatile trading. Yields are ticking lower again, dragged down by falling rates in Europe amid Draghi's comments. But Treasurys are lagging European sovereigns as investors await Friday's jobs report. The yield on the 10-year Treasury is at 2.110% versus 2.121% Wednesday while the 10-year bund fell to 0.354%, near a record low. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:54 EST - The pace of potential job growth at small businesses declined in February as winter storms swept across the East Coast, according to the \uc2 National Federation of Independent Business\uc2 . A net 12% of 716 small businesses surveyed said they were planning to hire new workers, down 2 points from January, with gains more than offset by an uptick in the number of owners planning job cuts. Despite the declines, small-business hiring remains strong, according to William Dunkelberg, the Washington lobby group's chief economist. He anticipates "a firm number" in tomorrow's government report, though shy of the 300K average seen in recent months. (angus.loten@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:40 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  such as the dollar and the euro continue to move in accordance with \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  news more so than economic fundamentals, says USForex's Lennon Sweeting. The euro has largely shrugged off recent upbeat data to trade lower leading into, during and following Draghi's press conference today. Meanwhile, "we've been seeing dollar strength while US fundamentals have been so-so." Investors are keying into the mid-month FOMC meeting, he notes. Still, tomorrow's jobs report should provide direction for the dollar--if only in relation to what the numbers mean for market expectations on the Fed and interest rates. The euro is down 0.5% at $1.1018 while the dollar rises 0.4% to Y120.20. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 05, 2015 16:16 ET (21:16 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150305eb35003wm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
