\par\par \b \uc2 Treasurys Tick Up After \uc2 ADP\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,273 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 14:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:24 EST - Treasury prices perk up after the in-line \uc2 ADP\uc2  employment report, which admittedly hasn't been a stable predictor of the government's data. That print Friday will be the key release ahead of the FOMC meeting on March 17-8, the focus of which being whether patience is removed. The yield on the 10-year Treasury is 2.108%, versus 2.122% Tuesday. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1321 GMT Poland is likely to keep interest rates unchanged in April after a biggger-than-expected cut in March, Capital Economics writes in a note. The rate setters cut the benchmark interest rate to 1.5% from 2%. The market expected a 25bps rate cut. Capital Economics expects the Polish interest rates to "stay extremely low over the next couple of years." (patryk.wasilewski@dowjones.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1313 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--A cut by the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Polish \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was likely, says Piotr Matys, emerging market strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London, but he didn't expected as much as 50 basis points. "This is a very bold move even though we did know that there was a lot of pressure on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to respond," he says. Overall, however, he says that it is unlikely to change the picture for the zloty. "I still think that the zloty will continue to appreciate. Today's decision may temporarily slow down the appreciation - but only temporarily." (josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1310 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--"We think that the robust move today will basically finish the easing cycle," says Michal Dybula at the French bank. "But post-meeting comments (at 4pm local time) with respect to the economic and policy outlook will be important to digest." EUR/PLN now trades at 4.1750, only a shade above its pre-decision levels. (Katie.martin@wsj.com/ @katie_martin_\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 fx\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1310 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ] After cutting its main rate by a larger-than-expected 50 basis points, to 1.5%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Poland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will likely signal an end to the current easing cycle when it holds its press conference at 1500 GMT Wednesday, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank Pocztowy\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The cut was likely prompted by an update to the bank's economic projections, presented to rate setters, which likely showed deeper deflation ahead compared to the projections from November, the bank says. Poland's CPI was -1.3% in annual terms in January. (martin.sobczyk@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1259 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Sweden's National Debt Office\f28 \uc2 \f2  will offer SEK4 billion worth of bonds at its auctions of nominal government bonds starting from March 11, lifting the amount from SEK3.5 billion so far. The higher offer volume follows the NDO's recent upward revision of its government bond issuance targets for 2015 and 2016 due to slowing tax income and interest payment increases. The NDO is scheduled to announce at 1520 GMT which bond or bonds it will offer at the next auction on March 11. Sweden will continue to offer SEK1 billion at inflation-linked auctions. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1257 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Brazil's hydroelectric reservoirs remain at historic low levels, having missed out on heavy February rains. "The situation is so critical that, even assuming the level of rainfall improves to historical average before the dry period starts in May, and the sharp increase in electricity prices reduces demand by 20%, the government would still be forced to implement an energy rationing program, thereby deepening the recession, " says Luciano Rostagno, chief strategist at Banco Mizuho do Brasil. (rogerio.jelmayer@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:54 EST - Banco de Credito sees more volatility ahead for the Peruvian sol, saying it could take place in the gap between \f28 \uc2 \f2 Peru's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  meeting on March 12 and the following week's FOMC gathering. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Peru\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been actively \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market by selling dollars (including $303M Monday) to ease volatility. Banco de Credito says the activity and an improved price for copper have eased depreciation pressures on the sol. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:51 EST - Treasury prices are little changed after two days of selling as today's US data loom, starting in minutes with \f28 \uc2 \f2 ADP\f28 \uc2 \f2 's employment report. Still, the key one is Friday's nonfarm-jobs print, which should further shape expectations on the Fed's rate outlook. This week, Treasurys have been hit by a flurry of new corporate-bond sales. More are scheduled by week's end. The yield on the 10-year Treasury is at 2.12%, versus 2.122% Tuesday. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1248 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Spain's fourth-quarter 2014 gross domestic product data confirms its "new role" as the eurozone's work horse, and it's in particular positive that the quarterly growth pace accelerated quarter to quarter last year, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 DZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Spanish economic growth should maintain its dynamic, fuelled by positive sentiment of corporates and consumers, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 DZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , adding that a reduction in income taxes should benefit households. But it's not all good news, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 DZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 , noting that the real level of Spain's GDP in 4Q 2014 was more than 5% below the level of the pre-crisis 1Q 2008, while unemployment is still very high. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) work \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1245 GMT Fears that Saudi Arabia might be forced to devalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and un-peg it from the U.S. dollar because of falling oil prices are unfounded, Capital Economics says in a note. The spot-market value of the Saudi riyal fell recently to its lowest point against the dollar in six years, leading to speculation about the oil exporter's response. Devaluation can't be completely ruled out, the note says, but history suggests Saudi Arabia's leadership will make fiscal adjustments rather than changing its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comes under further pressure.(asa.fitch@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @wsjmideast) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1242 GMT Spain's CIS, a government-run institute, released Wednesday an early peek at its February survey results, with a mixed bag of good and bad for conservative Prime Minister Mariano Rajoy. The percentage of Spaniards who think the economy is bad or very bad is down to 76.5% from 86.9% a year ago. Those who think the economy will improve or stay the same in a year rises to 70.7%, up from 61.5%. Asked about political orientation, 10.4% say they are hard left, up from 8.4%; after Mr. Rajoy won the 2011 election overwhelmingly, many of his voters seem to have forgotten they supported him: 26.5% say they voted for center-left PSOE, only 25.5% say they voted for Mr. Rajoy.(david.roman@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 04, 2015 08:24 ET (13:24 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150304eb34001vb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
