\par\par \b \uc2 More Volatility Seen for Peru's  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,412 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2015\par 13:54\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 7:54 EST - Banco de Credito sees more volatility ahead for the Peruvian sol, saying it could take place in the gap between \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting on March 12 and the following week's FOMC gathering. \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market by selling dollars (including $303M Monday) to ease volatility. Banco de Credito says the activity and an improved price for copper have eased depreciation pressures on the sol. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:51 EST - Treasury prices are little changed after two days of selling as today's US data loom, starting in minutes with \uc2 ADP\uc2 's employment report. Still, the key one is Friday's nonfarm-jobs print, which should further shape expectations on the Fed's rate outlook. This week, Treasurys have been hit by a flurry of new corporate-bond sales. More are scheduled by week's end. The yield on the 10-year Treasury is at 2.12%, versus 2.122% Tuesday. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1248 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--Spain's fourth-quarter 2014 gross domestic product data confirms its "new role" as the eurozone's work horse, and it's in particular positive that the quarterly growth pace accelerated quarter to quarter last year, says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2 . Spanish economic growth should maintain its dynamic, fuelled by positive sentiment of corporates and consumers, says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2 , adding that a reduction in income taxes should benefit households. But it's not all good news, says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2 , noting that the real level of Spain's GDP in 4Q 2014 was more than 5% below the level of the pre-crisis 1Q 2008, while unemployment is still very high. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) work \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1245 GMT Fears that Saudi Arabia might be forced to devalue its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and un-peg it from the U.S. dollar because of falling oil prices are unfounded, Capital Economics says in a note. The spot-market value of the Saudi riyal fell recently to its lowest point against the dollar in six years, leading to speculation about the oil exporter's response. Devaluation can't be completely ruled out, the note says, but history suggests Saudi Arabia's leadership will make fiscal adjustments rather than changing its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  regime if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  comes under further pressure.(asa.fitch@wsj.com; \f28 \uc2 \f2 Twitter\f28 \uc2 \f2 : @wsjmideast) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1242 GMT Spain's CIS, a government-run institute, released Wednesday an early peek at its February survey results, with a mixed bag of good and bad for conservative Prime Minister Mariano Rajoy. The percentage of Spaniards who think the economy is bad or very bad is down to 76.5% from 86.9% a year ago. Those who think the economy will improve or stay the same in a year rises to 70.7%, up from 61.5%. Asked about political orientation, 10.4% say they are hard left, up from 8.4%; after Mr. Rajoy won the 2011 election overwhelmingly, many of his voters seem to have forgotten they supported him: 26.5% say they voted for center-left PSOE, only 25.5% say they voted for Mr. Rajoy.(david.roman@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:30 EST - With \f28 \uc2 \f2 ADP\f28 \uc2 \f2 , service-sector PMI and Beige Book on tap today, there's plenty for investors to chew on as they consider the macro view and how things continue to stack up on the US rate-hike front. But with the big enchilada not until Friday and the ECB and BoE meeting tomorrow, there's plenty of news yet to come this week. US stock-index futures are lower, extending yesterday's selloff (which as modest as it was the largest in weeks), with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 S&P 500\f28 \uc2 \f2  down 6.5. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nymex\f28 \uc2 \f2  oil is modestly higher, gold is flat and the euro is lower, sitting nearly $1.1135. Treasurys are quiet, with the 10-year yield at 2.13%. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 7:27 EST - Europe seems well on the road to recovery. Service sectors in most major economies are posting solid growth and retail sales are booming, thanks not least to long-dormant German consumers. That Germany is a driver shouldn't be a surprise: Inflation is low, unemployment is at multidecade nadir, wage settlements are rising, finance is fantastically cheap. Could this be the beginning of a German boom? Global investors' concerns now have to some extent shifted to Asia, where we got improvement in China's services PMI and slightly higher-than-expected Australian GDP--both welcome but not quite enough to set aside nagging worries of a protracted regional slowdown. There, the cycle of regional central banks easing monetary conditions is set to continue as India surprisingly joined the fray. (alen.mattich@wsj.com and michael.casey@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1227 GMT The value of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves is likely to have edged up in February due largely to the gains in the euro and the dollar, according to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Commerzbank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . "There has been no sign or evidence of any significant SNB \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market recently, so any changes to the reserves are likely to come from valuation gains or losses," says analyst \f28 \uc2 \f2 Ulrich Leuchtmann\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Last month the euro gained around 3% versus the franc, while the dollar increased roughly 3.7%. The SNB reserves data are due Friday at 0800 GMT. (neil.maclucas@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1226 GMT "The new Croatia [euro bond] perfectly sums up the market," says Timothy Ash, head of emerging market research, ex-Africa, for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. He says the 10-year debt is due to pay a yield of around 3.3%. For a sub-investment grade country with "no growth", he says, this "looks mad". Nonetheless, he adds: "Even a sceptic like me finds myself saying it's cheap".(josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1212 GMT Worries about a Greek exit from the eurozone subsided at the end of February but they haven't disappeared as Greece needs to impose meaningful reforms, says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Peter Wilson\f28 \uc2 \f2 , managing director at First International Advisors, a subsidiary of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2 . There is still pressure on the new Greek government as the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  won't renew an exception regarding the acceptance of Greek government debt as collateral at the ECB's open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . This is even as Greek banks will be able to get funding from the Greek \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  via the Emergency Lending Assistance, albeit at a higher cost than at the ECB, Mr. Wilson says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1211 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--The Republic of Croatia is preparing to issue 10-year euro-denominated bonds later Wednesday, investor sources say, adding that demand for the paper had surpassed EUR5BN. Croatia is rated Ba1 by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Moody's\f28 \uc2 \f2 , BB by \f28 \uc2 \f2 Standard & Poor's\f28 \uc2 \f2  and BB by Fitch, all sub-investment grade. Banks leading the deal are \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Erste Bank, \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the sources say. (josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1142 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Fitch Ratings\f28 \uc2 \f2  affirms its rating of AA+ for Heta Asset Resolution's EUR1 billion government-guaranteed tier 2 subordinated notes maturing in 2022, the same rating as the Republic of Austria. On Sunday, Austria's bank supervisor announced a moratorium on payments for almost all of Heta's debt instruments until May 2016. The Austrian Finance Minister has said, however, that the Republic of Austria would be servicing those bonds with a federal guarantee. Fitch doesn't rate Heta or any other debt instruments issued by Heta or its predecessor \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hypo Alpe-Adria-Bank International AG\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (nicole.lundeen@wsj.com, @nicole_lundeen) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 March 04, 2015 07:54 ET (12:54 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150304eb34001m8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex1