\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Sells $90M in  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,363 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2015\par 20:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:16 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today, selling $90M in an effort to ease depreciation pressures on the sol. It ended at PEN3.082/dollar and has fallen 9.4% the past year. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  calls the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  oversold: "We expect \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support of the sol to continue and possibly increase in case depreciation pressures persist." (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:16 EST - Supporters of tighter gun controls are testing a new approach in their battle to cut firearm deaths: adding gun curbs for people linked to domestic violence. In all this year, lawmakers in at least 12 states have proposed legislation that would make it harder for people accused or convicted of domestic-violence charges to get or keep a firearm. The push comes after six states passed new gun laws in 2014 related to domestic violence, including two--Louisiana and Wisconsin--with Republican governors. The bills have been pushed often over the objection of gun-rights groups like the \uc2 National Rifle Association\uc2  who say they are unnecessary and, in some instances, violate gun-owners' rights. (ashby.jones@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:02 EST - It's been a strong February for \uc2 GM\uc2  shares. Kicking off the month at $32.67, 33 cents below the 2010 IPO price, a combination of strong earnings, a dividend raise and shareholder activism has taken shares to above $37.50 by mid-month. The key question facing shareholders now is who to hitch their wagon to: Mary or Harry? CEO \uc2 Mary Barra\uc2 's management team last week promised a 20% dividend hike and said \uc2 GM\uc2  will consider more shareholder-friendly actions later in the year. Shareholders responded, and \uc2 GM\uc2 's stock ended that week up 10% vs the Monday Feb. 2 open. A few days later, Harry Wilson, a former hedge fund manager representing him four investment firms, asked for a board seat and $8B in share buybacks by 2016. In mid-afternoon trading Friday, \uc2 GM\uc2  is up 5% vs the week's opening trade. (john.stoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:45 EST - Virtual reality isn't just for those who can afford an expensive Oculus Rift headset. \uc2 Google\uc2  (GOOGL) and \uc2 Mattel\uc2  (MAT)are partnering to update MAT's classic View-Master goggles, giving them a VR flavor. The new View-Master uses GOOGL's "cardboard" technology to create a sort of low-fi VR experience. "Cardboard" was a trinket GOOGL gave out at its developer conference last June--literally a folded piece of cardboard with a special lens designed to turn a smartphone into a cheap VR toy. MAT, which could badly use a new hit toy, is flat today. (rolfe.winkler@wsj.com; @rolfewinkler)\par \par \uc2 13:41 EST - Abbott (ABT) has $5B in the bank and could get $6B more from selling its stake in a Mylan (MYL) JV, estimates Leerink says, giving the medical-products company "significant financial flexibility" to pursue M&A targets. The investment bank notes discussions with ABT executives suggest ABT would eye acquisitions in the $5-7B range to bolster either its established products and diabetes or its medical-device lines. Leerink sees 1 or 2 such deals being likely the next 12-24 months. "ABT's appetite for M&A is greater than it's been in recent years--now a high priority for the company." (ron.winslow@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:31 EST - Want to learn to play guitar via video chat on \uc2 Google\uc2  (GOOGL)? You've got 2 more months. Helpouts--a service the company launched to help tutors, stylists and music teachers sell their advice via video chat--will shut down April 20. It never caught on, and the GOOGL executive who launched the product in November 2013 left the team soon thereafter. GOOGL has had many failed product experiments over the years, including most recently its web-connected eyewear, \uc2 Google\uc2  Glass. (rolfe.winkler@wsj.com; @rolfewinkler)\par \par \uc2 13:28 EST - The latest US rig-count data barely caused a ripple in the futures market as traders become increasingly wary of the existing supply glut. That's unlike the reaction some recent Fridays, when big weekly declines stoked price rallies into the close. Supply data this week have shown the market remains awash in crude, lessening the importance of the rig count number in some traders' minds, says Mizuho's Robert Yawger. "Ultimately, you will see a drift to the upside, but it's not something that's going to happen in the next week or so." (ira.iosebashvili@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:19 EST - The bankruptcy judge who oversaw Detroit's historic Chapter 9 restructuring says it's not just Puerto Rico's fault the island is struggling under a $73B debt load. "For every dollar a debtor shouldn't borrow, there's a creditor who shouldn't lend it," Judge Steven Rhodes says in Las Vegas at a conference of restructuring professionals. "The creditors claim the high ground here, but really?" A federal judge in San Juan recently disrupted Puerto Rico's plans to fix its finances by blocking a law which would have enabled public agencies to restructure some debt. (sara.randazzo@wsj.com; @sara_randazzo)\par \par \uc2 13:16 EST - The number of oil rigs operating in the US tumbled again this past week, falling 84 to 1,056; the year-earlier level was 1,423. Oil prices have staged an uneven rebound in recent weeks, helped in part by the data series, provided by \uc2 Baker Hughes (BHI) \uc2 and which seems to suggest lower oil prices are leading companies to drill less--which could eventually help end the supply glut. US natural-gas rigs fell 14 to 300 the past week; the year-ago count was 337. (dan.molinski@wsj.com; @dmwsj)\par \par \uc2 13:10 EST - There's seemingly no end in sight to the malaise for big food. Smucker (SJM), fresh off well-received deal last week to buy a pet-food company, said this morning that weakness in its coffee business likely will continue. But that's proven not to be enough to keep shares from rising to a fresh record high as SJM's F3Q EPS didn't fall as much as analysts anticipated. The news caps a week in which 6 of the 8 biggest US packaged-food companies by market cap reported weak results or warned about forthcoming results. While SJM rises 1%, Campbell (\uc2 CPB\uc2 ), \uc2 ConAgra\uc2  (CAG), Kellogg (K), Kraft (KRFT) and Mondelez (MDLZ) are all down at least that much. (jason.dean@wsj.com; @jasonrdean)\par \par \uc2 13:09 EST - Midway through 1Q, analysts have cut EPS views for the period on \uc2 S&P 500\uc2  companies at the fastest rate since 2Q09. The total EPS for the index members is now seen being 7.4% less than Jan. 1, says FactSet. That compares to the past year's average 2.3% decline and 1.6% during the past 5 years. While 1Q energy EPS has been halved by the financial-data firm's count, analysts have also been cutting expectations as it's become clearer how much of an impact the stronger dollar is having on corporate results. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 13:07 EST - In an eye-catching optics advance, Swiss researchers unveil experimental glasses and contact lens combination that allow the wearer to shift between normal and magnified vision with a wink. Working with US defense funding, researchers at the \uc2 Ecole Polytechnique Federale de Lausanne\uc2  in Switzerland developed a telescopic contact lens that can magnify 2.8 times, and then made electronic frames that can recognize winks and ignore blinks, allowing wearers of the contact lenses to switch between normal and magnified vision. The researchers demonstrated their prototype Friday at a meeting of the American Association for The Advancement of Science in San Jose, CA. Since the human eye needs a steady supply of oxygen, the scientific team is still fine-tuning the lenses to make them more breathable. (lee.hotz@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 13, 2015 14:16 ET (19:16 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150213eb2d0032a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
