\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Sells $90M in  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,274 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Februar 2015\par 20:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:16 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today, selling $90M in an effort to ease depreciation pressures on the sol. It ended at PEN3.082/dollar and has fallen 9.4% the past year. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  calls the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  oversold: "We expect \b \highlight22\uc2 central-bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support of the sol to continue and possibly increase in case depreciation pressures persist." (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:22 EST - Puerto Rico's debt downgrade weighs on the University of Puerto Rico, with \uc2 S&P\uc2  knocking the school's long-term rating down 3 steps to B from BB. It derives a big chunk of its funding from the commonwealth, and that's been a serious credit risk for the school as the island's finances remain distressed. \uc2 S&P\uc2  threatened to yank UPR's rating late last year because it hadn't been given updated financial statements; the firm has since gotten some and the university is expected to post its latest-year results in the next month. (melissa.korn@wsj.com; @melissakorn)\par \par \uc2 13:19 EST - The bankruptcy judge who oversaw Detroit's historic Chapter 9 restructuring says it's not just Puerto Rico's fault the island is struggling under a $73B debt load. "For every dollar a debtor shouldn't borrow, there's a creditor who shouldn't lend it," Judge Steven Rhodes says in Las Vegas at a conference of restructuring professionals. "The creditors claim the high ground here, but really?" A federal judge in San Juan recently disrupted Puerto Rico's plans to fix its finances by blocking a law which would have enabled public agencies to restructure some debt. (sara.randazzo@wsj.com; @sara_randazzo)\par \par \uc2 12:46 EST - \uc2 Canada's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to regard the 2006-7 pre-recession years as the norm by which to judge today's economy. But \uc2 BMO\uc2  calls then an exceptional period when the commodity and US housing booms greatly benefited Canada. Now that the commodity supercycle is over and US housing may never return to those heady days, \uc2 BMO\uc2  argues that by many measures, the Canadian economy can't get much better than it's been recently. In a growth-restrained world, maybe current levels are the new reality. As \uc2 BMO\uc2  paraphrases a comment about the Fed, "perhaps the \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  is trying to whip a 2% mule into a 3% racehorse." (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 12:27 EST - Concerns about a weak local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  raise the possibility that \uc2 Mexico's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will boost rates before the Fed, some analysts argue. The dollar was recently at MXN14.8820, down from Thursday's close of MXN14.9230 but well above the sub-MXN13 level seen last summer. Earlier this week, the peso hit a 6-year low of MXN15.1560. Nomura now assigns a high probability of a preemptive hike, "perhaps above 50%." The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  voted unanimously 2 weeks ago to hold rates steady. (amy.guthrie@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:54 EST - Cumberland Advisors' \uc2 David Kotok\uc2  argues "audit the Fed" supporters are operating out of pure ignorance and not being clear what their real motivations are. "Those who chant 'audit the Fed' represent the extremes of poor governance in Washington. They are fearful about debating policy because they are not sure of themselves. They would rather attack the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  with political rhetoric because it is an easy target for them." (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 11:49 EST - US technology companies have started the year by jumping into the equity markets at the highest rate since 2004, selling $4.6B of stock. Eleven years ago, volume this deep in the calendar was $6.1B. This year's biggest deal is last week's $1.5B convertible-bond deal from Microchip (MCHP). (vipal.monga@wsj.com; @vipalmonga)\par \par \uc2 11:45 EST - The tug of war in the Treasury market continues. As foreign buyers see US bonds as an attractive bargain, US-based investors shrug. "US investors have paid too high a price for the safety in the Treasury-bond market," contends \uc2 Donald Ellenberger\uc2 , money manager at \uc2 Federated Investors\uc2 . He's underweighting Treasurys and overweighting corporate bonds--both investment- and junk-grade. Ellenberger has bought the latter in recent weeks after shedding holdings late last year, seeing January's selloff in junk as a buying opportunity. It's had a total return of 1.14% this month through Thursday while Treasurys have lost 1.17%, according to \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1614 GMT [\uc2 Dow Jones\uc2 ]--An expected slowdown this year in economic growth, combined with deeper-than-expected price falls in early 2015, and relatively high debt levels are making Hungary vulnerable to a deflation threat, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . The outlook for deflation may support interest-rate easing from March onward, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2  economist Agata Urbanska-Giner. Expects GDP growth to slow this year to 2.4% from last year's stronger-than-expected 3.5% rise as numerous one-off measures that supported the economy in 2014 will gradually fade away. (margit.feher@wsj.com, @margitfeher) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:53 EST - Stronger-than-expected Canadian manufacturing-sales data appear to have quietened chatter of another two, rather than one, rate cut from the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Canada\f28 \uc2 \f2 , says Jimmy Jean at Desjardins. That's helped the loonie recover from the weak patch hit overnight trading despite higher oil prices. "A 2nd rate cut is well priced in (and) the possibility of a 3rd rate cut is what's being speculated on right now," Jean tells WSJ. "I guess the big surprise on the positive side in the manufacturing report quelled the expectations for a 3rd cut." The dollar is at C$1.2433 versus yesterday's close of C$1.2509. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10:50 EST - The dollar ticked down after the \f28 \uc2 \f2 University of Michigan\f28 \uc2 \f2  consumer-sentiment index, suggesting a turn in the outlook for consumer spending has started to align with shifting views on US growth, which recently have become more pessimistic. "The index number adds a little bit to the interest-rate expectations you've seen in the past couple of days," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of New York Mellon\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Simon Derrick, "moderating slightly where people expect rates to be by year-end." The dollar has eased to Y118.75 from Y119.01 ahead of the number to be down 0.3% for the day. The euro edged up to $1.1411 from $1.1406 and is up a touch for Friday. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1546 GMT [\f28 \uc2 \f2 Dow Jones\f28 \uc2 \f2 ]--Hungary is likely to enter the global "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  war," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rabobank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Given Hungary's economy is sensitive to external demand, "it is fair to assume that the monetary policy council might narrow the interest rate differential [versus other central banks' policy rates] to prevent substantial HUF appreciation," Rabo says. The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hungarian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's bias is "firmly skewed" in favor of further cutting rates, it adds. Hungary's economic expansion could still match last year's surprisingly high 3.5% "and perhaps even exceed it with domestic demand underpinned by the record low interest rates at 2.10%," Rabo says. Meanwhile, most economists expect a slowdown in Hungary's GDP growth in 2015. (margit.feher@wsj.com @margitfeher) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 13, 2015 14:16 ET (19:16 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150213eb2d00374\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
