\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2 , Sell Dollars -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,211 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2015\par 20:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2015, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:59 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was back in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today, though in a much-smaller way. It sold $5M after a $115M \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday in efforts to boost the sol, down 7.9% versus the greenback the past year. Despite today's increase in the supply of dollars, it edged up versus the sol to PEN3.045. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  president Julio Velarde said at a conference "I have the impression that there has been an overreaction in terms of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate." (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:55 EST - While Yellen's visit to Capitol Hill to speak with \uc2 Senate\uc2  Democrats has garnered plenty of attention today, she wasn't the only Fed official on the Hill today. Coincidentally, \uc2 St. Louis Fed\uc2  chief Bullard was spotted roaming the halls around the same time. He said he was meeting with members from his district and didn't coordinate his visit with Yellen's. The FOMC concluded a 2-day meeting in Washington yesterday. (ben.leubsdorf@wsj.com; @BenLeubsdorf)\par \par \uc2 14:43 EST - \uc2 Peru \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Julio Velarde says the body still has room to ease despite a series of cuts to both commercial-bank reserve requirements and the reference interest rate. "I believe there is a margin (to ease), but not that large either," he notes at a conference. The policy rate was cut a quarter-point in January to 3.25%; the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  began its current cycle of easing reserve requirements in June 2013. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:41 EST - Another landmark for a low yield world--the yield on the 10-year \uc2 U.K. government\uc2  debt closed at 1.419%, the lowest closing level on record, according to Tradeweb. The yield earlier Thursday touched 1.397%, the lowest intraday day low ever. The move suggests how uncertain global growth, deflation concerns and bold monetary stimulus from the ECB have continued to send bond yields tumbling. Just a day earlier, U.S. 10-yield closed at a fresh 20-month low and 30-year bond yield settled at record low. Both US and UK offer more attractive yields than Germany--the 10-year bund's yield settled little changed at 0.359%. The 10-year US bond yield was 1.763%. (min.zeng@wsj.com;@minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 14:40 EST - Yellen's message to \uc2 Senate\uc2  Democrats on the US economy was "very positive," says Minority Whip Richard Durbin after a luncheon with the Fed chairwoman. "She went through the issues of unemployment and inflation. Very positive. And economic-growth numbers were good, have been good." One other bright spot she pointed to was fiscal policy. After restraining growth earlier in the recovery, Yellen said it's now "in a more-neutral position rather than negative," says Durbin. But he notes she also pointed to risks. "We talked about the situation in Europe, one of those risks." (ben.leubsdorf@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:28 EST - The Canadian dollar is having a very bad start to 2015, falling 6.6% after dropping 9.8% last year as oil keeps falling. The \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2 's response to oil's implosion--last week's rate cut--added fuel to the fire. It's hard to find anybody who has a good word to say about the loonie; some analysts have penciled in even-lower targets today. Unanimity like that is often seen as an indicator an asset is oversold and maybe due for a rebound. But there are two factors arguing against that: the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market tends to push trends farther than other markets would and it's a cross-asset trade, with oil's tumble the singular, dominant driver of the CAD's decline. Until oil steadies, the loonie won't likely either. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 14:27 EST - Sen. Tim Kaine, (D-VA) said Fed Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2  drew a distinction between the U.S. economy and the rest of the world in her discussion at a luncheon with \uc2 Senate\uc2  Democrats Thursday. "She was generally pretty positive about fundamentals here, you know some concern about the foreign situation. Everyone affects everybody else these days," Mr. Kaine said. (michael.crittenden@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:26 EST - As the Canadian dollar continues slide amid oil's weakness (though the commodity has rebounded into the close), the greenback hitting C$1.30 may come into play sooner than many expected. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  company Knightsbridge had anticipated it happening within 3 months, but it could "easily" get there if oil falls to $40/barrel, says President Rahim Madhavji. He adds C$1.40 is possible if crude slumps to $30. Beyond oil and the potential of another rate cut, there's also policy divergence with the Fed. As \uc2 Nymex\uc2  oil futures earlier breached $44 for first time since 2009, the greenback hit a nearly 6-year high of C$1.2679. "It's a crazy, crazy day today," says Madhavji (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 14:19 EST - Sen. Joe Manchin, (D-W.V.), said Fed Chairwoman \uc2 Janet Yellen\uc2 's message at a luncheon with \uc2 Senate\uc2  Democrats was straightforward: "Things are going well" for the economy, unemployment is low, but some people are still hurting. "She feels the economy is strong, a lot is good," Mr. Manchin said leaving a lunch with fellow \uc2 Senate\uc2  Democrats Thursday. (michael.crittenden@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:18 EST - Peru Finance Minister Alonso Segura says at a conference he expects economic growth of 4.8% this year, bringing growth in the economy of 4.8% this year, bringing its view into line with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's. Segura notes domestic demand remains strong and the government is boosting spending. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Julio Velarde said at the same conference that growth this year will be in good part tied to a recovery in commodities after a dismal 2014 for things like fish meal and copper. GDP growth last year was likely around 2.4%. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:56 EST - Kicking the can down the road doesn't seem to work for many distressed companies. The percentage of repeat defaulters since the Great Recession is 39%, more than double the historical average going back to 1987. That suggests lax credit markets allowed troubled companies to paper over problems without fixing them, according to a new \uc2 Moody's\uc2  report. Many companies which either sought Chapter 11 protection or missed bond payments amid the crisis used the wide-open credit markets to borrow more money or restructure debt. "They were pushing off the inevitable," says \uc2 Moody's\uc2 David Keisman\uc2 . (vipal.monga@wsj.com; @vipalmonga)\par \par \uc2 13:50 EST - Risk on is back with volatile trading in risky markets. US stocks shake off earlier weakness and now rallying as crude oil rebounds--which drives more selling from safe-haven bonds. The action comes ahead of the initial read on US 4Q GDP tomorrow. The 10-year Treasury yield is now at 1.778%, compared with 1.723% late Wednesday, while the \uc2 Dow\uc2  industrials is up 140. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 29, 2015 14:59 ET (19:59 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020150129eb1t003rb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw442\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal6630\pichgoal60