ional operating charges of GBP171 million (2013 - GBP20 million) in the six months ended 31 December 2014 comprise:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- GBP18 million (2013 - GBPnil) in respect of the Global efficiency programme announced in January 2014;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- GBP11 million (2013 - GBP3 million) in respect of the Supply excellence restructuring programme;\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- a gain of GBP3 million (2013 - charge of GBP17 million) arising from a property sale in respect of the restructuring of the group's supply \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ; and\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -- GBP145 million (2013 - GBPnil) representing an estimate of the income statement charge for the settlement cost of several related disputes with the Korean customs authorities regarding the transfer pricing methodology applicable to imported spirits. Payments to be made pursuant to settlement of these related disputes will be reduced by GBP88 million which was paid to the customs authorities prior to 30 June 2014, and was previously accounted for as a receivable from Korea customs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the second half of the year an exceptional operating charge of approximately GBP80 million is expected to be incurred primarily in respect of the Global efficiency and Supply excellence programmes.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Non-operating items in the six months ended 31 December 2014 include a gain of GBP103 million (2013 - gain of GBP140 million) following the acquisition of additional equity shares in USL which increased the group's investment in USL from 25.02% to 54.78% excluding the 2.38% interest owned by the USL Benefit Trust (2013 - 10.04% to 25.02%). As a result of USL becoming a subsidiary of the group a gain is recognised on the difference between the book value of the 25.02% investment and the market value on the acquisition of the controlling stake on 2 July 2014. The gain includes a GBP79 million cumulative exchange loss recycled from other comprehensive income and GBP10 million of transaction costs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Exceptional non-operating items comprise transaction costs of GBP5 million (2013 - GBPnil) in respect of the expected disposal of Bushmills.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the six months ended 31 December 2013 represent a charge after taxation of GBP92 million in respect of the settlement of the thalidomide litigation in Australia and New Zealand and anticipated future payments to thalidomide organisations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cash payments in the six months ended 31 December 2014 in respect of exceptional restructuring items and thalidomide were GBP62 million (2013 - GBP41 million) and GBP8 million (2013 - GBP2 million), respectively. Cash expenditure on exceptional restructuring items in the second half of the year is expected to be approximately GBP120 million.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (d) Dividend\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 An interim dividend of 21.5 pence per share will be paid to holders of ordinary shares and ADRs on the register as of 27 February 2015. This represents an increase of 9% on last year's interim dividend. The interim dividend will be paid to shareholders on 7 April 2015. Payment to US ADR holders will be made on 10 April 2015. A dividend reinvestment plan is available to holders of ordinary shares in respect of the interim dividend and the plan notice date is 12 March 2015.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's dividend philosophy has been a consistent increase in the annual dividend payable while maintaining dividend cover between 1.8 and 2.2 times earnings per share. In recent years this has allowed the group to increase its dividend by 9% a year. Given the economic slowdown in emerging markets and the associated \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness expected in the future both of which had such a significant impact on \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's reported results in the year ended 30 June 2014, the group's dividend coverage ratio is now below the lower end of the range set. However cash flow is improving as these results show and the strength of the dollar is providing some offset to emerging market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakness. Therefore the interim dividend growth was maintained at 9%, but this will be reviewed for the full year and if necessary the rate of increase may be changed for the final dividend in order to maintain the coverage ratio, albeit at the lower end of the range.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  MOVEMENT IN NET BORROWINGS AND EQUITY \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Movement in net borrowings\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                               2014         2013 \par                                                        GBP million  GBP million \par -----------------------------------------------------  -----------  ----------- \par Net borrowings at 30 June                                  (8,850)      (8,403) \par -----------------------------------------------------  -----------  ----------- \par Free cash flow (a)                                             699          326 \par -----------------------------------------------------  -----------  ----------- \par Acquisition less sale of businesses (b)                      (664)        (420) \par -----------------------------------------------------  -----------  ----------- \par Net purchase of own shares for share schemes (c)               (7)         (61) \par -----------------------------------------------------  -----------  ----------- \par Dividends paid to non-controlling interests                   (34)         (61) \par -----------------------------------------------------  -----------  ----------- \par Purchase of shares of non-controlling interests (d)              -         (35) \par ---------------------------------