nancial Reporting Standards (IFRSs) as issued by the IASB and as adopted by the EU. As required by the Disclosure and Transparency Rules of the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Financial Conduct Authority\f28 \uc2 \f2 , the condensed set of financial statements has been prepared applying the accounting policies and presentation that were applied in the preparation of the company's published consolidated financial statements for the year ended 30 June 2014. IFRS is subject to ongoing review and endorsement by the EU or possible amendment by interpretative guidance and the issuance of new standards by the IASB.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The directors have a reasonable expectation that the group has adequate resources to continue in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  existence for the foreseeable future. Accordingly, they continue to adopt the going concern basis in preparing the condensed consolidated financial statements.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New accounting standards\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following amendments to the accounting standards, issued by the IASB or International Financial Reporting Interpretations Committee (IFRIC) and endorsed by the EU, have been adopted by the group from 1 July 2014 with no significant impact on the group's performance, financial position or disclosures:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IFRIC 21 - Levies\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amendment to IAS 19 - Defined benefit plans: Employee contributions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amendment to IAS 32 - Offsetting financial assets and financial liabilities\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amendment to IAS 39 - Novation of derivatives and continuation of hedge accounting\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amendment to IFRS 2 - Share based payments - Definition of vesting conditions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amendment to IFRS 3 - Accounting for contingent consideration in a business combination\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amendment to IFRS 3 - Scope exceptions for joint ventures\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amendment to IFRS 8 - Aggregation of operating segments and reconciliation of segment assets to entity's assets\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Amendment to IFRS 13 - Short-term receivables and payables\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The following standards issued by the IASB (not yet endorsed by the EU) have not yet been adopted by the group:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IFRS 9 - Financial instruments (effective in the year ending 30 June 2019, not yet endorsed by the EU) is ultimately intended to replace IAS 39 and covers the classification, measurement and derecognition of financial instruments together with a new hedge accounting model and new impairment methodology.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The group is currently considering the impact of IFRS 9 on its consolidated results and financial position.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 IFRS 15 - Revenue from contracts with customers (effective in the year ending 30 June 2018, not yet endorsed by the EU) is based on the principle that revenue is recognised when control of goods or services is transferred to the customer and provides a single, principles based five-step model to be applied to all sales contracts. It replaces the separate models for goods, services and construction contracts under current IFRS.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Based on a preliminary assessment the group currently believes that the adoption of IFRS 15 will have no significant impact on its consolidated results or financial position. [ 29-01-15 0701GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The comparative figures for the financial year ended 30 June 2014 are not the company's statutory accounts for that financial year. Those accounts have been reported on by the company's auditor and delivered to the registrar of companies. The report of the auditor was (i) unqualified, (ii) did not include a reference to any matters to which the auditor drew attention by way of emphasis without qualifying their report and (iii) did not contain a statement under section 498 (2) or (3) of the Companies Act 2006.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2. Segmental information\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The segmental information presented is consistent with management reporting provided to the executive committee (the chief operating decision maker).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The executive committee considers the business principally from a geographical perspective based on the location of third party sales and the business analysis is presented by geographical segment.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the six months ended 31 December 2014 \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2  changed its internal reporting structure to reflect changes made to management responsibilities as announced on 30 July 2014. As a result of this change, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2  reports the following geographical segments both for management reporting purposes and in the external financial statements: North America; Europe; Africa; Latin America and Caribbean; Asia Pacific and Corporate. As a consequence of this change, the financial information by geographical segments for comparative years has been amended and restated segmental information has been provided in the tables below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also include the Corporate function. Corporate revenues and costs are in respect of central costs, including finance, corporate relations, human resources and legal, as well as certain information systems, facilities and employee costs that are not allocable to the geographical segments or to ISC. They also include rents receivable and payable in respect of properties not used by the group in the manufacture, sale or distribution of premium drinks and the results of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Gleneagles Hotel\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2  uses shared services \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , including captive and outsourced centres, to deliver transaction processing activities for markets and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  entities. These centres are located in Hungary, Romania, Kenya, Colombia and the Philippines. The captive business service centre in Budapest also performs certain central finance activities, including elements of financial planning and reporting and treasury. The results of shared service \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  are recharged to the regions.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The segmental information for net sales and operating profit before exceptional items is reported at budgeted exchange rates in line with management reporting. For management reporting purposes the group measures the current period at, and restates the prior period net sales and operating profit to, the current year's budgeted exchange rates. These exchange rates are set prior to the financial year as part of the financial planning process and provide a consistent exchange rate to measure the performance of the business throughout the year. The adjustments required to retranslate the segmental information to actual exchange rates and to reconcile it to the group's reported results are shown in the tables below. The comparative segmental information, prior to retranslation, has not been restated at the current year's budgeted exchange rates but is presented at the budgeted rates for the year ended 30 June 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, for management reporting purposes \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2  presents separately the result of acquisitions and disposals completed in the current and prior year from the results of the geographical segments. The impact of acquisitions and disposals on net sales and operating profit is disclosed under the appropriate geographical segments in the tables below at budgeted exchange rates.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                Latin                    Eliminate \par                                              America                 