e exceptional items by the weighted average number of shares in issue.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earnings per share before exceptional items for the six months ended 31 December 2014 and 31 December 2013 are set out in the table below.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par                                                                     2014         2013 \par                                                              GBP million  GBP million \par  \par Profit attributable to equity shareholders of the \par  parent company                                                    1,311        1,599 \par Exceptional operating items attributable to equity \par  shareholders of the parent company                                  170           20 \par Non-operating items                                                 (98)        (138) \par Tax in respect of exceptional operating and non-operating \par  items                                                              (38)          (3) \par Discontinued \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2                                                 -           92 \par                                                                    1,345        1,570 \par  \par                                                                  million      million \par Weighted average number of shares in issue                         2,506        2,507 \par  \par                                                                    pence        pence \par Earnings per share before exceptional items                         53.7         62.6 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Free cash flow\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Free cash flow comprises the net cash flow from operating activities aggregated with the net movements in loans receivable and other investments and with the net purchase of property, plant and equipment and computer software that are included in net cash flow from investing activities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The remaining components of net cash flow from investing activities that do not form part of free cash flow, as defined by the group's management, are in respect of the acquisition and sale of businesses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The group's management regards the purchase and disposal of property, plant and equipment and computer software as ultimately non-discretionary since ongoing investment in plant, machinery and technology is required to support the day-to-day \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , whereas acquisitions and sales of businesses are discretionary. [ 29-01-15 0701GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Where appropriate, separate explanations are given for the impacts of acquisitions and sale of businesses, dividends paid and the purchase of own shares, each of which arises from decisions that are independent from the running of the ongoing underlying business.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Free cash flow reconciliations for the six months ended 31 December 2014 and 31 December 2013 are set out in the table below:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                  Six months ended  Six months ended \par                                                       31 December \par                                                              2014  31 December 2013 \par                                                       GBP million       GBP million \par  \par Net cash from operating activities                            957               606 \par Disposal of property, plant and equipment and \par  computer software                                             36                64 \par Purchase of property, plant and equipment and \par  computer software                                          (270)             (343) \par Movements in loans and other investments                     (24)               (1) \par Free cash flow                                                699               326 \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Return on average total invested capital\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Return on average total invested capital is used by management to assess the return obtained from the group's asset base and is calculated to aid evaluation of the performance of the business.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The profit used in assessing the return on average total invested capital reflects operating profit before exceptional items plus share of after tax results of associates and joint ventures after applying the tax rate before exceptional items for the period. Average total invested capital is calculated using the average derived from the consolidated balance sheets at the beginning and end of the period. Average capital employed comprises average net assets for the period, excluding post employment benefit net liabilities (net of deferred tax) and average net borrowings. This average capital employed is then aggregated with the average restructuring and integration costs net of tax, and goodwill written off to reserves at 1 July 2004, the date of transition to IFRS, to obtain the average total invested capital.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Calculations for the return on average total invested capital for the six months ended 31 December 2014 and 31 December 2013 are set out in the table below:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                             2014           2013 \par                                                      GBP million    GBP million \par  \par Operating profit                                           1,668    1     2,040 \par Exceptional operating items                                  171             20 \par Share of after tax results of associates and \par  joint ventures                                              113            181 \par Tax at the tax rate before exceptional items \par  of 18.3% (2013 - 19.4%)                                   (357)          (435) \par                                                            1,595          1,806 \par  \par Average net assets (excluding net post employment \par  liabilities)                                              8,649          8,468 \par Average net borrowings                                     9,759          8,750 \par Average integration and restructuring costs (net \par  of tax)                                                   1,587          1,457 \par Goodwill at 1 July 2004                                    1,562          1,562 \par Adjustment in respect of acquisition of USL                1,351              - \par Average total invested capital                            22,908         20,237 \par  \par Return on average total invested capital                   13.9%          17.8% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tax rate before exceptional items\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tax rate before exceptional items is calculated by dividing the total tax charge on continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before tax charges and credits, classified as or in respect of exceptional items, by profit before taxation adjusted to exclude the impact of exceptional operating and non-operating items, expressed as a percentage. The measure is used by management to assess the rate of tax applied to the group's continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before tax on exceptional items.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The tax rates from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before exceptional and after exceptional items for the six months ended 31 December 2014 and 31 December 2013 are set out in the table below:\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                                                                2014          2013 \par                                                         GBP million   GBP million \par  \par Tax before exceptional items (a)                                314           391 \par Tax in respect of exceptional items                            (38)           (3) \par Taxation on profit from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  (b)               276           388 \par  \par Profit from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before taxation \par  and exceptional items (c)                                    1,713         2,016 \par Non-operating items                                              98           138 \par Exceptional operating items                                   (171)          (20) \par Profit before taxation (d)                                    1,640         2,134 \par  \par Tax rate before exceptional items (a/c)                       18.3%         19.4% \par Tax rate from continuing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  after exceptional \par  items (b/d)                                                  16.8%         18.2% \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Other definitions\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Volume share is a brand's retail volume expressed as a percentage of the retail volume of all brands in its segment. Value share is a brand's retail sales expressed as a percentage of the retail sales of all brands in its segment. Unless otherwise stated, share refers to value share.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Price/mix is the number of percentage points by which the organic movement in net sales exceeds the organic movement in volume. The difference arises because of changes in the composition of sales between higher and lower priced variants/markets or as price changes are implemented.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 References to emerging markets include Russia, Eastern Europe, Turkey, Africa, Latin America and Caribbean, and Asia Pacific (excluding Australia, Korea and Japan).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 References to reserve brands include Johnnie Walker Blue Label, Johnnie Walker Green Label, Johnnie Walker Gold Label 18 year old, Johnnie Walker Gold Label Reserve, Johnnie Walker Platinum Label 18 year old, John Walker & Sons Collection, Johnnie Walker The Gold Route, Johnnie Walker The Royal Route, and other Johnnie Walker super premium brands; The Singleton, Cardhu, Talisker, Lagavulin and other malt brands; Buchanan's Special Reserve, Buchanan's Red Seal; Bulleit Bourbon, Bulleit Rye; Tanqueray No. TEN, Tanqueray Malacca; C\u238\'00\'EEroc, Ketel One vodka; Don Julio, Zacapa, Bundaberg SDlx, Haig Club whiskey and DeLe\u243\'00\'F3n Tequila.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 References to Global giants include the following brand families: Johnnie Walker, Smirnoff, Captain Morgan, Baileys, Tanqueray and Guinness. Local stars spirits excluding ready to drink include, but are not limited to, Bells, Buchanan's, Bundaberg, Bulleit, Cacique, Crown Royal, Don Julio, J&B, Old Parr, Yeni Raki, Ketel One vodka, scotch malts, Shui Jing Fang, White Horse, Windsor and Ypi\u243\'00\'F3ca.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 References to ready to drink also include ready to serve products, such as pre-mix cans in some markets, and progressive adult beverages in the United States and certain markets supplied by the United States.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 References to beer include Orijin ready to drink and non-alcoholic products such as Guinness Malta.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 RISK FACTORS\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's products are sold in over 180 countries worldwide, which subjects \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2  to risks and uncertainties in multiple jurisdictions across developed and developing markets. The group's aim is to manage risk and control its business and financial activities cost-effectively and in a manner that enables it to: exploit profitable business opportunities in a disciplined way; avoid or reduce risks that can cause loss, reputational damage or business failure; manage and mitigate historic risks and exposures of the group; support \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operational\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  effectiveness; and enhance resilience to external events. To achieve this, an ongoing process has been established for identifying, evaluating and managing risks faced by the group. A detailed description of the key risks and uncertainties facing the group are described in the 'Strategic report' section of the annual report for the year ended 30 June 2014 and under "Risk Factors" in the annual report on Form 20-F for the year ended 30 June 2014.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These key risks and uncertainties include: unfavourable economic conditions or political or other developments and risks in the countries in which \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2  operates; changes in consumer preferences and tastes and adverse impacts of a declining economy, among many factors, may adversely affect demand; litigation directed at the beverage alcohol industry and other litigation; climate change, or legal, regulatory or market measures to address climate change; water scarcity or poor quality; increased costs of raw materials or energy; regulatory decisions and changes in the legal and regulatory environment could increase \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's costs and liabilities or limit its business activities; increasing costs of monitoring and maintaining compliance with anti-corruption laws; failure to maintain \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's brand image and corporate reputation; competition may reduce \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's market share and margins; expected benefits may not be derived from \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's strategy focused on premium drinks or from its acquisitions or its cost-saving and restructuring programmes designed to enhance earnings; contamination, counterfeiting or other events could harm integrity of customer support for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's brands and adversely affect the sales of those brands; increased costs or shortages of talent; disruption to production facilities, business service centres or information systems and change programs may not deliver the benefits intended; movements in the value of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's pension funds, fluctuations [ 29-01-15 0701GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 in exchange rates and interest rates; failure to maintain or renegotiate distribution, supply, manufacturing and licence agreements on favourable terms; inability to protect \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's intellectual property rights; and difficulty in effecting service of US process and enforcing US legal process against the directors of Diageo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Cautionary statement concerning forward-looking statements\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This document contains 'forward-looking' statements. These statements can be identified by the fact that they do not relate only to historical or current facts. In particular, forward-looking statements include all statements that express forecasts, expectations, plans, outlook and projections with respect to future matters, including trends in results of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , margins, growth rates, overall market trends, the impact of changes in interest or exchange rates, the availability or cost of financing to \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 , anticipated cost savings or synergies, expected investments, the completion of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's strategic transactions and restructuring programmes, anticipated tax rates, expected cash payments, outcomes of litigation, anticipated deficit reductions in relation to pension schemes and general economic conditions. By their nature, forward-looking statements involve risk and uncertainty because they relate to events and depend on circumstances that will occur in the future. There are a number of factors that could cause actual results and developments to differ materially from those expressed or implied by these forward-looking statements, including factors that are outside \f28 \uc2 \f2 Diageo\f28 \uc2 \f2 's control.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 These factor