\par\par \b \uc2 Peru's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Sells $100M To Support Sol -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,318 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Dezember 2014\par 20:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:43 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell $100M, aiming to support the weakening sol. The sol ended the session at PEN2.971 per U.S. dollar, a 6.11% decline so far this year. "Exchange rate volatility may continue in 2015," Scotiabank in Peru says. (robert.kozak@wsj.com @robertkozak2)\par \par \uc2 1910 GMT - The rouble rout and continued declines in commodities failed to translate into a risk off move, with NZD and equity markets higher, says \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 . It notes that a rise in the global dairy prices in the overnight GlobalDairyTrade auction helped support NZD/USD although the fall in skim milk powder "was a poor sign." On the day, investors will be watching for the New Zealand balance of payments data but the main focus is on FOMC minutes due early Thursday morning in New Zealand. The pair is at 0.7788 and \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  tips a short-term range of 0.7730-0.7850. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 13:10 EST - With the US economy set to grow at a faster pace next year, helped along by the slump in oil prices, commodities markets must "fear a further strengthening" by the dollar, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 . \uc2 The Fed\uc2  is likely to start raising rates in mid-2015, the firm says, even as Europe moves into QE mode to stave off falling prices. \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says precious metals and energy markets are likely to be worst off as the dollar gains. (matthew.cowley@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:03 EST - The White House does not believe that Russia's ongoing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  troubles will have any major impact on the US economy. "If I was chairman of President Putin's Council of Economic Advisers, I would be extremely concerned. They are between a rock and a hard place in economic policy," Jason Furman, chairman of the Council of Economic Advisers, told reporters at the White House today. But he added: "In terms of the U.S. economy, we have enormous domestic strengths in terms of our consumers, their confidence, their increased spending, in terms of our businesses investing. Only 0.10 percent of the U.S. GDP is exports to Russia." (byron.tau@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:59 EST - This year's grads are set to start work with a bang. Fifty-one percent of employers plan to offer signing bonuses to Class of 2015 college graduates--more than to any class in 5 years, according to a survey from the \uc2 National Association of Colleges and Employers\uc2 . Most of the signing bonuses will go to business, engineering or computer-science majors. Chemical-engineering majors are expected to receive the largest average signing bonuses at $5,250, followed by engineering without a sub-specialization at $5,107 and computer science at $4,364. (lindsay.gellman@wsj.com; @lindsaygellman)\par \par \uc2 12:56 EST - In the wake of last night's surprise rate hike in Russia and the continued slide of the ruble, Capital Economics' Andrew Kenningham says the "prospects for Russia's economy look even bleaker than they did a few days ago," declaring there's "now little doubt that Russia is heading for a deep recession." He acknowledges Russia's problems "will clearly have knock-on effects elsewhere," but cautions about blowing them out of proportion. "The economic links between Russia and the rest of the world are relatively small." (erik.holm@wsj.com; @erikholmwsj)\par \par \uc2 1632 GMT Greece is on track to meet its fiscal goals for a third straight year despite the political and economic uncertainty that has gripped the country for the last few weeks. The \uc2 Bank of Greece\uc2 , the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says the budget target "is likely to be overachieved for the third year in a row, indicating that the improvement in fiscal aggregates is sustainable." Steep spending cuts have helped Greece's two-party coalition offset a drop in revenues in the first eleven months of the year. The latest data from the Finance Ministry shows that the central government's primary surplus--which doesn't take into account interest payments--rose to 3.53 billion euros for the January-to-November period, above the EUR2.9 billion target set by Greece's international creditors. (stelios.bouras@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:22 EST - The public finances of Canada's oil-rich provinces are taking it on the chin with the crude price decline. Alberta has warned of a C$7B hit to revenue. On Tuesday, Newfoundland and Labrador said the projected budget deficit for FY14-15 would be C$916M, or nearly double the earlier forecast of C$538M. Mining, and oil-and-gas extraction account for nearly a third of Newfoundland's GDP, meaning the Atlantic province will be one of the big regional losers in Canada from the crude swoon. CIBC expects the Newfoundland economy to shrink 1% in 2015. (paul.vieira@wsj.com, @paulvieira).\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:06 EST - Treasurys are paring today's rally as safe-haven demand eases with oil trimming earlier declines and US stocks turn higher, encouraging some bond investors to book profits. \f28 \uc2 \f2 David Ader\f28 \uc2 \f2  of CRT Capital says Treasurys' gains have been "exaggerated" by year-end impacts. "Leading into year-end, investors are not wiling to take new risk" in things like the ruble, other EM assets or high-yield bonds. "They want to preserve capital and make fresh asset-allocation decisions in January." The 10-year Treasury is 10/32 higher, yielding 2.082%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1603 GMT \f28 \uc2 \f2 Denmark's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pencils in DKK75 billion in domestic government bond issuance in 2015, it says, broadly in line with the market's expectations. Next year's domestic issuance target is below this year's actual issuance of DKK102.64 billion, which slightly overshot the DKK100 billion target. Next year's strategy is to focus on the issuance of the 10-year nominal bond but also to build up the planned new two- and five-year debts, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says. A new 2020-dated bond will be launched at the Feb. 4 auction, while a new 2018-dated two-year bond is planned in the first half of 2015, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  says. (emese.bartha@wsj.com; @EmeseBartha) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:02 EST - Strategists at Citi Private Bank estimate consumers of energy (that's everybody basically) stand to save nearly a $1T/year from the plunge in crude-oil prices. Given the scale of the savings, they downplay the idea that the fall indicates a softening of the global economy. Conversely, it's usually sharp oil-price spikes which touch off recessions. "There are no cases we know of when abundant, surplus crude-oil supplies and windfall savings for global consumers resulted in broad economic contractions." (anna.raff@wsj.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1548 GMT Law firm Linklaters says one side-effect of the plunging oil price might be a rise in Islamic finance capital markets activity. Neil Miller, global head of Islamic finance at Linklaters, says: "With the threat of a lower oil price landscape as we head into 2015, previous experience suggests that greater opportunities for the commercial financing of major projects may arise." Colleague, Jonathan Fried, Capital Markets partner at Linklaters in Dubai, adds "The flexibility and innovation seen in sukuk structures in 2014 will provide a strong foundation for the development of the sukuk market and its expansion into new sectors and geographies in 2015."(josie.cox@wsj.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 16, 2014 14:44 ET (19:44 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141216eacg003e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
