\par\par \b \uc2 CFTC Dollar Bulls Pull Back on Bets -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,421 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2014\par 22:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 16:45 EST - Investors grew less bullish on the dollar for the second straight week as growing fears over global growth and plunging oil prices sent investors in haven assets and prompted them to exit heavy strong-dollar bets. The net amount of investor cash wagering the greenback will rise against rivals fell 10.8% to $42.4B in the week ended Dec. 9, according to CFTC data. The moves unwound dollar gains on the back of robust US jobs data for November that had vaulted the greenback to fresh multi-year highs against the yen and euro just one trading session earlier. Accordingly, bearish euro and yen wagers thinned last week by 14.1% and 6.7%, respectively. Net bullish greenback positions should continue, as investors reduce risk and pull back on their largest bets, which include dollar-longs, ahead of next week's FOMC meeting. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:29 EST - Morningstar is lowering the rating for \uc2 Actavis\uc2  from triple-B to triple-B-minus, just one notch above junk, in response to the company's planned acquisition of \uc2 Allergan\uc2  for more than $65B. Morningstar notes that the cash portion of the deal will involve $27.5B of new term loans and bonds, and the company's debt to earnings ratio will rise to the mid-to-high 4s, up from 3.3 for \uc2 Actavis\uc2  at the end of September. But Morningstar says the firm's "commitment to deleveraging" and its increasing size and diversity keeps it in investment grade territory. (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:48 EST - Although "many investors are growing more positive" on America's biggest banks heading into 2015 on the grounds that regulatory and legal pressures are peaking and trading and investment banking are picking up, Nomura is "incrementally more cautious as we believe investors remain too complacent about the potential for sustained regulatory pressure." The investment bank expects further increases in capital requirements for the biggest banks as well as more pressure on FICC trading revenue--as big banks reduce short-term wholesale funding exposures--and net interest income as banks prepare for rules on liquidity and loss absorbing capital. (saabira.chaudhuri@wsj.com; @SaabiraC)\par \par \uc2 15:23 EST - The Fed's Board of Governors will hold a closed-door meeting Tuesday to discuss "medium-term monetary-policy issues," according to a notice posted today. That's also the first of the 2-day FOMC policy meeting. Meetings on the subject are becoming routine for the board, which also held them in October, September, July, June and April. It suggests officials are still working on, or at least discussing, the tools they'll eventually use to raise interest rates. (ben.leubsdorf@wsj.com; @BenLeubsdorf)\par \par \uc2 15:21 EST - Sanctions approved by the US this week on select members of Venezuela's regime come at a choice time, IHS says, as the risk consultancy expects "protests and riots to increase in the 1-year outlook because of an economic crisis." The sanctions won't affect Venezuela's vital oil industry, or any sector of the economy specifically. But that didn't stop President Maduro in a speech this week to lash out against congressional approval of the sanctions bill, which he told supporters were targeting the whole country. IHS says there is risk the government, already grappling with record-low approval ratings, may face instability if widespread protests--like the kind which broke out earlier this year--were to rise up again. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:52 EST - Investors can't always live with state-run companies and their politicized ways. But in emerging markets, can they always live without them? Wisdom Tree last week brought out the Emerging Markets ex-State-Owned Enterprises Fund, which tracks an index of only private-sector companies. Wisdom Tree advertises this as a way to bet on companies where there are lower risks of governments acting against the interests of minority shareholders. Trouble is, private companies in places like Asia, heavily controlled by families, aren't exactly paragons of investor friendliness. Plus, some investors may care about state firms precisely because they are sensitive to government actions, which matter much more in emerging economies than in richer ones. In India, a new business-friendly leader came to power this year and helped propel \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  shares 75%, compared with 31% for the top 100 stocks. In China, the CSI Central State-owned Enterprises Composite index is up 51% this year, beating the broader market's 39% gain. In many emerging markets, governments do pick winners. (abheek.bhattacharya@wsj.com; @abheekb)\par \par \uc2 13:19 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease volatility but won't affect broader direction, says the bank's chief economist. A good part of Peru's bank loans are in dollars, so big \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings could hurt companies which have loans in the greenback but revenue in the sol. "Our economy doesn't have the luxury to permit abrupt depreciations," says Armas. "We are going to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce volatility, but it won't go against the tendency." The sol has fallen some 6% this year against the dollar. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 13:00 EST - \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again trims its 2014 economic-growth view, lowering it another half-point to 2.6%. There's been multiple cuts as this year progressed in the wake of weak output at the country's key mining sector. This year's GDP will pale in comparison to 2013's 5.8% advance. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief economist Adrian Armas says GDP growth should improve next year as commodity output recovers. "We expect a good recovery in the agriculture, fishing and mining sectors." (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 1757 GMT As a major oil importer, the euro area should benefit from the slump in oil prices but the positive impact will be limited, \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says. "\uc2 OPEC\uc2 's decision to allow oil prices to fall creates a positive growth impact for the euro area, but only enough to broadly balance the weakness we had observed in investment and exports in recent months," the bank says in its European Outlook report for 2015. Lower oil prices should be at least modestly positive for real private consumption, the report says. The danger, however, is that cheaper oil could delay necessary monetary easing and well-designed structural reforms, the bank says. (georgi.kantchev@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 12:27 EST - Argentina's primary fiscal deficit widened sharply in October, with the Finance Ministry saying the shortfall totaled ARS15.2B ($1.8B), versus ARS2.8B a year earlier, as revenues rose ARS17.7B and primary spending jumped ARS30.2B. The primary fiscal deficit, which excludes debt payments, is considered an indicator of the government's ability to finance its spending commitments and meet financial obligations. (robert.kozak@wsj.com; @robertkozak2)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 11:50 EST - \f28 \uc2 \f2 Canada Mortgage and Housing Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . plans to hike the "guarantee fees" some financial institutions pay to have residential loans backstopped in its MBS program. CMHC, Canada's dominant mortgage insurer, gets a fee in exchange for assurances which cover principal and interest payments. The fee increases, which take effect April 1, vary depending on the term of the MBS pool and are intended to help encourage "the development of alternative funding options in the private market." It's not immediately known what impact the fee increases will have on consumer pricing for mortgages. (rita.trichur@wsj.com; @ritatrichur)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1647 GMT Croatia's GDP is seen contracting 0.2% next year from the year-on-year decline of 0.7% expected in 2014. "Without hard solid evidence of public investment growth, while private investments remain on hold, we see a mild GDP contraction during 2015," says \f28 \uc2 \f2 UniCredit\f28 \uc2 \f2  analyst Hrvoje Dolenec. Croatian households are focusing on paring their debts, leaving the country's hospitality sector as the main economic driver. With public debts at above 80% of GDP, the country will need cooperating closely with the \f28 \uc2 \f2 European Commission\f28 \uc2 \f2  on its excessive deficit procedure, or EDP, regime, he adds. (leos.rousek@wsj.com, @LeoRousek) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par markettalk@wsj.com \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 December 12, 2014 16:45 ET (21:45 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141212eacc003j1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
