\par\par \b \uc2 Will \uc2 MetLife\uc2  Sue If It's Tabbed a SIFI? -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,432 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2014\par 19:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:30 EST - \uc2 MetLife\uc2  (MET) continued to stay mostly mum during its investor conference call this morning about possibly suing a new federal panel to avert being named a "systemically important financial institution." CEO \uc2 Steven Kandarian\uc2  was asked whether the House's passage this week of a bill giving the Fed more flexibility to tailor capital rules to the insurance industry makes suing unlikely. While "delighted" with the bill, he noted the rules have yet to be drafted, so "we don't know what those rules will be even with the flexibility they've been provided." If named a SIFI, "we'll look at the [panel's] reasoning and then we'll make a decision." (leslie.scism@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:25 EST - The recent plunge in oil prices will have strong lasting positive impacts on many advanced economies including Continental Europe and Japan, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. Lower oil prices will buoy equities in Europe and act as a tax cut to consumers there, as well as the U.S., the bank says. But the downside to cheaper crude will be disruptions in credit markets and oil company spending cuts that will ripple across energy stocks and hurt employment in the sector. Emerging markets that are big oil exporters -- including Russia and Brazil -- stand out as "major losers," the report said. Even as European equities gain, the U.S. high-yield market has been severely affected and returns are expected to stay down, \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. (lynn.cook@wsj.com; @LynnJCook)\par \par \uc2 13:24 EST - \uc2 Duke Energy\uc2  (DUK) says it has reached a deal with rooftop solar companies for its South Carolina utility customers who install solar panels. More than a dozen states are struggling with rules that require utilities to pay their solar customers for excess power from their panels, over concerns that non-solar customers end up footing the bill. Under the deal, Duke will pay its South Carolina solar customers for their excess electricity at Duke's retail power rate, through 2020. To cover the cost of the payments, Duke will charge its other customers a fee that represents the difference between the retail rate that Duke pays the solar customers and the actual value of their excess power. Duke and the solar firms will revisit the payment scheme in 2020 and potentially make changes that would start Jan. 1, 2021. (cassandra.sweet@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:19 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to ease volatility but won't affect broader direction, says the bank's chief economist. A good part of Peru's bank loans are in dollars, so big \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  swings could hurt companies which have loans in the greenback but revenue in the sol. "Our economy doesn't have the luxury to permit abrupt depreciations," says Armas. "We are going to continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to reduce volatility, but it won't go against the tendency." The sol has fallen some 6% this year against the dollar. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 13:13 EST - As the Dow industrials is looking at its 2nd-biggest percentage drop of 2014--and worst since January--with its current 3.3% decline, performance for the broader S&P 500 and Nasdaq Composite both isn't as stark for the year with their 2%-plus slides. The \uc2 S&P\uc2 's 2.7% drop would only be the least in 2 months, though also the 2nd worst of 2014, while the Nasdaq's 2% decline only the 6th-biggest of 2014. Meanwhile, this would just be the 7th week this year that the DJIA falls more than 1%. (dan.strumpf@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:00 EST - \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  again trims its 2014 economic-growth view, lowering it another half-point to 2.6%. There's been multiple cuts as this year progressed in the wake of weak output at the country's key mining sector. This year's GDP will pale in comparison to 2013's 5.8% advance. \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief economist Adrian Armas says GDP growth should improve next year as commodity output recovers. "We expect a good recovery in the agriculture, fishing and mining sectors." (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 12:49 EST - German lender \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2  (CBK.XE) likely having to pay more than $1B to resolve probes examining whether the company violated US economic sanctions and money-laundering laws is a further sign of increased scrutiny on European banks by America. Prosecutors have been extracting massive fines from companies there for sanctions and money-laundering violations the last year, including an $8.9B settlement with \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  (BNP.FR). The Justice Department, Manhattan DA's office and New York state Superintendent of Financial Services have been investigating \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 's alleged dealing with an Iranian state-sponsored shipping company while the Manhattan US Attorney's office is probing poor money-laundering controls. Shares fell 2.6% in Frankfurt in a broadly weak equities market. (christopher.matthews@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:45 EST - An appeals court ruling that vacated the 2012 insider trading convictions of two former hedge-fund traders is reverberating through the downtown Manhattan federal court. Later this month, a judge set a hearing date to discuss whether the ruling would affect the guilty pleas of several other insider trading defendants. Some legal specialists are saying that prosecutors might have been wise to quit while they were ahead. Prosecutors continued to press ahead with cases that weren't as clear-cut, involving tippees further removed from the sources of information, and without the benefit of wiretaps that had bolstered the earlier string of convictions and guilty pleas. But alumni of the Manhattan U.S. attorney's office said the criticism is unfair and that the hardest cases were saved for last, and there was always a chance of defeat, a risk worth taking. (christopher.matthews@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:43 EST - Much is being made of Wall Street's "win" this week on Capitol Hill by securing several long-sought changes to Dodd-Frank, particularly House passage of language which would significantly scale back a requirement that banks spin off certain swaps-trading activities. But the big banks suffered a few big losses this week, too. At the start the week was the Fed unveiling a new capital requirement for the 8 largest banks. There was also the PR damage \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  (C) and Wall Street generally sustained in liberal Democrats' fight against the swaps change, which doesn't help an industry that's been desperately trying to regain the public's trust. KBW analyst \uc2 Brian Gardner\uc2  tweeted last night, "on net, Wall St lost this week." (victoria.mcgrane@wsj.com; @vgmac)\par \par \uc2 12:40 EST - Kellogg (K) raised some eyebrows when it reduced the payouts executives would get if K gets acquired. Such a move has sometimes been a precursor to a sale, though it's also something corporate-governance experts advocate for at all companies. The change to K's "change of control" policy, disclosed in an \uc2 SEC\uc2  filing Thursday, came as the company has been struggling with persistently declining sales. A spokeswoman says K is simply updating the change-of-control policy "to better align with evolving market practices." Shares, which rebounded 3.6% yesterday amid the \uc2 SEC\uc2  filing, are down 1.8% today amid the broader market's slide. (annie.gasparro@wsj.com; @annie_gasparro)\par \par \uc2 12:31 EST - \uc2 Sherwin-Williams\uc2  (SHW) rallied 3% Thursday after announcing its HGTV Home paints will be stocked in Lowe's (LOW) stores starting in March. But during a conference call today, some analysts pressed the paint maker about the risk that such sales will cannibalize business at SHW's stores. CEO \uc2 Chris Connor\uc2  insisted that won't happen, adding some professional painters prefer buying supplies at big-box retailers. In a generally weak market, SHW is more than reversing yesterday's gains as shares slide 3.8%. (bob.hagerty@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:27 EST - Argentina's primary fiscal deficit widened sharply in October, with the Finance Ministry saying the shortfall totaled ARS15.2B ($1.8B), versus ARS2.8B a year earlier, as revenues rose ARS17.7B and primary spending jumped ARS30.2B. The primary fiscal deficit, which excludes debt payments, is considered an indicator of the government's ability to finance its spending commitments and meet financial obligations. (robert.kozak@wsj.com; @robertkozak2)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 December 12, 2014 13:30 ET (18:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141212eacc002yl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
