\par\par \b \uc2 Peru  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Steps In Again to Slow Sol Slide -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,444 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 November 2014\par 20:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:43 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has stepped up \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in recent sessions as it looks to slow the depreciation of the sol against the US dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  today sold $200M in the spot market, its biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  since Sept. 26. That follows \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  yesterday and Friday, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $185M in each of those sessions. So far this year, the sol has depreciated 4.43% against the dollar. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 14:39 EST - A $1.5B bond sale from \uc2 Noble Energy\uc2  (\uc2 NBL\uc2 ) is causing a bit of a selloff in bonds from other energy companies, one investor says. The firm is selling 10- and 30-year notes, with the latter offering a yield of 2.05 percentage points more than Treasurys, according to \uc2 S&P Capital IQ\uc2  LCD. That's cheap compared to its outstanding 2043 bonds, which traded yesterday at a spread of 1.80. Meanwhile, the yield spread on a 2044 \uc2 Anadarko Petroleum\uc2  bond rose 15 bps to 1.70 today, and the yield spread on a 2020 Transocean bond rose 27 bps to 4.56, according to MarketAxess. (mike.cherney@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:36 EST - Two reports due tomorrow may shift expectations about Friday's October payrolls number. \uc2 Joseph LaVorgna\uc2  of \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says people concerned about the labor markets--and who isn't?--should pay attention to the \uc2 ADP\uc2  private-jobs report and the employment index within the ISM non-manufacturing report. An upside surprise for either could put an upside spin on projections for the payrolls number, says LaVorgna. For now, economists think the US economy added 233K net new jobs in October, a bit less than September's initial reading of 248K. (kathleen.madigan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:34 EST - The key message from last night's GlobalDairyTrade auction is that global dairy markets remain well-supplied in the short-term, says ANZ. "There were some signs" a 2Q revival could occur amid "some large improvement in powder prices for the April and May contracts," but that "was the only real ray of light." ANZ notes if weak prices persist through the peak sales period and the revival only occurs in the last quarter of the season, it will be very difficult for Fonterra to achieve its milk-price forecast of NZ$5.30/kilogram for solids. "We are anticipating further downside to the farm-gate price at the next update, which is likely to be in early December." ANZ is tipping a price around NZ$4.85/kg, which "would see further belt tightening from farmers." (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 14:31 EST - Campus Crest's (CCG) plethora of news this morning included what RBC calls "significant and necessary changes which we believe should bring about positive long-term change, both in strategy and in \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Leading off was the departure of the CEO and CFO, which the investment bank describes "as positive as confidence in prior management had eroded, making change in both leadership and direction necessary." Meanwhile, buying the remaining stakes in 30 properties owned with Copper Beach for $280M "should remove uncertainty, simplify CCG's ownership structure and allow CCG to better manage and dispose of assets as needed." And as the student-housing REITs stops its own construction, RBC says "development made little economic sense" for CCG. Shares bounce 16% to $7.32 after opening at a fresh record low of $6. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 14:30 EST - As \uc2 TransCanada\uc2  (TRP TRP.T) moves forward with a slate of multi-year, multi-billion dollar projects, it says it will look to raise money through project finance and joint venture partnerships, but also consider asset sales and common equity financing if needed. "As the balance sheet grows, we'll again chew through the cheaper sources of capital," such as debt, before tapping alternative financing methods, CFO Don Marchard said on a conference call. The Calgary-based company's capital spending program totals $46B, including $24B in oil and other liquids pipelines and $20B in natural gas pipelines. (chester.dawson@wsj.com; @DeleverTheFirm)\par \par \uc2 14:13 EST - "Despite a lackluster energy trading environment," accelerated cost-cutting at ICE "and an increased capital return are helping to offset these headwinds," says Jefferies in the wake of the exchange operator's 3Q profit beat. ICE last quarter accelerated cost synergies from its \uc2 NYSE\uc2  acquisition and has boosted the pace of stock buybacks, going from $350M in 2Q to $449M last quarter. Raymond James notes the earnings outperformance was largely on lower-than-expected compensation costs, coming as ICE again cut 2014 operating-expense guidance today. Shares rise 3.5% to $217.10, halving the year's decline. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 14:03 EST - Even the Eagle Ford Shale, considered one of the top oilfields to drill, has its rough spots. Baird has said the South Texas oilfield can be drilled with US benchmark crude as low as $53-$65 a barrel. But a new analysis by Bernstein shows that while some areas of the Eagle Ford can withstand low prices, a few of the region's counties are uneconomic even when oil is at $100 a barrel. Among the worst are Webb and McMullen counties, where Bernstein analysts say operators "could suffer if lower prices persist." (erin.ailworth@wsj.com; @ailworth)\par \par \uc2 14:00 EST - Amid \uc2 Vitamin Shoppe\uc2 's (\uc2 VSI\uc2 ) 3Q miss, Sterne Agee downgrades the retailer to hold as it highlights "a sector on very-fragile footing, uncertainty regarding the future of senior management and the company's inability to forecast/control" margins. As consumers potentially move "toward other forms of more "natural" nutrition (i.e., flat out eating healthier) and no signs of a tangible industry response to reignite growth via innovation and/or a large-scale campaign to fight the negative press, visibility into a recovery is hazy at best." Wedbush, which downgrades to neutral, admits its recently lowered views on \uc2 VSI\uc2  weren't conservative enough as "multivitamin and fish-oil categories have not yet recovered from negative press" while "increased advertising spend to spur online sales proved ineffective." \uc2 VSI\uc2  falls 5.5% to $43.22. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com; @kevinkingsbury)\par \par \uc2 13:56 EST - Mexican brakes-and-suspension maker Rassini (RASSINI.MX) expects annual sales to reach $1B by late next year or early 2016 from current the $860M, says finance executive Juan Pablo Sanchez, as the company continues to sign new contracts and invests $54M in expansions in Mexico, the US and Brazil. The company this month changed its corporate name from San Luis to Rassini, its better-known brand name, and it will make the brakes for \uc2 Audi\uc2 's Q5 when the \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  (VOW.XE) unit starts building those vehicles in Mexico in 2016. RASSINI.MX is up more than fivefold this year. (anthony.harrup@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:55 EST - The power and utilities market seems to have recovered from the impact of private equity-backed \uc2 Energy Future Holdings\uc2 ' collapse. Deal-making volume was up a141% to $11.1B during 3Q from $4.6B the prior year, according to \uc2 PricewaterhouseCoopers\uc2  US. The average deal size was also bigger as the same number of deals, 12, was recorded for both periods. The increase was due to strategic acquisitions by corporate players on the demand side, and divestitures of nonregulated power assets on the supply front, PwC says. Going forward, PwC predicts there may be "vertical integration of gas pipeline assets with power utilities." (shasha.dai@wsj.com; @ShashaDai)\par \par \uc2 13:48 EST - Arguments against current widespread use of antibiotics in meat production include fears that drug-resistant bacteria can spread to humans via meat products, but scientists see a new, six-legged threat: houseflies and other insects that could carry antibiotic-resistant bacteria from farms and wastewater facilities to cities or picnics. \uc2 Kansas State University\uc2  researchers tested bacteria found inside flies and roaches near swine and poultry farms and found they carried more drug-resistant bacteria than insects collected several miles away, according to KSU study published earlier this year. (jacob.bunge@wsj.com; @jacobbunge) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November 04, 2014 14:43 ET (19:43 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141104eab4003o2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
