\par\par \b \uc2 Peru Sol Weakens, Even With  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Selling Dollars -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,532 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2014\par 23:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 17:00 EDT - The \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market today to sell US dollars, however the local sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  still weakened. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $95M at an average of PEN2.912 per dollar. That wasn't enough to stem the weakening of the sol, which depreciated to PEN2.912 from PEN2.908 in the previous session. So far this year, the sol has weakened 4% against the greenback. (ryan.dube@wsj.com; @duberyan)\par \par \uc2 16:55 EDT - \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  (PFE) board member Suzanne Nora Johnson says the US could benefit from corporate tax reform. "The most important thing here is that we get some kind of tax reform that makes the U.S. [stronger] in a competitive situation and that is critical because if we really do want to be on a level playing field with the rest of the world, then we're going to need to get it right," Johnson says on CNBC. PFE earlier this year dropped a $120B bid for \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  (AZN) which would've been a so-called inversion. (gillian.tan@wsj.com, @GillianTan)\par \par \uc2 16:53 EDT - \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2  analyst \uc2 Mark Mahaney\uc2  expects \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (TWTR) to report $332M in revenue for the three months through September, a forecast that is below a consensus of around $352M. While Mr. Mahaney is optimistic TWTR can grow into one of the major consumer tech companies such as social networking rival \uc2 Facebook\uc2  (FB), he said the company still needs to prove it can increase its reach with advertisers as well as its user base and engagement metrics. "That said, we'd argue that TWTR's 'mainstreamization' efforts have just recently begun." Mr. Mahaney estimates \uc2 Twitter\uc2 's user base will reach 280 million, adding 9 million from the previous quarter. TWTR reports 3Q earnings on Monday after the market closes. (yoree.koh@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:38 EDT - \uc2 P&G\uc2  (PG) has been "a U.S. domiciled company for 177 years so if the past is any indication of the future, we're not going anywhere," CFO \uc2 Jon Moeller\uc2  says on CNBC in response to a question about inversions from Perella Weinberg co-founder Peter Weinberg. "When you have an uncompetitive tax rate and an economy that's globalizing, three things are bound to happen relative to domicile and \uc2 M&A\uc2 ," Moeller said. Companies will move, they'll will get started offshore rather than onshore and "U.S. assets will be purchased by foreign interests simply because of the tax arbitrage that's there to fuel those transactions," he added. Those are among reasons the US should consider tax reform, he says. "I think there are big stakes here, we're talking about American jobs, American industry and American standard of living," he added. (gillian.tan@wsj.com, @GillianTan)\par \par \uc2 16:24 EDT - When Venezuela earlier this month changed the terms under which it repays China with oil for billions of dollars in loans, speculation soared over what it means for the cash-strapped South American country. Some commentators called it a default. But BofA says it's wrong to do so because there is no change to the loans' face value and "does not alter the financial commitments of Venezuela." The new repayment structure scraps the daily requirement of 330,000 bpd of oil to China. Instead, it allows Venezuela to only send as much oil as necessary to pay for loans, BofA says. For example, high oil prices last year meant Venezuela was sending more barrels than were needed and ended up getting $9.6B back from overpayment, bank notes. (kejal.vyas@wsj.com; @kejalvyas)\par \par \uc2 16:14 EDT - Remember those edgy days for US stocks last week, when terms like "correction," and even "capitulation," were being tossed around? Feels kind of silly now, doesn't it, after S&P 500 vaults more than 4% higher for the week, \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Comp up 5.3% and blue chips up a relatively pedestrian 2.6%. All the worries of a global economic slowdown have faded -- though the risks still loom -- and investors are pleased with earnings reports that put companies on track to post roughly the same profit and sales growth they saw in 2Q, up about 7.5% and maybe 4%, respectively. DJIA up 127.51 to 16805.41, and \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Comp climbs 30.92 to 4483.72. S&P 500 ends 13.76 higher at 1964.58. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:07 EDT - Investors grew slightly less bullish on the dollar, and less bearish on the yen, after fears of a slowdown in growth in the world's largest economies erupted on Oct. 15, sending bond yields and stocks plunging as volatility throttled financial markets. The net amount of investor cash betting the dollar would rise slipped 4.3% to $41.4B in the week ended Oct. 21, according to CFTC data. Meanwhile, investors squared some of their yen short wagers versus the greenback, paring net bearish bets to 29% last week to $8.4B. Earlier this week, the dollar-selloff stabilized. Next week's numbers should reflect a moderate recovery in bullish dollar positions on the back of stronger US data. (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:50 EDT - The Fed's Board of Governors will hold a closed meeting Tuesday, with "Discussion of Medium-Term Monetary Policy Issues" on the agenda, according to a notice posted today. A two-day Federal Open Market Committee meeting starts the same day, and similar board meetings accompanied the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's September, July, June and April policy meetings. That suggests \uc2 Fed\uc2  officials are continuing to discuss the tools they'll use to eventually raise interest rates, even after releasing a "Policy Normalization Principles and Plans" statement last month. (ben.leubsdorf@wsj.com, @BenLeubsdorf)\par \par \uc2 15:33 EDT - The \uc2 USDA\uc2 's cattle-on-feed report provides new evidence that fewer female animals are entering the food supply chain as ranchers work to increase production by breeding the next generation of beef calves, rather than fattening those cattle for meat. Heifers and heifer calves, which are females that have yet to give birth, in commercial feedyards through Oct 1 totaled 3.55 million head, down 3% from the same time last year, compared with a 1% drop in the number of all cattle on feed. The nation's herd is at its smallest size in decades, contributing to a surge in the price of burgers and steaks. (kelsey.gee@wsj.com; @kelseykgee)\par \par \uc2 15:30 EDT - Railroad and truck freight broker \uc2 Hub Group (HUBG) \uc2 is down 12% at $34.33 after reporting a 76% decrease in 3Q net income amid special charges and tough market conditions. A shortage of truck drivers and traffic-clogged railroads contributed to a 2% decline in revenue from HUBG's intermodal rail business. The company estimates that rail-service problems cost about 7c/share in 3Q profit. Excluding the charges, HUBG reported EPS of 49c vs Street's 53c expectation. CEO \uc2 David Yeager\uc2  told analysts that rail service will improve as the railroads add more crews and locomotives, but he doesn't expect normal service to resume before next spring. (robert.tita@wsj.com, @bob_tita)\par \par \uc2 15:30 EDT - It's a kooky idea, and probably has zero chance of seeing the light of day. So why does \uc2 Marc Chandler\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2 , raise the possibility that the ECB might buy Treasuries instead of other assets like covered bonds and ABS as it looks to ease monetary policy? Because technically it gets around a key problem the ECB faces--which countries' or companies' bonds it should buy. "The ECB could take a page from the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's playbook," says Chandler. "It could say that it would like to buy member bonds, but the legal and political hurdles are insurmountable. Instead, it will buy foreign bonds, and for transparency, liquidity and safety, US Treasuries stand out." (pedro.dacosta@wsj.com; @pdacosta)\par \par \uc2 15:10 EDT - Got a feeling the top people running the company are sometimes out of touch? You're not alone. A survey of 316 executives and managers by a division of the \uc2 American Management Association\uc2  finds one-third of those polled said their organization's senior team operates within "a leadership bubble" most of the time. Half indicated this occurs at least occasionally. Asked if they've ever experienced out-of-touch leadership, 90% of respondents said the leadership team usually or sometimes wasn't aware of the problem. Most survey participants blamed poor internal communications or the corporate culture. But only 16% said the problem was due to the CEO's personality. (joann.lublin@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 October 24, 2014 17:00 ET (21:00 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141024eaao003ga\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
