\par\par \b \uc2 Peru  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Sells Dollars to Support Sol -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,374 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 Oktober 2014\par 22:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 16:30 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, selling $10M today at PEN2.9051 per US dollar, aiming to support the weakening sol. So far this year the sol has depreciated 3.82% against the greenback, ending today at PEN2.9070. A survey of 21 economic analysts conducted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  recently found expectations that the sol will end this year at PEN2.90 per dollar. (robert.kozak@wsj.com @robertkozak2)\par \par \uc2 16:02 EDT - Companies shipped 8.3B barrels of oil on US pipelines in 2013, up 11% from a year ago, according to federal data compiled by two industry associations and published today. The surging volume of crude shipments outpaced construction of new pipelines, which increased 6% in 2013 from the previous year, according to the \uc2 Association of Oil Pipe Lines\uc2  and the \uc2 American Petroleum Institute\uc2 . The data highlight how drillers are tapping oil from shale-rock formations faster than companies can build new pipelines, even as railroads are taking on a much bigger role in carrying crude. (daniel.gilbert@wsj.com; @WSJGilbert)\par \par \uc2 15:31 EDT - Overnight strength in AUD and JPY saw the respective NZD crosses push toward the bottom end of recent ranges. NZD/AUD is at 0.8895, close to the 2014 low of 0.8860. BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Raiko Shareef says if Thursday's jobs data out of Australia is robust, the pair may see a short-term break to 0.8800. He expects the pair at 0.8900 by year-end. NZD/JPY is at 84.66, close to the 84.50 level which proved "fairly robust" early in the year. He doesn't expect a sustainable break below 84.00 in the near-term. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 1924 GMT - NZD/USD is benefiting as markets continue to sell USD, says \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2 . Attention is now on the September FOMC minutes later in the global trading day and "we expect the USD to continue to consolidate implying NZD/USD support," it says. It notes the \uc2 HSBC China\uc2  PMI for September will also provide some insight into how the Chinese economy is faring. The pair is at 0.7836 early in New Zealand and \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  tips a short-term range of 0.7800 to 0.7900. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 15:09 EDT - \uc2 New York Fed\uc2  chief \uc2 William Dudley\uc2  says the market's view that the \uc2 Fed\uc2  will hold off on rate hikes until mid-2015 looks reasonable. "It still is premature to begin to raise interest rates--the labor market still has too much slack and the inflation rate is too low," he says, adding that policy moves will depend on how data evolve. While he welcomes the current path the economy is on, he sees little risk of an upside surprise, and adds that inflation will likely remain below the \uc2 Fed\uc2 's 2% target by 2015-end. Dudley's remarks are a reminder that as much as the heat in the \uc2 Fed\uc2  debate centers around raising rate sooner than expected, core officials are still reluctant to end their de facto zero-bound interest-rate stance. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:44 EDT - The bond rally is extending as stocks hit fresh session lows, dragging the 30-year yield down to 3.054%, its lowest of 2014. Slumping stocks and concerns about economies overseas are fueling a flight into safer assets. Bond investors, for now, seem to be setting aside concerns about \uc2 Fed\uc2  tightening, with shorter-dated notes rallying as well. Still, the long bond is leading the way given a lack of global inflation. That's ironing out the Treasury yield curve. The 2- to 30-year yield gap now just 2.55 percentage points, the smallest this year. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 14:22 EDT - Uber-dove Kocherlakota is warning against any push to raise interest rates next year. The \uc2 Minneapolis Fed\uc2  chief doesn't expect inflation, as measured by the PCE price index, to rise to the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 2% goal until 2018. Because of that, "it would be inappropriate for the [Federal Open Market Committee] to raise the target range for the Fed-funds rate at any such meeting" that's scheduled for 2015. Kocherlakota also wants the \uc2 Fed\uc2  to defend its price target just as strongly from the low side as the high side, and he's worried the current arc of \uc2 Fed\uc2  policy won't do that. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:57 EDT - In the latest warning sign about Venezuela's precarious financial position, the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  hoard at \uc2 Venezuela's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shrank by about $1.8B, or 8%, last Friday. Analysts at \uc2 Barclays\uc2  bet the cash left the bank's accounts to pay off the government's $1.5B in sovereign debt maturing on Wednesday. Conventional economic policy says countries shouldn't make a habit of tapping their \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  backstop to pay down debt. Venezuela, facing a dollar shortage and severe structural issues, is in trouble but widely expected to scrape together the cash to pay off the debt maturing this year. That may be harder in 2015. (matt.day@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:40 EDT - As U.S. recovery continues and markets price in greater chance of \uc2 Fed\uc2  rate hikes, Canadian market rates will also likely rise, tightening domestic financial conditions and raising mortgage costs, \uc2 Bank of America Merrill Lynch\uc2  says. This, it says, will cool Canada's housing market, push back the timing of \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  rate hikes, and reduce the need for hawkish guidance. "Thus, the \uc2 BOC\uc2  can sit back, relax and let the \uc2 Fed\uc2  take the punchbowl away." BOC deputy governor \uc2 Tim Lane\uc2  acknowledged recently that "renormalization" of U.S. monetary policy will tighten Canadian monetary and financial conditions, signaling no hurry to hike rates. (nirmala.menon, @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 13:20 EDT - \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's 2.1% year-end forecast for the 10-year yield isn't looking so unreasonable with the rate falling another 6 basis points today to 2.361%. The investment bank has maintained its out-of-consensus call all year, arguing that \uc2 Fed\uc2  policy and economic uncertainties will prevent the yield from rising to the more-widely expected 3% by year-end. "We believe the US economy cannot take higher yields and that the \uc2 Fed\uc2  will adjust policy in response to bond-market moves, as it did with tapering in 2013." And when \uc2 Fed\uc2  tightening does come, \uc2 HSBC\uc2  sees shallower hikes than the market expects, which should support 3- to 5-year Treasurys. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 13:11 EDT - Solid demand at the 3-year auction allows \uc2 Treasury\uc2  to pay a borrowing cost of just 0.994%. There were enough bids to cover the $27B sale 3.42 times over, matching 2014's high for 3s. Indirect bidders, often a reflection of overseas appetite, take 35.5% of the supply--above the group's recent 33.5% average--while direct bidders get an on-par 17.4%. Treasurys continue to fly high after the auction results, with prices holding around session highs. Looks like concerns about global growth is trumping concerns about the \uc2 Fed\uc2  potentially raising rates next year. Ten-year notes up 17/32 to yield 2.364%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 13:02 EDT - The IMF's latest barometer of global home prices in 50 countries shows they're little changed since 2008, but averages in this case are misleading. The IMF divided those countries into two groups. The first group of 33 "recovering" housing markets had a big property boom before the recession and a crash thereafter. Prices in those countries are around 20% below their 2008 level. That group includes the U.S., Spain, Italy, and Japan. The second group of 17 "rebounded" housing markets had less of a downturn and a much stronger recovery from the 2008 crash. Prices in this group, which includes China, Australia, and Canada, are up 25% since 2008. (nick.timiraos@wsj.com; @NickTimiraos)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 October 07, 2014 16:30 ET (20:30 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020141007eaa7003c7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
