\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again to Support Sol -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,348 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 23:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 17:10 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  today sold $60M in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, aiming to support the sol, which closed at PEN2.895 per US dollar, compared with PEN2.888 Friday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday sold $423M, also aiming to ease depreciation pressures on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So far this year, the sol has weakened 3.4% against the dollar. (Robert.kozak@wsj.com @robertkozak2)\par \par \uc2 17:02 EDT - Cocoa butter prices are heating up again, one more factor keeping cocoa-bean futures aloft. In the week ended September 26, cocoa butter, the substance that gives chocolate its creamy texture, was going for $8,542 a ton in the US, according to the Cocoa Merchants Association of America, a trade group. Cocoa-butter prices are up 7.2% over the past two weeks, just as cocoa futures were touching fresh 3 1/2-year highs, fueled by concerns that Ebola could spread into Ivory Coast from Liberia, and hinder the harvest and transport of cocoa from the world's top producer. ICE cocoa ended flat at $3,311/ton. (leslie.josephs@wsj.com; @lesliejosephs)\par \par \uc2 16:44 EDT - The US Consumer Financial Protection Bureau says it has the power to go back several years in scrutinizing whether banks mistreated homeowners during the foreclosure crisis. The consumer-finance regulator today reached a $37.5M settlement with \uc2 Flagstar Bancorp\uc2  (FBC) over alleged mistreatment of homeowners dating back to 2011. CFPB officials say they have the power to pursue financial firms under tough mortgage-servicing standards that went into effect earlier this year. In addition, CFPB officials say the 2010 Dodd-Frank law allows them to pursue similar allegations for conduct that happened before the mortgage rules went into effect. That aggressive posture from the government's consumer watchdog is likely to unnerve many in the banking industry. (alan.zibel@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 16:35 EDT - AUD/NZD is likely to target a move above 1.1310 in the days ahead, which would mark the highest since November 2013, as "the NZD is likely to remain weighed down by the threat of further RBNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Imre Speizer. He adds that the highlight of the upcoming session should be the \uc2 HSBC China\uc2  manufacturing PMI, although NZ has building permits and monthly business confidence and Australia has credit data and weekly consumer confidence data as well. Speizer adds it seems a multi-year reversal in the cross probably began in January at 1.0493. The pair last at 1.1226. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 16:27 EDT - With a pair of \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  Democrats on one end and the \uc2 European Union\uc2  on the other, the Obama administration finds itself in the middle of a tug-of-war over whether exports of oil and natural gas should be included in any trade agreement between the U.S. and the EU. Democratic Sens. \uc2 Barbara Boxer\uc2  of California and Edward Markey of Massachusetts are urging against such a move, while the EU has been asking for just that. It's unlikely any trade agreement is imminent, but this tension will only grow alongside the growing U.S. oil and gas production. (amy.harder@wsj.com; @AmyAHarder)\par \par \uc2 16:26 EDT - NZD/USD has been the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past session after the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction figures show it sold NZ$521M in August, says BNZ strategist Kymberly Martin. "However, after its precipitous fall, we are wary of a bounce...particularly if the USD uptrend runs out of momentum." Pair down 1.25% at 0.7766. Martin says the upcoming session's domestic focus will be the release of the ANZ business confidence survey for September. She now sees a clear band of support at the mid-2013 lows around 0.7690. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 16:23 EDT - \uc2 Moody's\uc2  says the declining student-loan default rate that the Department of Education recently posted is a credit positive for US higher education. Improving figures lesson some risks that accompany high default rates, like reputational damage for schools, loss of access to federal financial aid programs and accreditation sanctions, the ratings firm says. \uc2 Moody's\uc2  expects default rates to decline during the next few years, though "pockets of risk will persist" at community colleges, for-profit institutions and historically black colleges and university. (melissa.korn@wsj.com; @melissakorn)\par \par \uc2 16:13 EDT - Moral victory today for bulls, who were able to recover the lion's share of steep opening losses by the closing bell. The \uc2 Dow\uc2  Industrials also avoided a sixth straight triple-digit daily move after falling almost 180 points shortly after the open. Some of the nervousness in equities markets gets pinned on pro-democracy protests in Hong Kong, though the lurching moves during the past few sessions shows investors can be fickle when eyeing expensive stock markets. Materials, energy and financials were the biggest laggards, while utilities finish higher. DJIA falls 41.93 to 17071.22, and \uc2 Nasdaq\uc2  Comp sheds 6.34 (earlier off about 48) to 4505.85. S&P 500 ends 5.05 lower at 1977.80. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:55 EDT - \uc2 Ford Credit\uc2 , the finance arm of \uc2 Ford Motor\uc2  (F), says today that subprime auto lending has returned to the levels seen in 2007, but loan losses remain consistently healthy. The company says 15% of loans qualify as subprime -- a rate not seen since before the financial crisis. Ford will now begin offering 75-month terms on loans, a new high in terms of length of maturity. (john.stoll@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:51 EDT - Boutique researcher MoffettNathanson's first ratings on network operators Level 3 (LVLT) and Cogent (CCOI) are a buy. The firm reasons that both telcos, despite very different business models, should see free cash flow "surge" in coming years as they skim more profitable business customers off incumbents providers' base. LVLT could also save hundreds of millions by refinancing debt. Still, fears of network connection disputes and LVLT's unwieldy footprint have kept both companies' multiples relatively low. The firm suggests both are bargains as it pins a $43 price target on CCOI and forecasts $57 for LVLT. Bandwidth discounter CCOI gains 1.8% to $34.23, while much larger LVLT jumps 3% to $45.98. (andrew.fitzgerald@wsj.com; @drewfitzgerald)\par \par \uc2 15:47 EDT - So much for the Bill Gross-driven selloff. \uc2 Treasury\uc2  prices up broadly today as haven demand trump the fallout from Gross's abrupt departure from Pimco, announced Friday. "The Treasury bond market is very large...I am confident that the bond market will move beyond this news as it has so many other noteworthy events," says \uc2 James Sarni\uc2 , senior managing partner at \uc2 Payden & Rygel\uc2 , which manages about $85B. The 10-year note is 12/32 higher, yielding 2.490%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 15:39 EDT - The dollar store saga over Family Dollar (FDO) is "far from over," \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  says, as it calls on investors to be patient over the next couple months as the discount chain decides between being sold to \uc2 Dollar Tree\uc2  (DLTR) or \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  (DG). FDO needs more "downside protection" before cozying up to DG because should both deals fall apart, FDO would likely trade around $59, more than 23% off current levels. The firm expects DLTR to up its "last & final" offer in the coming weeks, but still is betting on DG to prevail in this fight. FDO nearly flat at $77.52. (paul.ziobro@wsj.com; @pziobro) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com \par Cynthia Lin, 212 416-4403, Cynthia.lin@wsj.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181, john.shipman@wsj.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326, Patrick.sullivan@wsj.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September 29, 2014 17:10 ET (21:10 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140929ea9t003oc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
