\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  Again to Support Sol -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,365 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 September 2014\par 23:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 17:10 EDT - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  today sold $60M in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, aiming to support the sol, which closed at PEN2.895 per US dollar, compared with PEN2.888 Friday. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Friday sold $423M, also aiming to ease depreciation pressures on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So far this year, the sol has weakened 3.4% against the dollar. (Robert.kozak@wsj.com @robertkozak2)\par \par \uc2 17:02 EDT - Cocoa butter prices are heating up again, one more factor keeping cocoa-bean futures aloft. In the week ended September 26, cocoa butter, the substance that gives chocolate its creamy texture, was going for $8,542 a ton in the US, according to the Cocoa Merchants Association of America, a trade group. Cocoa-butter prices are up 7.2% over the past two weeks, just as cocoa futures were touching fresh 3 1/2-year highs, fueled by concerns that Ebola could spread into Ivory Coast from Liberia, and hinder the harvest and transport of cocoa from the world's top producer. ICE cocoa ended flat at $3,311/ton. (leslie.josephs@wsj.com; @lesliejosephs)\par \par \uc2 2051 GMT - NZD/USD remains under pressure with the USD supported by data that showed personal income growth, spending growth was faster than expected and the \uc2 Dallas Fed\uc2  activity survey was stronger than expected, ANZ says. "Housing remains the Achilles' heel with pending home sales dropping 1% in August." The pair is at 0.7766. ANZ adds that the Chicago PMI should provide positive ISM signals late in the global day "but before that we have ANZ NZ Business Confidence." The pair is expected to trade in a range of 0.7740 to 0.7850. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 16:35 EDT - Baby products maker Kid Brands (KIDBQ) says it needs more time to file a Chapter 11 plan that divvies up the proceeds from the sale of its various business units. The company seeks a 90-day extension of its exclusive right to file a liquidation plan without interference from creditors. Since filing for bankruptcy the company has sold off its Sassy, Kids Line and CoCaLo businesses for about $22M. Kid Brands filed for Chapter 11 in June after Salus Capital declared it in default of its $60M loan. A bankruptcy judge is set to consider the request on Oct. 30. (patrick.fitzgerald@wsj.com; @patfitzgerald23)\par \par \uc2 16:35 EDT - AUD/NZD is likely to target a move above 1.1310 in the days ahead, which would mark the highest since November 2013, as "the NZD is likely to remain weighed down by the threat of further RBNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Imre Speizer. He adds that the highlight of the upcoming session should be the \uc2 HSBC China\uc2  manufacturing PMI, although NZ has building permits and monthly business confidence and Australia has credit data and weekly consumer confidence data as well. Speizer adds it seems a multi-year reversal in the cross probably began in January at 1.0493. The pair last at 1.1226. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 16:26 EDT - NZD/USD has been the worst-performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  over the past session after the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's latest \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction figures show it sold NZ$521M in August, says BNZ strategist Kymberly Martin. "However, after its precipitous fall, we are wary of a bounce...particularly if the USD uptrend runs out of momentum." Pair down 1.25% at 0.7766. Martin says the upcoming session's domestic focus will be the release of the ANZ business confidence survey for September. She now sees a clear band of support at the mid-2013 lows around 0.7690. (lucy.craymer@wsj.com; @lucy_craymer)\par \par \uc2 16:17 EDT - \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  expects \uc2 Bill Gross\uc2  to attract some $50B in additional assets to Janus (JNS) by the end of 2017 reversing the company's organic growth forecast from -4% to plus 5% a year over the next three years. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says BlackRock (BLK) looks best positioned to benefit from Pimco outflows, though it may come up against overlapping customers looking to diversify their holdings. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  maintains its $17.25 price target on the shares, which end down 7.5% at $14.70, trimming some of Friday's 43% rally. (patrick.sullivan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:47 EDT - So much for the Bill Gross-driven selloff. \uc2 Treasury\uc2  prices up broadly today as haven demand trump the fallout from Gross's abrupt departure from Pimco, announced Friday. "The Treasury bond market is very large...I am confident that the bond market will move beyond this news as it has so many other noteworthy events," says \uc2 James Sarni\uc2 , senior managing partner at \uc2 Payden & Rygel\uc2 , which manages about $85B. The 10-year note is 12/32 higher, yielding 2.490%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 15:18 EDT - \uc2 Dallas Fed\uc2  leader \uc2 Richard Fisher\uc2  says in an interview with WSJ today that he'd like to see the \uc2 Fed\uc2  consider raising rates in the springtime, rather than the later summer period favored by officials at the center of the decision-making process. Fisher shrugs off the recent round of weak inflation data and says a lot of the softness comes from supply rather than demand-related factors. "Does it worry me that we are not at our 2% intermediate-term target? It does not" because what's being seen now in the data represents "benign price stability," he says. Fisher is an FOMC voter and a long-time opponent of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's easy money policy stance. (michael.derby@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:49 EDT - The US 10-year \uc2 Treasury\uc2  yield falls to its lowest level in more than two weeks. Stocks in Hong Kong sank by nearly 2% overnight and the selling spread to equity markets in Europe and the US as Hong Kong police clash with protesters. "At this point the heightened tensions is providing just the latest distraction for the market that has been on edge due to other geopolitical concerns," says Adrian Miller at \uc2 GMP Securities\uc2 . "Depending on how the protests advance, the situation in Hong Kong could increasingly become problematic for mainland China and the government's ability to institute reforms while also boosting growth." Ten-year notes up 12/32, yielding 2.490%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 14:32 EDT - A U.S. judge will decide this afternoon whether Argentina should be held in contempt of court, which could open a whole new can of worms in this debt case. The holdout creditors want the judge to impose a fine of $50,000 a day, and this is not unprecedented: U.S. judges have ordered similar contempt sanctions for Russia and the Democratic Republic of Congo. The problem is that court-ordered sanctions would raise questions about a New York judge setting U.S. foreign policy, and no one can really force Argentina to comply with the sanctions. But if the judge doesn't punish Argentina for circumventing his orders, will that make sovereign nations less likely to comply with U.S. rulings in the future? (nicole.hong@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:29 EDT - \uc2 The Fed\uc2  says it will make interest payments on bank reserves a primary part of its strategy for raising rates in the future, but some observers tell the WSJ that could be a political minefield. As the \uc2 Fed\uc2  raises rates, those payments will increase. Since the \uc2 Fed\uc2  remits most of its profits to the \uc2 US Treasury\uc2 , the higher payments will lower the revenue the \uc2 Fed\uc2  sends to taxpayers. As former St. Louis Fed President William Poole tells the Journal, "It's highly likely that some politicians will notice that and given the proclivity of some politicians anyway to demagogue issues, the \uc2 Fed\uc2  is going to have some political explaining to do." WSJ's analysis also shows those payments are increasingly going to foreign banks, which could raise eyebrows on Capitol Hill. (ryan.tracy@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 29, 2014 17:10 ET (21:10 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140929ea9t003q7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
