\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Intervenes \highlight\uc2  to Stem  \highlight22\uc2 Currency \highlight\uc2  Slide -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,484 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2014\par 23:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 17:24 EDT - \uc2 Central Reserve Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the first time it has done so in over a month in order to slow the weakening of the sol \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the US dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $3Mat a rate of PEN2.88 per dollar. The small \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was enough to keep the sol from depreciating further. It closed Wednesday at PEN2.871 per dollar compared to PEN2.876 per dollar in the previous session. So far this year, the sol has weakened 2.54% against the greenback. (ryan.dube@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:05 EDT - Betsey Stevenson, a member of the White House Council of Economic Advisers, pushed back against the notion that declining labor force participation is a long-term trend that cannot be remedied in any way. Some of the recent drop in the workforce participation rate is due to the retirement of baby boomers, Ms. Stevenson acknowledged. But she added: "Not all of the decline in participation we have seen is the result of natural and good developments. In fact, the long-term trend of declining participation in employment rates for many groups, such as prime-age men, and particularly young black men, is troubling, and merits a policy response," she said. (pedro.dacosta@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 17:00 EDT - AUD/USD recovering slightly from an early-week slump, but investors will be watching for comments from RBA Governor Stevens, who is speaking on an economic panel today, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Raiko Shareef. He notes the RBA "came out swinging" against the speculative elements of Australia's housing market in its semi-annual Financial Stability Report, calling the housing market "unbalanced" and flagging it was in discussions with other regulators about further steps to curb pressures. Steven's remarks will be "watched closely for hints as to the types and timing of macro-prudential tools," Shareef says. AUD/USD last at 0.8886, from 0.8925 at the start of the week. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 16:43 EDT - Commercial paper outstanding from nonbank borrowers totaled $307B as of Sept. 17, \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  data show, the highest amount since January 2001. The surge is fueled by low interest rates and rising demand by money market funds that are struggling to find higher-yielding investments, said Chris Conetta, head of global CP trading at \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . Yields for the shortest-term Treasurys fell below zero after the \uc2 Fed\uc2  said it is curbing its overnight-lending program. Companies with the highest ratings were able to borrow from the \uc2 Fed\uc2  at 0.04% for seven days as of Tuesday, down from 0.07% on Sept. 17. Conetta observed, "Money in the shortest-term markets is basically free and it's only getting freer." (vipal.monga@wsj.com; @vipalmonga)\par \par \uc2 16:31 EDT - Forsyth Barr lifts its 12-month target price on the \uc2 Fonterra Shareholders Fund\uc2  (FSF.NZ) by NZ$0.30 to NZ$6.80 on increased optimism about long-term growth opportunities in Asia. FSF closed at NZ$6.29. Wednesday. Firm notes that Fonterra reported mixed fiscal 2014 results, with EBIT coming in at the bottom end of the guidance range, so it retains a neutral rating due to short-term capacity risks. However, "in a year of significant headwinds, the Asia division achieved a sound result, with price increases helping partially offset the higher input costs and disrupted sales volumes." Fonterra is "well-leveraged" to the growing Asian dairy market, says Forsyth Barr. (rebecca.howard@wsj.com; @FarroHoward)\par \par \uc2 16:16 EDT - Today brought a bit of good news and a hint of bad news for \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 ' (PBR) costly Abreu e Lima refinery project. First, the country's oil regulatory body approved the lifting of crude from the refinery, a small but important step to its \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which is set to begin in November. But the country's main auditing body says today that it found irregularities concerning the refinery, without elaborating. The refinery has been a sore spot for \uc2 Petrobras\uc2 , with delays and financing difficulties. The final cost could eventually balloon to as much as $20B, quite a bit higher than the original $4B price tag. (william.connors@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 15:55 EDT - Oil cartel struggles to cope with falling prices--the heart bleeds, surely. Sympathizing aside, \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's fiscal health is a big factor in arresting the slide in oil prices, down 15% in three months. In a report published Wednesday, \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  estimates oil prices at which various major exporters balance their books. These range from $44 a barrel for Kuwait up to $184 for Libya. Some big producers are squarely in deficit territory. Iran's price is $130, for example, while Venezuela's is $161. Brent's average so far this year--the key metric rather than the current price--is $107. The key country to watch is Saudi Arabia, with an estimated breakeven of $89. That provides comfort in Riyadh, but not for bulls. To stop oil's slide, they need Saudi Arabia to curb supply further. In theory, though, oil could average $39 for the rest of 2014 and still come in at $89 for the year as a whole. Riyadh would obviously react well before prices got anywhere close to that level. Still, this suggests there is room for Brent to fall further before \uc2 OPEC\uc2 's kingpin steps in. (liam.denning@wsj.com; @liamdenning)\par \par \uc2 15:26 EDT - Jobless rates in Canada and the US do not fully capture their labor market slack, says Bank of Canada deputy governor \uc2 Tim Lane\uc2 . Like \uc2 Fed\uc2  chief Yellen, Lane notes long-term unemployment and 'involuntary' part-time employment remain elevated and wage gains are moderate relative to historical levels. "Our comprehensive measure of labor market slack has shown less slack than in the US, but the gap has been narrowing." (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 15:17 EDT - Argentina's black-market peso careened to another record low, closing at 15.83 to the US dollar. Despite a government call to stop buying dollars in the black market, there seems to be no stopping the peso's decline. The gap with the official exchange rate of 8.432 now totals around 88%. Economists say that gap feeds inflation and devaluation expectations. That, in turn, gives exporters such as soybean farmers an incentive to hold onto their goods instead of shipping them abroad. Indeed, last month's exports fell 12% on the year, hurting tax revenue. Exports could continue falling in line with the peso's slide, economists say. (taos.turner@wsj.com; @taos)\par \par \uc2 14:57 EDT - Another senior \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  official is signaling that \uc2 BOC\uc2  may lag the \uc2 Fed\uc2  in hiking rates. BOC deputy governor \uc2 Tim Lane\uc2  suggests in a speech that \uc2 BOC\uc2  won't have to move in lockstep with Fed. He notes that \uc2 BOC\uc2 's key rate of 1% is already higher than the \uc2 Fed\uc2 's near zero rate, suggesting \uc2 BOC\uc2  can wait longer before pulling the trigger. USD/CAD earlier today hit a 6-month high of 1.1123, now at 1.1067. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 14:44 EDT - Revisions made in the past week to official statistics on the size of the US corporate bond universe have caused that market to contract--in reporting terms--to $7.7T from close to $10T earlier in September. The culprit: an amendment to the \uc2 Fed\uc2 's flow of funds data. \uc2 The Fed\uc2  changed its methodology for calculating the amount of outstanding non-financial corporate bonds, according to the Securities Industry and Financial Markets Association. SIFMA reflected the changes in its own data this month, causing revisions to the bond data from 1995 to now. The data changes mirror a similar shift in calculations for municipal bonds in 2011, when the muni bond market had to be revised by $1T. (katy.burne@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 14:32 EDT - US ethanol output fell to a six-month low of 889K barrels/day in the week through September 19, from 931K the prior week, according to federal data. It comes amid softer gasoline demand in the US following summer driving season and weaker export demand for the biofuel, analysts say. Ethanol futures are trading near 11-month lows. Another issue: persistent rail-logistics snarls in the Midwest make it difficult for ethanol processors to transport the corn-based additive. Arlan Suderman, analyst at Water Street Solutions, says he's "fairly optimistic" ethanol makers will "see profitable margins as we go into 2015," but a significant concern is whether "we'll have the locomotives to move the ethanol." Ethanol futures for October delivery slip 0.5% to $1.609/gallon. (david.kesmodel@wsj.com; @davidkesmodel)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 24, 2014 17:24 ET (21:24 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140924ea9o003o3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
