\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  May Not Buoy the Sol -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,455 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2014\par 14:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:24 EST - Weaker copper prices and less abundant global liquidity will cause Peru's sol to depreciate more and to close at PEN2.86 per US dollar this year, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. The sol closed slightly weaker at PEN2.825 per dollar yesterday, even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $275M. Jefferies meanwhile notes that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shown little tolerance for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness on concerns that this "may trigger outflows of the weighty foreign holdings of domestic debt." The sol has depreciated 9.6% in the past year. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:20 EST - Downbeat 4Q results from \uc2 Arch Coal\uc2  (ACI) as the company was hit by "a softer pricing environment" and rail disruptions. Cash margin tumbles 40% amid a 10% increase in costs, primarily due to the rail woes. Coal-price softness persisted despite total US output falling below 1B tons for the first time since 1993, which helped power producers' year-end stockpiles be at their lowest since 2006. But ACI is hopeful for what 2014 brings, saying US thermal-coal markets should "tighten further...with favorable weather trends and healthier economic activity driving increased power demand. In addition, elevated natural gas prices compared with prior years should ensure that western coals--as well as most eastern coals--are competitively priced for power generation." ACI is down 2.2% premarket at $3.92 after skidding 5.4% Monday. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:15 EST - "With rising inventory, increasing production capacity, and slowing demand, February is shaping up to be an important indicator of the health of the auto industry," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  writes after January's weaker-than-expected sales. Weather "clearly played a materially negative role," MS says, particularly for the Detroit Three as much of their sales are in the Midwest and Northeast. Firm thinks the industry "stands at a cross-road," where "pricing is going to have to keep melting" if US sales are to get to a 17M SAAR in the next 12-18 months. The best of the auto replacement cycle is over, MS thinks, with "the incremental buyer moving from someone who needs to replace their car to one who wants to...making bank willingness to lend and credit availability more important than ever." (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:11 EST - So \uc2 JC Penney\uc2 's (JCP) F4Q same-store sales rose 2%. Congratulations on the first increase in 2 1/2 years. But remember that they crumbled 32% a year earlier during a disastrous holiday which cost supposed savior Ron Johnson his job. Exceedingly low bars make for easy targets to overcome. Now, as the year-earlier sales declines lessen as 2014 progress, JCP needs to show it can log growth over those 2013 levels for investors to really get a sense that the sales hemorrhage is gone. Shares, which hit a fresh 31-year low Monday while falling another 4%, are up 1.9% premarket at $5.79. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 8:01 EST - Nu Skin's (NUS) 4Q results proved even better than the high hopes the multilevel marketer set in October. The update comes as CEO Truman Hunt says, "Given the current review of our business in China and our desire to provide the most-informed guidance possible," NUS is pushing back the release of its full 4Q report to later this month. The company was set to post Thursday, but since setting that date came word Chinese officials would investigate accusations NUS is operating an illegal pyramid scheme. That caused the highflying stock to lose 1/3 of its value, and the stock has marked time the past few weeks. It's up 0.9% premarket at $83.50. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:49 EST - Sterne Agee launches coverage on \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  (MSFT) with a neutral rating and $40 target. MSFT "is in a significant transition period regarding its management team and products," the firm writes, "accentuated by underlying technology shifts" related to PCs, tablets, mobile and cloud. Sterne Agee notes the "tectonic shift of the technology landscape" has enabled an array of competitors to gain market share against MSFT's customer base and put pressure on its dominant Windows OS business. "The uncertainties of the transition keep us on the sidelines at this point after recent stock outperformance," firm adds. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:47 EST - Stout 4Q results and guidance for this quarter and 2014 from Dice Holdings (DHX) as the professional-focused website operator continues to benefit from recent acquisitions. The company's offerings focus on job finding, and DHX says, "Recruitment activity improved in financial services in Europe and Asia while activity was fairly consistent in technology and energy." The company notes profit this year will be held back a bit by "growth investments." Sounds like more M&A is coming. DHX, inactive premarket, fell 4.4% Monday to hit a 4-year low and it's off 28% the past year. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:36 EST - After dropping 11% this year, Entegris (ENTG) gives investors some comfort with a nice 4Q beat from the chip-services provider. But what's set to steal the show today is the company's planned acquisition of peer ATMI that will cost ENTG $850M. The deal values ATMI shares at a level last seen in late 2007 as the company recently agreed to sell its lifesciences business to focus on semiconductors. The companies "share a long and successful history of solving some of the most-difficult yield challenges facing the industry," says ENTG CEO Bertrand Loy. Both stocks are inactive premarket. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:30 EST - US stock markets look to be bargaining for another oversold bounce after the S&P 500's biggest percentage drop in more than seven months. Stocks in Asia hit hard, Tokyo pounded down into correction territory overnight, but the selling seems to have moderated somewhat in the European session. That helps to lighten the mood a little in the US premarket, but bulls have had a hard time lately maintaining any positive momentum. The early gains signaled by rising futures will no doubt be tested. December factory orders due at 10:00am ET. S&P futures up 6.25; 10-yr note a shade lower, yield at 2.60%. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:28 EST - After in-line 4Q results, \uc2 Boston Scientific\uc2  (BSX) gives better-than-expected profit views for both this quarter and all of 2014 as the medical-device maker logged a 3rd-straight period of improved results last quarter. The improvement has been making BSX a big stock repurchaser--as many companies have been--with it spending $500M last year, 45% of it in 4Q. Shares rise 1.8% premarket to $13.25, partially offsetting Monday's 3.8% drop. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:18 EST - Despite a year-end volume pickup thanks to the FOMC starting the taper and traders taking action in the interest-rate pits, CME's 4Q revenue and profit growth weren't as robust as Wall Street anticipated. Average daily volume remained strong last month, rising 13%, but it would appear the futures-exchange operator isn't mightily benefiting just yet from that increased market action. CME's average rate/contract fell 6% in 4Q from a year earlier. Shares, inactive premarket, are down 6.7% in 2014. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 7:07 EST - Coach's (COH) dour quarterly report 2 weeks ago gave a hint that Michael Kors (KORS) continued to batter the fellow handbag-and-accessories company through the holidays. KORS' F3Q report shows just how much as same-store sales jumped 28% and overall results completely crushed expectations, with quarterly revenue topping $1B for the first time. Top-line gains were led by Europe's 73% SSS surge while North America had 24% growth. That as COH logged a 14% slump in North America last quarter. Unsurprisingly, KORS gives just an in-line 4Q view, which if the past is any indication should be easily surpassed. After falling 4.1% Monday, shares jump 13% premarket to $86.97, which would be a fresh high. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 February 04, 2014 08:24 ET (13:24 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140204ea24001ty\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
