\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  May Not Buoy the Sol -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 2,040 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 Februar 2014\par 14:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 8:24 EST - Weaker copper prices and less abundant global liquidity will cause Peru's sol to depreciate more and to close at PEN2.86 per US dollar this year, even as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to actively \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  says. The sol closed slightly weaker at PEN2.825 per dollar yesterday, even after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $275M. Jefferies meanwhile notes that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has shown little tolerance for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakness on concerns that this "may trigger outflows of the weighty foreign holdings of domestic debt." The sol has depreciated 9.6% in the past year. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1319 GMT [Dow Jones] Short-term technical signals for USD/CAD are mixed but becoming increasingly bearish, says Scotiabank. However, until there's a daily close below the January 22 open level, 1.0967, it's too early to call a temporary top, says the bank. USD/CAD now at 1.1070 from the 4 1/2 year high 1.1224 seen last Friday. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1250 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY's breach of 101.60 support is a signal for further downside toward the 100.00 area, says \uc2 Barclays\uc2 . Below there, the bank would expect to see buyers return near the former rang highs close to 99.00. USD/JPY now at 101.28 within the day's 101.50-100.76 range.gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1236 GMT [Dow Jones] PLN/HUF uptrend extended to \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 's 74.00 target overnight, allowing the bank to lock-in a healthy 6.1% profit. While the fundamental rationale behind its long PLN/HUF recommendation (cyclical and monetary differentiation, Hungary's greater vulnerability to external shocks) still holds, the bank says its happier to move to the sidelines for now in this cross, as it sees the chance of increased near-term PLN volatility given recent EM contagion, which could result in a higher EUR/PLN cross. That said, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  retains a bearish view on HUF and remains long USD/HUF from 217.59, targeting 240.00. USD/HUF now trades at 230.00, PLN/HUF at 73.75. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1130 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/GBP's timely recovery ahead of what is expected to be a dovish ECB rate-setting meet Thursday allowed \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  to enter a short trade from 0.8280, targeting 0.80 with a protective stop up at 0.8415. The bank already had short EUR/GBP as one of its top trades for 2014, with conviction strengthened by the considerable improvement seen in the UK economy against the background of a lackluster growth/inflation mix suffered by the eurozone. EUR/GBP now at 0.8272. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1109 GMT [Dow Jones] The March Bund future is consolidating the push to Monday's contract high at 144.32, although the strong support area between 143.74 and 143.85 should manage to contain the risk. The 144.32 high will remain vulnerable to fresh bull pressure while support at 143.74 holds, putting objectives at 144.40 and 144.68 within reach. The 144.40 objective is a wave equality target projected from the Jan. 2 higher low at 138.68, and 144.68 is extracted from the Equalized Active chart. Monday's low at 143.59 shields single print support at 143.50 on last Friday's Market Profile chart, and only below 143.50 would concern short-term bulls. The March Bund future is at 144.10. Market Profile is an intraday charting technique, seeking to evaluate market value as it develops during the day. The technique displays price on a vertical axis, and time/activity on a horizontal axis, creating bell curves that show normal and variable distributions in financial markets. -By Francis Bray, Dow Jones Chief Technical Analyst For Europe.(francis.bray@dowjones.com) (This article is general financial information, not personalized investment advice, as it does not consider the unique circumstances affecting an individual reader's decision to buy or sell a specific security or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dow Jones does not warrant the accuracy, completeness or timeliness of the information in this article, and any errors will not be made the basis for any claim against Dow Jones. The author does not invest in the instruments or markets cited in this article.)\par \par \uc2 1100 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD has bounced strongly from the lower limit of its multi-year channel line, and, with the help of a hawkish RBA statement overnight, printed a fresh 2-week high of 0.8914 Tuesday. However \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  warns against getting too carried away, noting that the rate is still trading below the downward-sloping trendline resistance, which would suggest the current recovery is corrective in nature, adding that only a decisive daily close above 0.9267 will provide an early bullish signal. AUD/USD now at 0.8875, a 42-month low of 0.8660 was seen on January 24. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1033 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Barclays\uc2  has left a trailing stop on its EUR/USD short at 1.3580 and, while the pair is below here, targets 1.34 and 1.33. Now at 1.3520, the bank says a break below 1.3475 will strengthen its bearish view. gary.stride@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 1025 GMT [Dow Jones] If the market sentiment remains relatively calm next days, KBC Bank expects that there may be good demand for Hungarian bonds at Thursday's auction, helped by increasing yields and a large bond redemption on Feb. 12, which is also the payment date for the upcoming tender. Hungary will offer a total of HUF 50 billion of the April 2018, June 2019 and June 2025-dated bonds. It also looks, KBC says, that the short end of the curve is more sensitive to the forint's weakening. "So, in case EURHUF moves back to around 305 [from 310.87/311.10 currently], the Hungarian [curve] may steepen, in other case the bearish flattening may continue," KBC Bank says. In the latter case, KBC sees room for roughly 50-70 bps narrowing of the 3/10-year spread, while in a positive market this spread may widen by roughly 50 bps. (emese.bartha@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 By Francis Bray, CFTe MSTA\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar has been confined in a long-term downtrend, reflected by a three-and-a-half year low at $0.8658 against the US dollar. But technical analysis suggests the Aussie is due for a bounce, albeit a corrective one, with a little help from the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For some time now, the RBA has been on an easing bias with regard to its base interest rate cycle, and bemoaning the fact that its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is too strong, helping the Aussie lose over 14% in value against USD and over 21% against EUR during 2013.\par \par \uc2 But the shackles were loosened on Tuesday, when the RBA delivered a much more balanced statement.\par \par \uc2 The bank left its policy rate on hold at 2.50%, but refrained from commenting about a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  that "remains uncomfortably high". Also in the statement, RBA Governor \uc2 Glenn Stevens\uc2  stated that, "on present indications, the most prudent course is likely to be a period of stability in interest rates".\par \par \uc2 Prior to this, the bank's indications of further rate cuts had sent the AUD/USD spot to a three-and-a-half year low at $0.8658 in January. Also, fears of slower growth in China, Australia's largest export trading partner, has played a role in keeping the Aussie weak.\par \par \uc2 So will this change of rhetoric from the RBA board prompt a recovery for the ailing Aussie? Technical analysis suggests it can.\par \par \uc2 Already, the response has been positive. The AUD/USD pushed strongly into two-week highs above $0.8834, and a break above $0.8924 is anticipated in the coming sessions. This would lead to further AUD/USD gains to $0.8950 and the $0.90 level.\par \par \uc2 But the weekly chart below shows how the Jan. 13 lower high at $0.9087 is the important level to break, because that would lead to the prospect of a more meaningful recovery.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/6557"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/6557}}}\par \par \uc2 That $0.9087 lower high lies at the junction of the 38.2% Fibonacci retracement level of the three-month decline from $0.9758 to $0.8658.\par \par \uc2 Additional upside risk to the pivotal $0.9250 area would be signalled on a break above $0.9087, close to the 50% retracement level at $0.9210.\par \par \uc2 But the powerful long-term downtrend from the July 2011 peak at $1.1083 will remain a dominant force, and the structure of the decline would only be damaged on a break above $0.9568.\par \par \uc2 The recovery would have to break above the late October lower high at $0.9758 to receive an upgrade.\par \par \uc2 At 1023 GMT, AUD/USD was trading at $0.8890.\par \par \uc2 (This is an opinion column by Francis Bray. He is Dow Jones's chief technical analyst for Europe, and has worked as a technical analyst and trader for 20 years at Pyne Trading and Informa Global Markets in London, Barcelona and Guernsey. He was nominated in the best equity research category at The Technical Analyst Awards 2013. He can be reached at +44 (0)20 7842 9249; or by email: francis.bray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 For more technical analysis see: Dow Jones Newswires, N/DJTA; Bloomberg, NI DJTA; and Reuters key word search "INSI-DJN"\par 