\par\par \b \uc2 Peru  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Sells $275M to Support Sol -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,492 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Februar 2014\par 20:12\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 14:02 EST - \uc2 Peru's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  started the week where it left off Friday, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell U.S. dollars. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $275M, after selling $130M on Friday. From Jan. 22 to Jan. 28 it sold $469M, as turmoil in emerging markets hit \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have helped ease any volatility as the sol has stayed more or less stable, closing Monday at PEN2.825 per U.S. dollar. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has a lot of ammunition, with net international reserves of $65.73B at the end of January. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:15 EST - The IMF suggests, again, that the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2  has room to delay its plan to balance the budget in 2015 if there is no "meaningful increase" in economic growth. But according to IMF mission chief to Canada, Roberto Cardarelli, \uc2 Canada's government\uc2  may not be comfortable doing so, worried that even delaying it by a year could have negative consquences. Cardarelli says its a "legitimate concern" that a delay could affect consumer confidence. For the \uc2 Canadian government\uc2 , it's a "tug of war" between healthier finances and the potential drag that could have on growth, Cardarelli said. Finance Minister Jim Flaherty said recently there would be "no doubt" that the 2014 budget, due Feb. 11, would show the government is on course to return to a surplus in 2015/2016. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com, @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 13:06 EST - The IMF says there's a missing piece in Canada's financial system -- a single body with an explicit mandate to take a comprehensive view of systemic risks, or to undertake crisis preparedness. To be sure, Canada's regulatory system and supervisory framework is strong and complemented by a "credible system of safety nets." But according to Aditya Narain, a senior IMF official, there is no forum that brings federal and provincial agencies "in a manner that allows for a discussion of emerging risks" in all parts of the financial system. "Nobody is therefore in a position to have at any one point in time, a very comprehensive view of risks across the system," Narain said. (nirmala.menon@wsj.com; @NirmalaMenon)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 12:55 EST - Median forecast from WSJ's survey points to 189K net nonfarm jobs growth in January. \uc2 UBS\uc2  economists come in at the higher end of those projections, estimating a 200K gain, partly because weather during the week the survey was conducted (Jan. 12) happened to be a bit better. Firm believes bad weather and statistical one-offs subtracted about 110K from December payrolls, reported at 74K. A full reversal of those anomalies would've put January gains at 240K. "With several weeks of record cold and several large winter storms in January, [a full] reversal might seem unlikely," it says. "However, the unusually cold weeks and the storms bracketed the payroll survey week -- the payroll survey week was neither unusually cold nor unusually stormy." (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 12:36 EST - Why did the US dollar strengthen against emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after weaker-than-expected manufacturing data earlier this morning? Apparently, investors saw in the January ISM manufacturing numbers another sign that economies across the globe aren't expanding. The weak US report, along with some other recent weaker data, joins evidence of a slowdown in China's economy and troubled emerging-market economies to paint an increasingly uncertain picture for investors. And as uncertainty increases, it prompts more volatile reactions in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Clem Miller, an investment strategist at \uc2 Wilmington Trust Investment Advisors\uc2 . (james.ramage@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 12:19 EST - Citi says \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading flows among its clients suggest the trend toward selling the Canadian dollar among hedge funds and deep-pocketed "real money clients" is reversing. It says CAD registered the strongest buying last week among G10 \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while NZD and GBP showed the strongest selling. "CAD buying and GBP selling are both reversals of strong client trends -- flows signal that positions are at risk this week," Citi says. USD/CAD last around 1.1078 from 1.1130 late Friday, according to CQG. CAD has lost about 4.5% of its value to the US dollar since the beginning of the year. (don.curren@wsj.com; @dbcurren)\par \par \uc2 11:56 EST - Some long-term investors decided to stay on the sidelines while many others dialed back risk exposure and piled into Treasury bonds. Richard Sichel, who oversees $2B as chief investment officer at Philadelphia Trust that includes money from pension funds, says he isn't a fan of Treasury bonds, especially longer-dated maturities that have led the broad rally since the start of this year. "I am still bullish on stocks over the longer term," he says. "The bout of gyration at the moment is not enjoyable, but it could translate into a buying opportunity. I think stocks now provide better value than before." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 11:26 EST - Big drop in January ISM manufacturing index -- led by a huge tumble in new orders -- stuns economists, and while bad weather may've played a role, some are wary of leaning too much on the weather excuse. \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  economists say they're "somewhat skeptical that was the only culprit, as ISM and factory activity in general is typically not as weather-sensitive as other sectors, such as construction." Firm says February figures "will be the real test." Hard to believe weather "was the whole or even most of the story, but it's not clear what else triggered the suddenly much slower growth these results point to," \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  writes. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 10:56 EST - US dollar weakens on a sharp drop in ISM's January manufacturing gauge. USD/JPY last at 101.37 from 101.73 before the report. The problem for dollar bears is that the Fed is tapering, and it'll likely take a prolonged series of bad data like this to stop. As the Fed sends fewer dollars into the economy, the greenback should be strengthening. That's likely preventing the dollar from falling more than it would've on such a lousy ISM result. Key driver is this Friday's January jobs report, which is still expected to prove December's weak 74k gain to be a fluke. EUR/USD at 1.3508 from 1.3488 beforehand. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 10:54 EST - The Yellen-led Fed won't change policy makers' tapering path despite today's soft manufacturing ISM and that Friday's jobs report will be the key, says Charles Comiskey at \uc2 Bank of Nova Scotia\uc2 . He predicts strong job growth would calm sentiment, shooting Treasury yields higher, but a weak one would send the 10-year toward 2.5% and hit stocks. That as Comiskey admits it has been difficult trading these days. "Everybody's head is spinning because we are basically trading on every piece of economic data. Everyone needs to readjust their portfolio as the Fed winds down stimulus. This is going to generate lots of volatility this year." (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 10:40 EST - Chile's weakening economic growth may spur a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary policy rate cut. \uc2 UBS\uc2  says the IMACEC GDP proxy will show signs of moderation for December "after weak retail sales and industrial production releases last week." BofA/Merrill notes that the recent release of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  board minutes outlines a view of a more pronounced domestic slowdown. "They clearly indicate more stimulus is needed and we expect a 25bp rate cut as soon as February," Merrill added. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its policy rate to 4.5% in November. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 1532 GMT xx-DJ Polish sovereign bonds recover some ground after last week's losses tied with outflow of cash from emerging markets. "However, there is not much room for a bigger move up," a dealer at Warsaw bank says. The trade is dominated by global sentiment even on a day when Polish privately managed pension funds return bond portfolios worth PLN 153 billion ($48.8 billion) to the state as part of a pension system overhaul. Average yield on 2-year bond is at 3.16% from 3.20% late Friday, 5-year yield is at 4.02% from 4.09% and the 10-year is at 4.66% from 4.69%. (patryk.wasilewski@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 February 03, 2014 14:12 ET (19:12 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140203ea23002rq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
