\par\par \b \uc2 Peru  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Steps Up Dollar Sale -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,390 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Januar 2014\par 19:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:58 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold $440M, the largest \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year, as it aims to stem any depreciation of the sol. In the three previous trading sessions, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a total of $460M. The heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to be working as the sol has remained stable. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed Wednesday at PEN2.823 per US dollar, unchanged from the previous session. Still, the sol has depreciated 0.82% so far this year against the greenback. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:54 EST - Because primary dealers took 53% of today's inaugural $15B floating-rate Treasury sale, it's likely many money-market accounts have yet to get their fill of the new notes. The auction offered a 0.045% spread over 3-month T-bills. OppenheimerFunds portfolio manager Christopher Proctor says that spread could even decrease at the next few sales as money funds continue to pile into the market. Proctor said that while he was "very impressed by the initial demand," he didn't put in an order today, wanting to see a yield spread of at least 0.05%. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 13:53 EST - \uc2 Marathon Petroleum\uc2 's (MPC) huge 4Q EPS beat was in notable part thanks to how the turnaround at the Garyville, La., refinery panned out, says Cowen. The facility was fully shut, instead of just some downstream or crude units. As such, the investment bank "overstated the downtime impact in our unit margin and capture-rate forecast." MPC's gross refining margin was $15.69/barrel, some 50% above Cowen's estimate. The stock, the best-performer in the S&P 500 today, climbs 4.6% to $87, nearly halving the month's decline. (kevin.kingsbury@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:50 EST - Stocks could stay relatively weak for the next couple months, says Janney technician Dan Wantrobski, but he believes the S&P 500 should bottom before it enters official correction territory. His downside target is 1700, 4.4% below current levels and 8% from Jan. 15's record high of 1848.38. The rule of thumb among technicians is pullbacks turn into corrections when they surpass the 10% threshold. Meanwhile, Wantrobski calls the current pullback a temporary "blip" within a new secular "reflationary bull cycle" that should see stocks and Treasury yields rise and commodities underperform over the very-long term. (tomi.kilgore@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 13:29 EST - Consumer staples are the S&P 500's worst-performing sector, as investors get skittish over the exposure many of these multinationals may have to developing markets, particularly ones in the headlines like Turkey and South Africa as their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tumble. A host of food companies and personal products makers -- including Avon (AVP), Kellogg (K), Coke (KO), Mondelez (MDLZ), \uc2 P&G\uc2  (PG) and Colgate (CL) -- are among the sector's hardest hit stocks. In general, a stronger US dollar vs the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in developing markets pinches profits. Consumer staples down 1.6%, while S&P 500 slides 0.7%. DJIA down 138, or 0.9%. (john.shipman@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 13:13 EST - Any extra sliver of yield on top of what T-bills pay can be attractive for funds which can only invest in the safest short-term assets. But for other short-duration strategies not under strict money-market-fund rules, the new Treasury floaters aren't necessarily the answer to better returns--at least based on today's 0.045% spread. "I see it as a placeholder for cash," says Aberdeen's Kam Poon, who focuses on short-duration mandates. "It's nice to have another asset class to invest in, but I think that's all it is. It's a way of parking your cash." Poon prefers short-term debt sold by high-quality companies. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 12:52 EST - Treasury yields just hit fresh 2-month lows as stocks slink to their worst levels of the session as investors seek shelter from the market's gyrations and for now appear to show no fear about further tapering in an hour. "Taper is already in the market and accounts are buying before rates fall too far," says Tom di Galoma at ED&F. "Additionally, you have month-end extensions taking place at the same time." The 10-year note is 11/32 higher, yielding 2.706%. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 12:50 EST - There's no question the Treasury's first-ever floating-rate auction saw strong demand. Question now is who exactly did the bidding. Market participants believe money-market funds largely drove the demand. Outside that conservative group, some say the 0.045% yield spread may not be so appealing. "It would appear fully priced at this time for investors beyond money-market strategies," says Pimco portfolio manager Jerome Schneider, who oversees short-term markets. "As more supply comes to market, the initial demand fever should subside and valuations should adjust." This adjustment to a juicer yield spread will be key because money funds can only buy so much before reaching their weighted-average life cap of 120 days. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 12:39 EST - The sharp drop in emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is bringing in some long-term buyers, especially to countries offering higher yields like Turkey and Brazil. That's why the dollar has weakened to the lira, trading now at TRY2.2431 after hitting TRY2.322 as panicked selling gripped emerging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the day. Investors note Brazil's bonds yield even a bit higher than comparable Nigerian bonds, which are rated lower, offering an attractive opportunity to earn higher yield and look to future appreciation. These investors believe emerging economies will in the long-run continue to grow at a faster pace than the developed world. "The EM growth deceleration is well-documented and priced in already," said Paul DeNoon, portfolio manager at AllianceBernstein. He's leaning toward adding positions in Turkey, Brazil and Indonesia. (prabha.natarajan@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 12:34 EST - Pimco says it's prepared to "buy the dips" in the Canadian dollar this year, amid the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rapid weakening so far in 2014. CAD has depreciated about 10% against the US dollar in the past year, and just shy of 5% so far this month, as investors take into account Fed tapering and an underperforming Canadian economy. Still, Pimco's head of Canadian portfolio management, Ed Devlin, said the firm is "still secularly bullish on the Canadian dollar based on the country's strong economic fundamentals, prudent fiscal situation and long history of the rule of law." (paul.vieira@wsj.com; @paulvieira)\par \par \uc2 12:31 EST - Primary dealer Nomura grades today's floating-note auction an "A" while calling it a "strong success," adding, "the rich valuation did not deter investors." Indirect bidders took 38% while direct bidders bought 9%, leaving primary dealers with more than half of the $15B sale. But that's likely because dealer bidding was aggressive as Treasury FRNs have been a hot-ticket item in the repo market, so dealers needed to load up. Look for similarly strong demand at the next few auctions as liquidity improves. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 12:30 EST - Speculation the U.S. Federal Reserve could modify its approach to rolling back its bond-buying programs in light of disturbances in emerging market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overlooks the fact that central banks and asset markets experience time differently. Markets are immersed in the present, reacting rapidly to events that emerge rapidly and loom large without the chance to put them in a bigger context of cause and effect. That bigger context is a key focus for central banks: near-term events don't impinge on them nearly as much. (don.curren@wsj.com @dbcurren) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par To contact the Market Talk Editors, \par Bradley Davis, 212 416-2654, bradley.davis@dowjones.com \par Kevin Kingsbury, 212 416-2354, kevin.kingsbury@dowjones.com \par John Shipman, 212 416-2181,john.shipman@dowjones.com \par Patrick Sullivan, 212 416-2326,Patrick.sullivan@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 January 29, 2014 13:58 ET (18:58 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140129ea1t0036t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
