\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Peru \highlight\uc2 's  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight \highlight22\uc2 Intervenes \highlight\uc2  to Sell $178M -- Market Talk\b0\par\par\uc2 1,342 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Januar 2014\par 19:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2014, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 13:56 EST - \b \highlight22\uc2 Peru\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the spot market to sell $178 million, aiming to bolster the sol. The sol has come under depreciation pressures, alongside other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The heavily-managed sol has depreciated about 10.5% against the greenback in the past 12-month period. It ended today's trading session at PEN2.823 per greenback. (robert.kozak@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 13:09 EST - \uc2 Barclays\uc2  announces plans to launch a standalone \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  Floating Rate Index to cater to the \uc2 US government\uc2 's new securities, debuting Jan. 29 in a $15B auction. The new securities will not be eligible for \uc2 Barclays\uc2 ' existing US Agg, Global Agg and \uc2 US Treasury\uc2  indexes, which are widely used by bond funds. The creation of an individual index, however, will help track market performance and facilitate bond mangers in incorporating the new notes into their portfolios. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 12:45 EST - By reputation, sharp selloffs in certain emerging markets quickly raise fears about contagion, often fueling a downward spiral for the asset class as a whole. But Capital Economics warns it's no longer fair to bucket, say, Mexico with Ukraine. "The need for investors to discriminate between individual EMs has never been greater," says CapEcon's Neil Shearing. While he acknowledges that serial fiscal mismanagement in places like Argentina and Venezuela and persistent overspending in South Africa and Turkey will catch up with those nations, he also maintains that things are looking up economically in places like South Korea, the Philippines and Mexico. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 Call us at (212) 416-2181 or john.shipman@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 11:36 EST - Yields on T-bills due in late February and March are up, with some hitting their highest levels since October, when investors feared delayed payments amid a government shutdown. Traders say today's selling reflects renewed concerns about the nation's borrowing limit, with the clock scheduled to reset on Feb 7. Adding pressure to T-bills, traders say some foreign central banks are selling US bills and using those proceeds to prop up local \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid the broad emerging-market selloff. Among the hardest hit is the bill due March 6. Its yield rose as high as 0.101% today from near zero. The yield is now at 0.08%, still double the yield on bills due in May. (min.zeng@wsj.com; @minzengwsj)\par \par \uc2 1556 GMT [Dow Jones] If the euro zone debt crisis were to re-escalate there's virtually nowhere in the financial system that would be immune, but, all the same, there are clear degrees of exposure. According to Capital Economics, Hungary, Romania and Bulgaria are the three emerging markets with most to lose. Economist Neil Shearing says these three have the 'legacy problem' that the last decade's credit bubble continues to cloud their outlooks. Strong financial ties to the euro zone also mean their bank sectors remain fragile, especially in Hungary and Romania.(margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:49 EST - When it rains, it pours. Investors aren't just worried about global growth today. There's US finances to worry about as well. The fact that the clock starts ticking on the US debt limit in two weeks is not new news, but looks like nerves are finally getting to T-bill investors. The yield one-month Treasury bills surges to 0.07% today from near zero. This will likely be a replay of October, as fears about delayed repayments push bond investors out of specific T-bill issues. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1540 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  extends its PLN/HUF target to 74.00 after hitting its initial target of 73.00 last week. With Tuesday's rate cut of 15bp to 2.85% in the policy rate, the \uc2 Hungarian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has delivered a mildly dovish surprise, which reinforced \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 's bearish view of the HUF, and helped it reach the PLN/HUF 73.00 target, it says. PLN/HUF is at 72.8. Continues to look for further upside in USD/HUF to a medium-term target of 240, it adds. USD/HUF is at 223.57. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 1528 GMT [Dow Jones] PLN, HUF, CZK start to look attractive for entry, says \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 . Both PLN and HUF have come under pressure against the EUR, with breaking key levels; EUR/PLN has risen above 4.20 for the first time since early December and EUR/HUF has broken above a resistance level at 305. Further weakness in Central and Eastern European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is being driven by external, and local factors alone--which \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  reckons is the case at present--may provide attractive levels long positions, it says. But it "would wait for more orderly price behaviour in external markets before recommending such positions." However, given the rise in EUR/USD, both HUF and PLN are still faring relatively well against other emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  globally, which is justified given the improving macroeconomic and technical backdrop for both, \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  adds. (margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:20 EST -- The Colombian peso is following the selloff in emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , now trading at COP2,002 to the dollar after ending 2013 at COP1,941. Unlike other countries also seeing their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  plunge, however, the peso's decline is a welcomed development for Colombia. For years, the government has been under pressure to push the peso weaker against the US dollar to help Colombian exporters who were struggling because of the peso's strength. Now with USD/COP topping the 2,000 mark, the political pressure has been defused. Moreover, there are few signs the peso's slide might generate any fiscal or economic imbalances for the government. (darcy.crowe@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 10:12 EST - Mexican peso has reason to cheer today from upbeat domestic data, but instead it's down another leg as emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to sell off. \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  says retail sales in November were reported up a solid 3% from the month before, while employment in the sector gained 4% from the year before. Yet, USD/MXD is up 0.8% at 13.513. "For any MXN bull who thinks the EM-\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  panic is overdone, this could be your catalyst to go long if you've been on the sidelines," says Dan Dorrow at Faros Trading. (cynthia.lin@wsj.com; @cynthialin_dj)\par \par \uc2 1507 GMT [Dow Jones] Micex closes down 0.15% at 1494.58 as volatility persists in emerging-market stocks and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , prompting investors to flee riskier assets. The Russian ruble hit a record low against the euro today (47.383), as well as sinking further vs USD (now 34.54, worst since March 2009). Ruble weakening also prompted the USD-denominated RTS drop of 1.27% at 1364.11. \uc2 Sberbank\uc2  down 1.97%, \uc2 Gazprom\uc2  up 0.67%, Lukoil down 1.07%. (olga.razumovskaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1456 GMT [Dow Jones] PLN and HUF crosses have also been swept away by the wave of investor flight from emerging-market \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but such treatment of these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the market is incorrect, says RBS. The Polish zloty has lost 4% and Hungary's forint has shed 3.1% versus the EUR year-to-date, respectively. "When the current bout of irrationality ends, long HUF and PLN versus EUR trades should come back into the spotlight, especially given the attractive entry levels," it adds. EUR/HUF is at 305.8, having peaked at a 10-month record of 306.66 earlier in the day. EUR/PLN is at 4.20, a level last seen in early December.(margit.feher@wsj.com)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 January 24, 2014 13:56 ET (18:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020140124ea1o002nj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
